Only the two within-part disjointness assertions are required; curves from the two different parts may meet. They never coincide. In fact, if $t_s C_p = t_t Sigma_p$, then the fixed degree-two line $omega_C \( - 2 p - s + t \)$ has an effective divisor containing every varying $q in C$. It must have at least two independent sections, contrary to nonhyperellipticity. Hence every $C_i$ and every $t_(- x) Sigma_j$ have zero-dimensional intersection or are disjoint, for every $x in X$.

=== 4.3 The relative Fourier–Mukai kernel and its coherent dual <the-relative-fouriermukai-kernel-and-its-coherent-dual>

Fix a seed supplied by Proposition 17. We now realize the cohomological argument following that proposition: first identify the fixed kernel as a flat family of product ideals, then use its fiber amplitude to construct the coherent dual.

==== 4.3.1 The fixed transform and its kernel <the-fixed-transform-and-its-kernel>

Put $M = X times hat(X)$. A point $\( x \, N \)$ of this product will parameterize a translation and a Picard twist in one fixed kernel on the chosen Jacobian $X$. The flatness proved below concerns this family over $M$; deformation of the sixfold itself is treated later. We use the derived category of bounded coherent complexes and the following fixed equivalence:

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.29)")
 $ Psi \( F \) & = R "pr"_(hat(X) \, *) \( "pr"_X^(*) F ⊗ cal(P)^(- 1) \) \[ 3 \] \,\
mu : X times X & arrow.r X times X \, #h(2em) mu \( x \, y \) = \( x + y \, y \) \,\
Phi & = \( 1 times Psi \) compose mu^(*) \, #h(2em) tilde(Phi) = Phi compose #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( H times.square H \) ⊗ - #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <p3sw:support-fixed-transform>]

Here $1 times Psi$ is the relative transform on the second factor. Mukai’s inversion theorem [29] Theorem 2.2 gives the equivalence for the normalized Poincaré kernel. Pulling that kernel by inversion on one factor and shifting by $\[ 3 \]$ are autoequivalences, so it also gives the stated equivalence for $Psi$. The shear and tensor factors in (4.29) are equivalences as well. Thus (4.29) specifies the transform completely; no postcomposition shear is implicit.

Use the source and its transform

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.30)")
 $ I = I_Sigma times.square I_A \, #h(2em) G = tilde(Phi) \( I \) \[ - 3 \] . $ <p3sw:support-source-object>]

On $X times M$ use coordinates $\( y \, x \, N \)$, and let

$ pi \( y \, x \, N \) = \( x \, N \) \, #h(2em) alpha_X \( y \, x \, N \) = y + x . $

Define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.31)")
 $ cal(I) & = "pr"_y^(*) I_A \, #h(2em) cal(J) = alpha_X^(*) I_Sigma \,\
cal(H) & = "pr"_y^(*) H ⊗ alpha_X^(*) H ⊗ "pr"_(y \, N)^(*) cal(P)^(- 1) \, #h(2em) cal(F) = cal(H) ⊗ \( cal(I) ⊗^L cal(J) \) . $ <p3sw:support-kernel-sheaf>]

At first the last tensor product is derived. Expanding the shear in (4.29) changes $I_Sigma H times.square I_A H$ into $alpha_X^(*) \( I_Sigma H \) ⊗^L "pr"_y^(*) \( I_A H \)$; the second Fourier–Mukai kernel then contributes $cal(P)^(- 1) \[ 3 \]$. The shift in (4.30) cancels this $\[ 3 \]$. Consequently, as globally defined derived objects,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.32)")
 $ G = R pi_(*) cal(F) . $ <p3sw:support-kernel-identity>]

This is the fixed-kernel form of [25] (6.2.3) and (9.2.1).

#thm([Lemma 4.5.])[
  (Local Tor and product ideals).  #emph[Let $V$ be a smooth threefold and let $D_1 \, D_2 subset V$ be smooth curves with finite, possibly empty, intersection. Then 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.33)")
 $ "Tor"_k^(cal(O)_V) \( I_(D_1) \, I_(D_2) \) = 0 quad \( k > 0 \) \, #h(2em) I_(D_1) ⊗ I_(D_2) med arrow.r.hook med cal(O)_V $ <p3sw:support-tor-product>]

 by multiplication, with image $I_(D_1) I_(D_2)$. The quotient from $cal(O)_V \/ I_(D_1) I_(D_2)$ to the structure sheaf of the reduced union has zero-dimensional kernel. The same statements hold when each $D_i$ is a disjoint union of smooth curves.]
] <p3sw:support-local-tor>

#proof[
  At an intersection point choose a regular sequence $f_1 \, f_2$ for $D_1$. Its Koszul resolution restricted to the regular one-dimensional local ring of $D_2$ has injective left arrow: at least one $macron(f)_i$ is nonzero because the intersection is zero-dimensional, and that local ring is a domain. Thus $"Tor"_k \( cal(O)_(D_1) \, cal(O)_(D_2) \) = 0$ for $k gt.eq 2$. The two ideal sequences give
  $ "Tor"_k \( I_(D_1) \, I_(D_2) \) = "Tor"_(k + 2) \( cal(O)_(D_1) \, cal(O)_(D_2) \) = 0 quad \( k > 0 \) $
  and $"Tor"_1 \( cal(O)_(D_1) \, I_(D_2) \) = 0$. Tensoring the first ideal sequence with $I_(D_2)$ proves the injection and product image in (4.33). Away from the intersection the product ideal equals the reduced-union ideal, proving the assertion about its kernel. For disjoint unions, at any point at most one component from each part is present, so the same local calculation applies. This is the local fact behind [25] Appendix 11, pp. 91–93, in the form needed here. ◻
]

#thm([Proposition 4.6.])[
  (The relative kernel and all its base changes).  #emph[For the seed of Proposition 17, the derived tensor in (4.31) is the ordinary coherent sheaf $cal(I) cal(J) subset cal(O)_(X times M)$. Both this ideal and its quotient in $cal(O)_(X times M)$ are flat over $M$. Ideal multiplication and both ideal sequences commute with every base change $T arrow.r M$. In particular, $cal(F)$ is $M$-flat, and at $m = \( x \, N \)$ it has the ordinary fiber 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.34)")
 $ cal(F)_m & = I_(Z_x) ⊗ cal(M)_(x \, N) \,\
I_(Z_x) & = I_A I_(Sigma_x) \, #h(2em) Sigma_x = product.co_j t_(- x) Sigma_j \, #h(2em) cal(M)_(x \, N) = H^(⊗ 2) ⊗ a \( - x \) ⊗ N^(- 1) . $ <p3sw:support-fiber-formula>]

 Moreover $G = R pi_(*) cal(F)$ has a local finite locally free model, and for every $h : T arrow.r M$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.35)")
 $ L h^(*) G tilde.eq R pi_(T \, *) \( cal(F) \|_(X times T) \) . $ <p3sw:support-kernel-base-change>]

]
] <p3sw:relative-kernel>

#proof[
  The subscheme $A times M$ is $M$-flat, and the subscheme defined by $cal(J)$ is isomorphic to $Sigma times M$ under $\( y \, x \, N \) mapsto \( y + x \, x \, N \)$. Their ideal sequences make $cal(I)$ and $cal(J)$ $M$-flat. Locally along $A times M$, its regular codimension-two embedding gives the ideal resolution
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.36)")
   $ 0 arrow.r cal(O) arrow.r cal(O)^(xor 2) arrow.r cal(I) arrow.r 0 \, $ <p3sw:support-ideal-resolution>]
  whose restriction to each fiber is the corresponding curve-ideal resolution. Tensor (4.36) with $cal(J)$. On every fiber the left arrow is injective by Lemma 21.
  We use the following precise local flatness criterion. For a local map $R arrow.r S'$ with $S'$ Noetherian, a finite $S'$-module $Q$, an $R$-flat module $P$, and an $R$-linear map $u : Q arrow.r P$, injectivity modulo the maximal ideal of $R$ implies injectivity of $u$ and $R$-flatness of its cokernel [39] Lemma 10.99.1, Tag 00MD. Its mechanism is useful here: flatness compares the maximal-ideal filtrations and gives injectivity modulo every power; Krull intersection gives injectivity. Repeating modulo every ideal of $R$ and using the Tor criterion gives flatness of the cokernel. Apply it at a total-space stalk to $cal(J) arrow.r cal(J)^(xor 2)$, with $R = cal(O)_(M \, m)$. It proves that the tensor of (4.36) is exact on the left and has $M$-flat cokernel. Where $cal(I) = cal(O)$ the conclusion is immediate. Thus the derived tensor is the ordinary $M$-flat tensor.
  Its multiplication map to $cal(O)_(X times M)$ is injective on every fiber by Lemma 21. The same criterion, now with the $M$-flat target $cal(O)_(X times M)$, gives an injection with $M$-flat quotient. Tensoring either exact sequence with any $cal(O)_T$ stays exact because its quotient is $M$-flat. This proves multiplication and arbitrary base change before pushforward. The fiber formula follows from $t_x^(*) H = H ⊗ a \( - x \)$ and $cal(P) \|_(X times { N }) = N$. Finally $M$ is smooth and regular and $pi$ is projective. Lemma 20, applied to the actual flat $cal(F)$, gives the local finite model and (4.35). ◻
]

The scheme $Z_x$ may have embedded points at intersections. Lemma 21 says that its reduction is $A union Sigma_x$ and that the difference has zero-dimensional support. In particular $Z_x$ always has one-dimensional support.

==== 4.3.2 The shifted dual is an ordinary sheaf <the-shifted-dual-is-an-ordinary-sheaf>

#thm([Proposition 4.7.])[
  (The actual coherent dual sheaf).  #emph[Let $D_M \( - \) = R cal(H) o m_M \( - \, cal(O)_M \)$. Every derived fiber of $G$ has cohomology only in degrees one and two, and its degree-two cohomology vanishes on a nonempty open set. Consequently 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.37)")
 $ E = D_M \( G \) \[ - 1 \] $ <p3sw:support-dual-sheaf>]

