  For the shear,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.54)")
   $ mu^(*) \( a \( u \) times.square a \( v \) \) tilde.eq a \( u \) times.square a \( u + v \) \, #h(2em) mu^(- 1) t_(\( g_1 \, g_2 \)) mu = t_(\( g_1 - g_2 \, g_2 \)) . $ <p3sw:support-shear-conjugation>]
  Thus tensor conjugation and shear give the line $a \( - g_1 \) times.square a \( - g_1 - g_2 \)$ and the translation $\( g_1 - g_2 \, g_2 \)$. Applying (4.52) on the second factor gives (4.46)–(4.47). Commuting the target translation past $cal(P)_(- g_2)$ introduces only a constant one-dimensional biextension fiber; choosing its trivialization changes the graph kernel identification by a scalar.
  All the displayed identities are identities of integral kernels: they are obtained by pullback, projection formula, and the normalized biextension multiplication. That multiplication is associative. Convolution with the fixed kernel of $tilde(Phi)$ and its inverse therefore transports the genuine source-translation compositors and their associativity diagrams. The kernel of each functor in (4.47) is an invertible sheaf on the graph of $t_(b_g)$. The product kernel and the kernel for $g + h$ have the same graph, since $b_(g + h) = b_g + b_h$. A degree-zero derived isomorphism between these line sheaves is an actual line isomorphism. Any two choices differ by a scalar, since $H^0 \( M \, cal(O)_M \)^times = bb(C)^times$. Transporting the compositors through the chosen graph identifications consequently gives (4.48) with its unital associativity, and transports the source linearization to $T_g G tilde.eq G$. This does not infer a scalar composition from simplicity of $G$ or $E$; the scalars come from the line kernels and their compositors.
  Duality commutes with translation by an isomorphism and changes a line factor to its inverse. Dualize the preceding compatible isomorphisms and invert them. With the shift in (4.37) this gives (4.49) and the inherited associative line compositors. Because (4.37) has only its degree-zero cohomology sheaf, these are actual sheaf isomorphisms.
  Finally, $b_g = 0$ implies $g_1 = g_2$ and $2 g_1 = 0$, since $a$ is injective. The intersection $G_1 ∩ G_2 = 0$ therefore makes the kernel trivial. Nonzero translations have no fixed points. The quotient by this finite flat closed subgroup exists [13] Section 2, p. 136; its quotient map is a $macron(G)$-torsor, hence finite étale because that holds after the trivializing base change. The quotient is a proper smooth connected group space. Over $bb(C)$ it is a quasi-projective group scheme [13] Section 1.1(a), p. 131, hence is a projective abelian variety. This proves (4.50). ◻
]

We have constructed the positive-rank coherent perfect sheaf $E$, its coherent line-twisted action (4.49), and the finite étale quotient (4.50). The action is defined on the entire sheaf by the kernel construction; neither simplicity nor reflexivity was needed. In the subsequent obstruction and character calculations we write

$ cal(I) = I \, #h(2em) cal(G) = G \, #h(2em) cal(E) = E . $

The obstruction calculation applies to $cal(G)$, while the character and deformation arguments use its coherent shifted dual $cal(E)$. The flat family constructed here belongs to the fixed seed. Subsequent sections use semiregularity to deform an associated ordinary sheaf locally, and then propagate the resulting algebraic classes through the period domain.

== 5 Hochschild evaluation and the invariant obstruction space <p3:sec:obstruction>

The geometric construction has produced a coherent perfect sheaf with a finite line-twisted action. To use its quotient in a deformation argument, we must control the entire invariant derived obstruction space. We first prove the exact Atiyah trace identity and its transport through the fixed Fourier–Mukai equivalence, and then compute the invariant space for the Abel-orbit seed.

=== 5.1 Hochschild evaluation and the Atiyah trace <p3sw:hh-evaluation>

We will relate the evaluation of a Hochschild class on a perfect complex to contraction against its Chern character, and then compare these two maps under a fixed Fourier–Mukai equivalence. This is the compatibility used in the obstruction argument of [25] Diagram (8.3.4) and Lemma 8.3.10. The proof below calculates the normalized Hochschild–Kostant–Rosenberg map on the formal diagonal of an abelian variety. It then obtains the trace identity from the diagonal adjunction. In particular, the calculation fixes one convention for the bivector sign on both sides.

==== 5.1.1 Grading, contraction, and the first-jet class <grading-contraction-and-the-first-jet-class>

Let $A$ be a complex abelian variety of dimension $d$, let $Delta : A arrow.r.hook A times A$ be the diagonal, and let $Q in "Perf" \( A \)$. Write $cal(O)_Delta = Delta_(*) cal(O)_A$. We use cohomological shifts, so a vector bundle shifted by $\[ 1 \]$ lies in degree $- 1$. Put

$ V = H^0 \( A \, T_A \) \, #h(2em) cal(S)_A = "Sym"_(cal(O)_A) \( Omega_A^1 \[ 1 \] \) = xor.big_(k = 0)^d Omega_A^k \[ k \] . $

The complex on the right has zero differential. Translation gives $T_A = cal(O)_A ⊗_(bb(C)) V$. Our gradings are

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.1)")
 $ H T^r \( A \) & = xor.big_(a + b = r) H^a \( A \, and.big^b T_A \) \,\
H Omega_m \( A \) & = xor.big_(k - h = m) H^h \( A \, Omega_A^k \) \,\
bb(H)^r \( A \, cal(S)_A \) & = H Omega_(- r) \( A \) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:gradings>]

All exterior degrees in these sums are between $0$ and $d$.

We specify contraction including its order. If $iota_xi : and.big^k V^(*) arrow.r and.big^(k - 1) V^(*)$ is ordinary interior multiplication, define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.2)")
 $ iota_(xi_1 and dots.h.c and xi_b)^L = iota_(xi_1) compose dots.h.c compose iota_(xi_b) \, #h(2em) s_b = \( - 1 \)^(b \( b - 1 \) \/ 2) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:left-interior>]

Composition is read right to left. Thus $iota_(u and v)^L = iota_u^L iota_v^L$, and

$ iota_(xi_1 and dots.h.c and xi_b)^L \( theta.alt_1 and dots.h.c and theta.alt_b \) = s_b det #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] theta.alt_j \( xi_i \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $

The factor $s_b$ is the graded dual pairing of $"Sym"^b \( V \[ - 1 \] \)$ with $"Sym"^b \( V^(*) \[ 1 \] \)$: interleaving their odd factors requires $b \( b - 1 \) \/ 2$ crossings. The pairing of one vector and one covector is positive.

Use invariant Dolbeault representatives, with the antiholomorphic cochain before the holomorphic form. For $eta in H^a \( A \, cal(O)_A \)$, $u in and.big^b V$, $omega in H^h \( A \, cal(O)_A \)$, and $theta.alt in and.big^k V^(*)$, define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.3)")
 $ \( eta ⊗ u \) corner.r.b \( omega ⊗ theta.alt \) = \( - 1 \)^(b h) \( eta and omega \) ⊗ iota_u^L theta.alt . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:left-action>]

The products used with this action are

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.4)")
 $ \( eta ⊗ u \) \( eta' ⊗ v \) & = \( - 1 \)^(b a') \( eta and eta' \) ⊗ \( u and v \) \, &  & eta' in H^(a') \( A \, cal(O)_A \) \,\
\( omega ⊗ theta.alt \) \( omega' ⊗ theta.alt' \) & = \( - 1 \)^(k h') \( omega and omega' \) ⊗ \( theta.alt and theta.alt' \) \, &  & omega' in H^(h') \( A \, cal(O)_A \) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:products>]

These are the total-complex Koszul rules; in particular (5.3) is a left action for the first product. They also determine the meaning of powers of a $\( 1 \, 1 \)$-class. For example, if $j \( xi \) = xi corner.r.b theta.alt$ for such a class, then $xi corner.r.b theta.alt^2 = 2 j \( xi \) theta.alt$.

Write $cal(I)_Delta$ for the diagonal ideal. Fix the conormal identification

$ d f = f ⊗ 1 - 1 ⊗ f med \( mod med cal(I)_Delta^2 \) . $

Let $a_Delta$ be the class of the first infinitesimal diagonal:

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.5)")
 $ 0 arrow.r Delta_(*) Omega_A^1 arrow.r cal(O)_(A times A) \/ cal(I)_Delta^2 arrow.r cal(O)_Delta arrow.r 0 . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:jet-extension>]

For a kernel $M$ from $X$ to $Y$, we use $Phi_M \( - \) = R p_(Y *) \( L p_X^(*) \( - \) ⊗^(upright(bold(L))) M \)$. Evaluation of $a_Delta$ by this operation defines the raw first-jet class

$ a_Q : Q arrow.r Q ⊗ Omega_A^1 \[ 1 \] . $

For a bundle it is the class of its first jet sequence. For a perfect complex it is the same derived natural transformation. The power $a_Q^b : Q arrow.r Q ⊗ Omega_A^b \[ b \]$ means successive applications of this class, with each new one-form appended on the right, followed by the exterior projection. This fixes both the Yoneda and exterior orders; $a_Q^0$ is the identity.

For a coefficient complex $C$, the perfect partial trace is

$ "Tr"_(Q \, C) : "Hom" \( Q \, Q ⊗ C \) arrow.r "Hom" \( cal(O)_A \, C \) . $

Precisely, it is the composite

$ cal(O)_A arrow.r^(upright("coev")) Q ⊗ Q^or arrow.r^(upright("braid")) Q^or ⊗ Q arrow.r^(1 ⊗ f) Q^or ⊗ Q ⊗ C arrow.r^(upright("ev") ⊗ 1) C $

for $f : Q arrow.r Q ⊗ C$. These are the duality and symmetry maps of perfect complexes, with their usual Koszul signs. On a bounded locally free representative this is supertrace. The definition applies without change to shifted $C$, and it does not require a cohomology sheaf of $Q$ to be locally free.

==== 5.1.2 The normalized HKR map and the trace identity <the-normalized-hkr-map-and-the-trace-identity>

Set

$ H_A = L Delta^(*) cal(O)_Delta \, #h(2em) H H^r \( A \) = "Ext"_(A times A)^r \( cal(O)_Delta \, cal(O)_Delta \) . $

We use the derived adjunction $L Delta^(*) tack.l Delta_(*)$, with unit

$ eta_Delta : cal(O)_Delta arrow.r Delta_(*) H_A . $

The usual sheafified local bar map has degree-$n$ formula

$ \[ f_1 \| dots.h.c \| f_n \] mapsto frac(d f_1 and dots.h.c and d f_n, n !) \, $

with the two outer coefficients multiplied. This formula specifies the normalized bar morphism $upright(bold(I))_A : H_A arrow.r cal(S)_A$; see [7] Section 4 and Corollary 4.2 for the local bar construction. An element of $H T^r \( A \)$ is a morphism $alpha : cal(S)_A arrow.r cal(O)_A \[ r \]$ by the graded dual pairing in (5.2). Define its adjoint Hochschild class by

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.6)")
 $ "HKR"_A \( alpha \) = Delta_(*) alpha compose Delta_(*) upright(bold(I))_A compose eta_Delta : cal(O)_Delta arrow.r cal(O)_Delta \[ r \] . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:adjoint-hkr>]

Let $chi_Q : H H^r \( A \) arrow.r "Ext"_A^r \( Q \, Q \)$ denote evaluation of a diagonal morphism on $Q$. For $alpha^(a \, b) in H^a \( A \, and.big^b T_A \)$, contraction with an Atiyah power means $alpha^(a \, b) corner.r.b a_Q^b = \( 1_Q ⊗ alpha^(a \, b) \) compose a_Q^b$, using the graded-dual morphism $alpha^(a \, b) : Omega_A^b \[ b \] arrow.r cal(O)_A \[ a + b \]$. Define the raw Hodge Chern components by

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.7)")
 $ "ch"_(H \, j) \( Q \) = "Tr"_(Q \, Omega_A^j \[ j \]) (frac(a_Q^j, j !)) in H^j \( A \, Omega_A^j \) \, #h(2em) "ch"_H \( Q \) = sum_(j = 0)^d "ch"_(H \, j) \( Q \) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:hodge-character>]

#thm([Proposition 5.1.])[
  (Hochschild–Atiyah trace identity).  #emph[Let $A$ be a complex abelian variety of dimension $d$, and let $Q in "Perf" \( A \)$. Use the raw first-jet class $a_Q$ from (5.5), and the left-interior and HKR conventions (5.3) and (5.6). Define 
$ "ob"_Q = chi_Q compose "HKR"_A : H T^2 \( A \) arrow.r "Ext"_A^2 \( Q \, Q \) . $

 For $alpha = sum_(a + b = 2) alpha^(a \, b)$, this evaluation is 
$ "ob"_Q \( alpha \) = sum_(a + b = 2) alpha^(a \, b) corner.r.b frac(a_Q^b, b !) . $

 On the full group $"Ext"_A^2 \( Q \, Q \)$, define 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.8)")
 $ sigma_Q & = \( sigma_(Q \, j) \)_(0 lt.eq j lt.eq d - 2) : "Ext"_A^2 \( Q \, Q \) arrow.r H Omega_(- 2) \( A \) = xor.big_(j = 0)^(d - 2) H^(j + 2) \( A \, Omega_A^j \) \,\
sigma_(Q \, j) \( v \) & = "Tr"_(Q \, Omega_A^j \[ j + 2 \]) ((frac(a_Q^j, j !)) \[ 2 \] compose v) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:sigma>]

 The direct sum is zero if $d < 2$. Set 
$ c_Q \( alpha \) = alpha corner.r.b "ch"_H \( Q \) in H Omega_(- 2) \( A \) . $

 Then 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.9)")
 $ sigma_Q compose "ob"_Q = c_Q . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:triangle>]

 More precisely, with $"ch"_(H \, k) \( Q \) = 0$ for $k in.not \[ 0 \, d \]$, each component is 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.10)")
 $ sigma_(Q \, j) \( "ob"_Q \( alpha \) \) = sum_(a + b = 2) alpha^(a \, b) corner.r.b "ch"_(H \, j + b) \( Q \) quad upright("in ") H^(j + 2) \( A \, Omega_A^j \) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:triangle-components>]

]
] <p3sw:hh-triangle>

The proof uses two identities on the diagonal. We first show that the normalized bar map carries the Hochschild action to the fixed left contraction. We then show that the adjunction unit becomes the exponential of the first-jet class. Naturality of the unit and of perfect trace will give the stated triangle.

==== 5.1.3 The normalized HKR action on the formal diagonal <the-normalized-hkr-action-on-the-formal-diagonal>

We next verify globally the normalization and action of these maps on an abelian variety.

The difference isomorphism

$ A times A arrow.r A times A \, #h(2em) \( x \, y \) mapsto \( y \, x - y \) $

takes $Delta$ to $A times { 0 }$, and hence identifies the formal completion along $Delta$ with $A times hat(A)_0$. In characteristic zero a commutative formal group has logarithm coordinates. In this case they can be obtained by integrating the closed invariant formal one-forms term by term, with constant term zero. The differential of the difference between the logarithm of the formal sum and the sum of the two logarithms is zero, and its constant term is zero; thus the logarithm carries the formal group law to addition. Its linear term is invertible. Choose coordinates $z_1 \, dots.h \, z_d$ whose linear terms are a basis of invariant one-forms. The completed structure sheaf is then

$ cal(R) = cal(O)_A hat(⊗)_(bb(C)) bb(C) \[ \[ z_1 \, dots.h \, z_d \] \] . $

These are formal normal coordinates. No global nonconstant regular or holomorphic functions on $A times A$ cutting out $Delta$ are asserted.

We explain why this completion computes the derived operations that will be used. Noetherian completion is flat. If $R$ is a Noetherian local ring along the diagonal, $J$ is its diagonal ideal, and $F arrow.r R \/ J$ is a finite free resolution, then $hat(R) ⊗_R F$ remains a resolution and

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.11)")
 $ "Hom"_(hat(R)) \( hat(R) ⊗_R F \, R \/ J \) = "Hom"_R \( F \, R \/ J \) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:completion-hom>]

The same equality holds with a locally free $R \/ J$-module as target. Such finite resolutions exist locally because the diagonal of the smooth variety $A$ is a regular immersion. The identifications in (5.11) commute with composition and with tensoring by $R \/ J$. They therefore identify the derived sheaf Hom complexes on the diagonal, their Yoneda pairings, and the action on $L Delta^(*) cal(O)_Delta$. After sheaf cohomology they identify the corresponding global operations. In particular, the diagonal-supported morphisms with locally free diagonal coefficients below can be compared after completion and then descended by these identifications.

Let $e_i$ have cohomological degree $- 1$ and correspond to $d z_i$, and let $xi_i$ be the dual invariant vector. A global finite free resolution on the formal completion is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.12)")
 $ K = (cal(R) ⊗ and.big^bullet V^(*) \, med d_K = sum_i z_i C_i) \, #h(2em) C_i = iota_(xi_i) \, #h(2em) epsilon : K arrow.r cal(O)_A . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:koszul>]

Here $K^(- n) = cal(R) ⊗ and.big^n V^(*)$. The formal variables form a regular sequence, so $K$ resolves the diagonal. Its pullback and derived Hom are represented by

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.13)")
 $ cal(O)_A ⊗_(cal(R)) K & = cal(O)_A ⊗ and.big^bullet V^(*) = cal(S)_A \,\
cal(H) o m_(cal(R)) \( K \, cal(O)_A \) & = cal(O)_A ⊗ and.big^bullet V \, $ <p3sw:hh-eq:finite-diagonal-complexes>]

with zero internal differentials and with the graded dual identification just specified.

It remains to identify this splitting with the normalized bar map, rather than merely with some splitting of cohomology. Complete the sheafified local bar resolution on the small diagonal in $A^(n + 2)$. Taking the last point as base, use the formal logarithm to write $z^(\( 0 \)) \, dots.h \, z^(\( n \))$ for the logarithms of the other points minus the last point. The outer $cal(R)$-action uses $z^(\( 0 \))$. Each completed bar term is a formal power series extension of the Noetherian sheaf $cal(R)$, hence is flat over $cal(R)$. The ordinary bar contracting homotopy after forgetting one outer action inserts a unit. It is continuous for these formal-adic topologies, so its identities extend to the completion. Thus the augmented completed bar complex is a flat resolution of the diagonal.

There is a global map from $K$ to this resolution whose term in degree $- n$ is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.14)")
 $ F_n \( e_(i_1) and dots.h.c and e_(i_n) \) = sum_(tau in S_n) "sgn" \( tau \) z_(i_(tau \( 1 \)))^(\( 1 \)) dots.h.c z_(i_(tau \( n \)))^(\( n \)) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:koszul-bar>]

An interior bar face identifies two successive middle points, so the alternating determinant on the right becomes zero. The last face sets $z^(\( n \)) = 0$, again giving zero. The first face sets $z^(\( 1 \)) = z^(\( 0 \))$. Expansion along that column gives, for $I = \( i_1 \, dots.h \, i_n \)$,

$ b F_n \( e_I \) = sum_(ell = 1)^n \( - 1 \)^(ell - 1) z_(i_ell)^(\( 0 \)) F_(n - 1) \( e_(I \\ i_ell) \) = F_(n - 1) \( d_K e_I \) . $

In degree zero the map is the identity on the augmentation. It is therefore a comparison of resolutions. It is global on the formal neighborhood: the logarithm makes every face substitution just used compatible on that neighborhood.

The normalized local bar formula defining $upright(bold(I))_A$ restricts to this completed resolution. Indeed, it uses only the first jet in each middle variable along the small diagonal, so is continuous for the completion; naturality of differentials identifies the formulas on overlaps. After pullback to the diagonal, each term of (5.14) maps to $d z_(i_1) and dots.h.c and d z_(i_n) \/ n !$: the permutation sign and the exterior sign cancel. The $n !$ terms sum to $d z_I$. Consequently $upright(bold(I))_A$ is the identity $e_I mapsto d z_I$ on the first complex in (5.13). In particular it is a quasi-isomorphism. Because the comparison (5.14) is global, this conclusion has no additional off-diagonal or higher-cohomology component. This is the restriction of the usual sheafified bar HKR morphism, not a newly chosen splitting.

We now compute its cap action. The $C_i$ have degree $1$, satisfy

$ C_i C_j = - C_j C_i \, #h(2em) d_K C_i + C_i d_K = 0 \, $

and, for $u = xi_1 and dots.h.c and xi_b$, give a closed lift $C_u^L = C_(xi_1) dots.h.c C_(xi_b)$. Its augmentation on $K^(- b)$ is $s_b$ times determinant evaluation. More explicitly, the map

$ cal(O)_A ⊗ and.big^bullet V arrow.r cal(E) n d_(cal(R)) \( K \) \, #h(2em) u mapsto C_u^L $

is a morphism of differential graded algebras and is a quasi-isomorphism. To see the last assertion, postcompose with $epsilon$. The resulting map to $cal(H) o m_(cal(R)) \( K \, cal(O)_A \)$ is the basis isomorphism given by the graded dual pairing. The map $cal(E) n d_(cal(R)) \( K \) arrow.r cal(H) o m_(cal(R)) \( K \, cal(O)_A \)$ is itself a quasi-isomorphism, because $K$ is bounded free and $epsilon$ is a quasi-isomorphism. This gives global closed lifts for the sheaf-level Yoneda and cap pairings.

For clarity about global cohomology, first perform the derived Hom and derived pullback with the finite free complex $K$. The two resulting complexes in (5.13) are finite complexes of trivial $cal(O)_A$-bundles. The closed lifts $C_u^L$ compute their sheaf-level pairings. Resolve only these $cal(O)_A$-coefficients by the ordinary Dolbeault resolution $cal(A)_A^(0 \, bullet)$, and totalize the pairings with Dolbeault wedge. The Dolbeault Poincaré lemma computes their sheaf cohomology. Projective GAGA for coherent cohomology and Ext identifies these cohomology groups and pairings with their algebraic counterparts. Neither a Dolbeault resolution of the infinite formal sheaf nor GAGA for such a sheaf is used.

Every class in $H^a \( A \, cal(O)_A \)$ has an invariant Dolbeault representative; these representatives form $and.big^a H^1 \( A \, cal(O)_A \)$. This standard computation for a complex torus, together with the triviality of $T_A$, shows that decomposable classes $eta ⊗ u$ span $H T^r \( A \)$. For such a class the totalized operator on a normal Koszul term $k$ and a Dolbeault cochain $omega$ is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.15)")
 $ L_(eta \, u) \( omega ⊗ k \) = \( - 1 \)^(b \| omega \|) \( eta and omega \) ⊗ C_u^L \( k \) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:totalized-lift>]

This formula denotes the induced pairing on the finite diagonal complexes above. The normal lift is closed by its anticommutation with $d_K$; totalization is closed because $eta$ is closed. Its augmentation is the cochain for the graded dual pairing. The sign moves the degree-$b$ normal operator past the Dolbeault cochain.

Take the Yoneda convention $beta smile gamma = beta \[ \| gamma \| \] compose gamma$. Composing (5.15) gives the first product in (5.4), because $C_u^L C_v^L = C_(u and v)^L$. Tensoring the same lift with the diagonal gives precisely (5.3). The roof for $eta_Delta$ on $K$ has denominator $epsilon : K arrow.r cal(O)_A$ and numerator the projection $K arrow.r cal(O)_A ⊗_(cal(R)) K$. The augmentation just calculated is therefore the adjoint class (5.6). Derived adjunction and the quasi-isomorphism $upright(bold(I))_A$ show that $"HKR"_A : H T^r \( A \) arrow.r H H^r \( A \)$ is an isomorphism. The calculation proves, in particular,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.16)")
 $ upright(bold(I))_A #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( L Delta^(*) "HKR"_A \( alpha \) \) h #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = alpha corner.r.b upright(bold(I))_A \( h \) \, quad alpha in H T^2 \( A \) \, quad h in bb(H)^0 \( A \, H_A \) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:cap-hkr>]

Here a derived endomorphism acts on hypercohomology by composition. The same calculation of the pairings accounts for every summand of the target $H Omega_(- 2) \( A \)$, since invariant representatives span the relevant groups.

The cochain normalization can also be read directly from (5.14). In unshifted differential-operator notation it is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.17)")
 $ "HKR"_A \( xi_1 and dots.h.c and xi_b \) = frac(s_b, b !) sum_(tau in S_b) "sgn" \( tau \) xi_(tau \( 1 \)) ⊗ dots.h.c ⊗ xi_(tau \( b \)) quad upright("in ") H H^b \( A \) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:cochain-sign>]

Indeed, evaluation on $F_b$ gives $s_b$ times determinant evaluation, the augmentation of $C_u^L$. This is the cochain convention in [6] Equation (3.20). In particular $s_2 = - 1$; replacing left interior multiplication by determinant-order contraction would precompose both evaluation and contraction by $"diag" \( 1 \, 1 \, - 1 \)$ on $H^2 \( cal(O)_A \) xor H^1 \( T_A \) xor H^0 \( and.big^2 T_A \)$. We make no such replacement.

The formal coordinates, their comparison, and (5.15) are fixed by diagonal translation. Translations also fix invariant Dolbeault representatives. Thus translations act trivially on $H T^r \( A \)$, and $"HKR"_A$ identifies this with their action on $H H^r \( A \)$. Finally, $T_A$ is holomorphically trivial, so its positive Chern classes and its Atiyah class vanish and $"td" \( A \) = sqrt("td" \( A \)) = 1$. The explicit identity (5.16) is the abelian specialization of the compatibility in [6] Theorem 1.4; it was proved here without using the general formality theorem or a separate convention for dual chains.

==== 5.1.4 The exponential first-jet class and evaluation <the-exponential-first-jet-class-and-evaluation>

The preceding comparison fixes the homological map. We next compute its adjoint on a perfect complex; this supplies the factorials in the obstruction and trace maps.

On $K^(- 1)$, the extension (5.5) is represented by $e_i mapsto d z_i$. The sign can be checked by mapping $K^0 = cal(R)$ to $cal(R) \/ \( z \)^2$ and $K^(- 1)$ to $\( z \) \/ \( z \)^2$ by $e_i mapsto z_i$. The square with the Koszul differential commutes, and the conormal identification is exactly the one in (5.5). A closed lift is

$ cal(A) \( k \) = sum_i C_i \( k \) ⊗ d z_i : K arrow.r \( K ⊗ V^(*) \) \[ 1 \] . $

Successive application, with the forms appended in the order fixed above, followed by exterior projection, is the lift

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.18)")
 $ cal(A)^(\( b \)) \( k \) = sum_(i_1 \, dots.h \, i_b) C_(i_b) dots.h.c C_(i_1) \( k \) ⊗ d z_(i_1) and dots.h.c and d z_(i_b) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:jet-power-lift>]

It represents the spliced $b$-fold first-jet class: composing the next closed lift splices the next extension, while the displayed form order is the appended order. On $e_(j_1) and dots.h.c and e_(j_b)$, its nonzero augmented terms are indexed by permutations. The contraction and exterior factors have the same permutation sign, which cancels. The result is $b ! thin d z_(j_1) and dots.h.c and d z_(j_b)$. The augmentation is zero on other Koszul degrees.

On the other hand, the degree-$b$ component of $Delta_(*) upright(bold(I))_A compose eta_Delta$ is the projection from $K^(- b)$ followed by $e_I mapsto d z_I$. The preceding calculation and (5.11) therefore prove, on the original diagonal, the identity of morphisms

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.19)")
 $ Delta_(*) upright(bold(I))_A compose eta_Delta = sum_(b = 0)^d frac(a_Delta^b, b !) : cal(O)_Delta arrow.r xor.big_(b = 0)^d Delta_(*) Omega_A^b \[ b \] . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:universal-exponential>]

This is a direct abelian proof of the universal exponential identity of [7] Proposition 4.4, with its sign and divided powers specified by the same Koszul comparison.

Let

$ A H_Q = Phi_(eta_Delta) \( Q \) : Q arrow.r Q ⊗ H_A . $

The projection formula for a diagonal kernel identifies its target as written. Applying the derived integral-kernel operation to (5.19) gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.20)")
 $ \( 1_Q ⊗ upright(bold(I))_A \) compose A H_Q = sum_(b = 0)^d frac(a_Q^b, b !) : Q arrow.r Q ⊗ cal(S)_A . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:perfect-exponential>]

For a vector bundle, the first infinitesimal diagonal is finite over the second factor, so its evaluation is the ordinary first jet sequence. For a perfect complex the same equality is the derived kernel operation, computed locally on a bounded complex of vector bundles. It commutes with splicing and exterior projection. Consequently the powers in (5.20) are the raw powers $a_Q^b$ already defined, also when $Q$ is shifted.

Equations (5.6) and (5.20) give

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.21)")
 $ chi_Q \( "HKR"_A \( alpha \) \) = \( 1_Q ⊗ alpha \) compose sum_(b = 0)^d frac(a_Q^b, b !) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:evaluation>]

If $alpha^(a \, b) in H^a \( A \, and.big^b T_A \)$, its morphism from $cal(S)_A$ is zero on all summands except $Omega_A^b \[ b \]$. Its value in (5.21) is exactly $alpha^(a \, b) corner.r.b \( a_Q^b \/ b ! \)$, of Ext degree $a + b$, with the graded dual and total-complex signs fixed above. This proves the evaluation identity in the present perfect abelian setting; compare [21] Theorem A.

The components in (5.7) are the unscaled $d log$ Hodge components. To identify them with the ordinary character, first consider a line bundle. Its first jet extension is the $d log$ transition cocycle; thus (5.7) is the exponential of that first Hodge Chern class. Perfect trace is additive for a bounded locally free exact triangle, as is seen from supertrace on its cone. Pullback naturality of the first-jet class and the projective bundle formula allow a vector bundle to be pulled to its flag bundle, where its successive line quotients give the line-bundle calculation. Pullback on the Hodge cohomology in question is injective by that formula. Finally, the resolution property of a smooth projective variety represents every perfect class by an alternating sum of vector bundles; additivity treats $Q$. This gives the character identification of [7] Theorem 4.5 in the present convention.

Let $"ch"_(B \, j) \( Q \) in H^(2 j) \( A \, bb(Q) \)$ denote the rational Betti Chern component. Under the Dolbeault–de Rham identification $H^j \( A \, Omega_A^j \) = H^(j \, j) \( A \) subset H^(2 j) \( A \, bb(C) \)$, the raw comparison is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.22)")
 $ "ch"_(H \, j) \( Q \) mapsto \( 2 pi i \)^j "ch"_(B \, j) \( Q \) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:tate-normalization>]

Indeed, the exponential sequence with kernel $2 pi i bb(Z)$ sends the $d log$ cocycle to $2 pi i thin c_1^B$, and the line splitting and additivity just used give the formula in every weight. Dividing by $\( 2 pi i \)^j$ is the Tate-normalized comparison. The trace identity (5.9) uses $"ch"_H$ throughout.

==== 5.1.5 Proof of the trace identity <proof-of-the-trace-identity>

#proof[
  For $beta : cal(O)_Delta arrow.r cal(O)_Delta \[ 2 \]$, write $a_A \( beta \) = L Delta^(*) beta : H_A arrow.r H_A \[ 2 \]$. Naturality of the adjunction unit, with its canonical shift isomorphisms, is the equality
  $ Delta_(*) a_A \( beta \) compose eta_Delta = eta_Delta \[ 2 \] compose beta . $
  Evaluate this equality of diagonal morphisms on $Q$. The projection formula gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.23)")
   $ \( 1_Q ⊗ a_A \( beta \) \) compose A H_Q = A H_Q \[ 2 \] compose chi_Q \( beta \) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:unit-module>]
  This is an identity of derived morphisms before any trace is taken.
  Define
  $ h_Q = "Tr"_(Q \, H_A) \( A H_Q \) in bb(H)^0 \( A \, H_A \) \, #h(2em) sigma_Q^H \( v \) = "Tr"_(Q \, H_A \[ 2 \]) \( A H_Q \[ 2 \] compose v \) in bb(H)^2 \( A \, H_A \) . $
  The perfect partial trace commutes with a morphism of coefficients: for $f : C arrow.r C'$ and $w : Q arrow.r Q ⊗ C$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.24)")
   $ "Tr"_(Q \, C') \( \( 1_Q ⊗ f \) compose w \) = f compose "Tr"_(Q \, C) \( w \) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:partial-trace>]
  This follows directly from its coevaluation–braiding–evaluation definition: $f$ can be placed after evaluation since it acts only on the coefficient factor. Apply it to (5.23). We obtain
  $ sigma_Q^H \( chi_Q \( beta \) \) = a_A \( beta \) compose h_Q . $
  There is no odd exchange sign, since the degree here is two.
  By (5.20) and (5.24),
  $ upright(bold(I))_A \( h_Q \) = "ch"_H \( Q \) \, #h(2em) upright(bold(I))_A \( sigma_Q^H \( v \) \) = "Tr"_Q ((sum_(j = 0)^d frac(a_Q^j, j !)) \[ 2 \] compose v) . $
  In the second equality the component with coefficient $Omega_A^j \[ j + 2 \]$ is $sigma_(Q \, j)$; those with $j > d - 2$ vanish because their sheaf cohomology degree exceeds $d$. Take $beta = "HKR"_A \( alpha \)$ and apply (5.16). This proves (5.9). For the component statement, contraction of $"ch"_(H \, j + b) \( Q \) in H^(j + b) \( A \, Omega_A^(j + b) \)$ removes $b$ holomorphic forms and adds cochain degree $a$. Its target is $H^(j + b + a) \( A \, Omega_A^j \) = H^(j + 2) \( A \, Omega_A^j \)$. This gives (5.10). In particular the factors $1 \/ b !$ in evaluation, $1 \/ j !$ in $sigma_(Q \, j)$, and $1 \/ \( j + b \) !$ in the Chern component have already been accounted for; there is no further scalar. ◻
]

The factorization just proved is the perfect-complex specialization of the character and module identities in [5] Proposition 6.2.1, Corollary 6.3.2, and Proposition 6.4.4. Their differential-operator convention has $a_Q = - "At"_Q^(B F)$. Thus their terms $\( - 1 \)^j \( "At"_Q^(B F) \)^j \/ j !$ are exactly $a_Q^j \/ j !$. There is no residual sign in (5.10). The proof above establishes the identity from the diagonal unit and the fixed bar comparison, rather than importing a compatibility between two unspecified HKR normalizations.

=== 5.2 Fourier–Mukai transport of evaluation and contraction <p3sw:hh-fourier-mukai>

We now show that a specified Fourier–Mukai equivalence preserves the equality $ker "ob"_Q = ker c_Q$. Evaluation is transported by convolution of kernels. For character contraction we construct a homological map using perfect Serre pairings; this also records the sign of the shift in the fixed transform.

==== 5.2.1 Enhanced convolution and evaluation <enhanced-convolution-and-evaluation>

Let $A$ and $B$ be smooth projective complex abelian varieties of dimensions $d_A \, d_B$. Let $Phi = Phi_K : "Perf" \( A \) arrow.r "Perf" \( B \)$ be a Fourier–Mukai equivalence with perfect kernel $K$, equipped with a fixed enhanced inverse kernel $L$, inverse adjunction units and counits, and their triangle identities. Here #emph[enhanced] means that convolution, morphisms, and the isomorphisms commuting with cohomological shifts are those of the derived enhancement, not just chosen identifications of objects in the triangulated category.

For kernels $P$ from $X$ to $Y$ and $R$ from $Y$ to $Z$, write

$ R star.op P = R p_(X Z *) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] L p_(X Y)^(*) P ⊗^(upright(bold(L))) L p_(Y Z)^(*) R #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $

Thus $Phi_(R star.op P) = Phi_R compose Phi_P$. We use the standard enhanced associativity and projection formula for this convolution. The products are smooth proper and the projections are proper perfect morphisms, so these operations send perfect kernels to perfect kernels. The fixed adjunctions give

$ K star.op L tilde.eq cal(O)_(Delta_B) \, #h(2em) L star.op K tilde.eq cal(O)_(Delta_A) . $

Conjugation

$ cal(C)_Phi \( M \) = K star.op M star.op L : "Perf" \( A times A \) arrow.r "Perf" \( B times B \) $

is an exact enhanced equivalence, with the opposite conjugation as inverse. Let $t : cal(C)_Phi \( cal(O)_(Delta_A) \) arrow.r^tilde.op cal(O)_(Delta_B)$ be its counit identification. For $beta in H H^r \( A \)$, define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.25)")
 $ Phi^(H H) \( beta \) = t \[ r \] compose cal(C)_Phi \( beta \) compose t^(- 1) in H H^r \( B \) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:hh-conjugation>]

It is a graded isomorphism and preserves our Yoneda convention, because every displayed shifted morphism uses the enhanced shift isomorphism.

For $Q in "Perf" \( A \)$, let $Phi_(upright("Ext")) : "Ext"_A^2 \( Q \, Q \) arrow.r^tilde.op "Ext"_B^2 \( Phi Q \, Phi Q \)$ be the induced map with the same shift structure. Associativity of convolution, evaluated on $Q$, gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.26)")
 $ chi_(Phi Q) \( Phi^(H H) beta \) = Phi_(upright("Ext")) \( chi_Q \( beta \) \) \, #h(2em) beta in H H^2 \( A \) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:evaluation-square>]

Indeed, evaluation on $Phi Q$ is the composite $K star.op beta star.op L star.op K$ evaluated on $Q$. The inverse unit and counit cancel the adjacent $L star.op K$ by the adjunction triangle identities. The outer $t$ then gives exactly the morphism obtained by applying $Phi$ to the evaluation on $Q$. This proves the equality on morphisms, including their shifts, rather than only identifying the dimensions of Ext groups.

Use the fixed HKR maps of Proposition 25 to put

$ Phi^(H T) = \( "HKR"_B \)^(- 1) Phi^(H H) "HKR"_A : H T^2 \( A \) arrow.r^tilde.op H T^2 \( B \) . $

The evaluation square is already established. The contraction map has the corresponding compatibility, with the homological comparison map constructed below.

#thm([Proposition 5.2.])[
  (Transport of evaluation and contraction).  #emph[For the fixed enhanced Fourier–Mukai equivalence $Phi : "Perf" \( A \) arrow.r "Perf" \( B \)$, there exists an isomorphism 
$ Phi_(H H)^H : H Omega_(- 2) \( A \) arrow.r^tilde.op H Omega_(- 2) \( B \) $

 depending only on that enhanced equivalence, not on a choice of perfect complex, such that for every $Q in "Perf" \( A \)$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.27)")
 $ Phi_(upright("Ext")) compose "ob"_Q & = "ob"_(Phi Q) compose Phi^(H T) \,\
Phi_(H H)^H compose c_Q & = c_(Phi Q) compose Phi^(H T) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:transport-squares>]

 Here $"ob"$ and $c$ have the conventions of Proposition 25, and $Phi_(upright("Ext"))$ is the induced enhanced map on degree-two Ext. Consequently 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.28)")
 $ Phi^(H T) \( ker "ob"_Q \) & = ker "ob"_(Phi Q) \,\
Phi^(H T) \( ker c_Q \) & = ker c_(Phi Q) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:kernel-transport>]

 In particular $ker "ob"_Q = ker c_Q$ implies $ker "ob"_(Phi Q) = ker c_(Phi Q)$.]
] <p3sw:hh-transport>

We construct $Phi_(H H)^H$ using perfect Serre pairings and then prove the two squares. The construction will also retain the Chern-character sign of a shift in the equivalence.

==== 5.2.2 Serre pairings and the homological map <serre-pairings-and-the-homological-map>

We state explicitly the duality used to define homological transport. On a smooth proper variety $X$, Serre duality is the perfect natural pairing

$ "Hom" \( P \, R \)^(*) tilde.eq "Hom" \( R \, sans(S)_X P \) \, #h(2em) sans(S)_X P = P ⊗ omega_X \[ dim X \] \, $

for perfect $P \, R$; write $"Tr"_(X \, P) : "Hom" \( P \, sans(S)_X P \) arrow.r bb(C)$ for its trace. We use its standard expression as the Serre trace on $cal(O)_X$ after the perfect partial trace. In particular it is supertrace on a shifted complex.

Put

$ Sigma_A = Delta_(*) \( omega_A \[ d_A \] \) \, #h(2em) Sigma_A^(- 1) = Delta_(!) cal(O)_A = Delta_(*) \( omega_A^(- 1) \[ - d_A \] \) . $

Here $Delta_(!) = sans(S)_(A times A)^(- 1) Delta_(*) sans(S)_A$ is left adjoint to $L Delta^(*)$. This is the adjunction obtained by applying Serre duality to $L Delta^(*) tack.l Delta_(*)$. The displayed formula for $Delta_(!) cal(O)_A$ follows from $Delta^(*) omega_(A times A) = omega_A^(⊗ 2)$. Define Hochschild homology by

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.29)")
 $ H H_m \( A \) = "Hom"_(A times A) \( Sigma_A^(- 1) \[ m \] \, cal(O)_(Delta_A) \) tilde.eq bb(H)^(- m) \( A \, H_A \) \, $ <p3sw:hh-eq:hh-homology>]

where the isomorphism is this adjunction. For $beta in H H^2 \( A \)$ and $h : Sigma_A^(- 1) \[ m \] arrow.r cal(O)_(Delta_A)$, set

$ beta ∩ h = \( beta compose h \) \[ - 2 \] in H H_(m - 2) \( A \) . $

Naturality of the adjunction in (5.29) identifies this with the action of $L Delta^(*) beta$ used in (5.16).

For $m = 0 \, - 2$, let

$ cal(T)^m \( A \) = "Hom"_(A times A) \( cal(O)_(Delta_A) \, Sigma_A \[ m \] \) . $

The Serre functor of $A times A$ takes $Sigma_A^(- 1) \[ m \]$ to $Sigma_A \[ m \]$. Therefore Serre duality gives the perfect pairing

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.30)")
 $ chevron.l h \, delta chevron.r_(A \, m) = "Tr"_(A times A \, thin Sigma_A^(- 1) \[ m \]) \( delta compose h \) : H H_m \( A \) ⊗ cal(T)^m \( A \) arrow.r bb(C) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:serre-pairing>]

This kernel description and its categorical character are the ones studied in [8] Theorems 6 and 10, Section 6.2. We give the required transport and its cap verification in these pairings.

The Grothendieck-duality adjoint formulas for a perfect kernel on smooth proper varieties are

$ K^R = K^or ⊗ p_A^(*) omega_A \[ d_A \] \, #h(2em) K^L = K^or ⊗ p_B^(*) omega_B \[ d_B \] \, $

viewed after exchange of the two factors as kernels from $B$ to $A$. We use these formulas with their adjunction units and counits. They give

$ K^R tilde.eq Sigma_A star.op K^L star.op Sigma_B^(- 1) . $

For an equivalence the fixed inverse $L$ carries both inverse adjunctions. Taking the mate of the last isomorphism with these adjunctions gives the kernel Serre isomorphism

$ theta : K star.op Sigma_A arrow.r^tilde.op Sigma_B star.op K . $

Its evaluation is the trace-preserving Serre isomorphism: for $f : P arrow.r sans(S)_A P$,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.31)")
 $ "Tr"_(B \, Phi P) \( theta_P compose Phi f \) = "Tr"_(A \, P) \( f \) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:serre-coherence>]

To see why the coherence matters, transport the natural Serre duality pairing through the equivalence. Its uniqueness determines the isomorphism $Phi sans(S)_A arrow.r sans(S)_B Phi$ making those pairings commute; evaluation at the identity gives (5.31). The adjoint formulas with the same units and counits realize this isomorphism by $theta$. An isomorphism between $K^R$ and $K^L$ as unstructured objects would not specify it. Smoothness, properness, and perfection are exactly the hypotheses for these duality and adjunction statements.

Let $u : cal(C)_Phi \( Sigma_A \) arrow.r^tilde.op Sigma_B$ be $theta star.op L$ followed by $Sigma_B star.op t$, and set

$ Psi^m \( delta \) = u \[ m \] compose cal(C)_Phi \( delta \) compose t^(- 1) : cal(T)^m \( A \) arrow.r^tilde.op cal(T)^m \( B \) quad \( m = 0 \, - 2 \) . $

For $delta in cal(T)^(- 2) \( A \)$ and $beta in H H^2 \( A \)$, associativity and the middle cancellation $t^(- 1) t$ give

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.32)")
 $ Psi^0 \( delta \[ 2 \] compose beta \) = \( Psi^(- 2) delta \) \[ 2 \] compose Phi^(H H) \( beta \) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:test-composition>]

For $delta in cal(T)^0 \( A \)$, the same associativity and the inverse adjunction triangle identities give the evaluation identity

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.33)")
 $ \( Psi^0 delta \)_(Phi Q) = theta_Q compose Phi \( delta_Q \) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:serre-evaluation>]

Here $delta_Q : Q arrow.r sans(S)_A Q$ is evaluation of the Serre-valued diagonal morphism. In this second equality the triangles cancel the adjacent $L star.op K$ in the evaluation on $Q$. Thus both identities use the same $t$, $u$, and inverse adjunctions.

Define the homological isomorphisms for $m = 0 \, - 2$ by the perfect pairings:

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.34)")
 $ chevron.l Phi_(*)^(H H) h \, Psi^m delta #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[chevron.r]_(B \, m) = chevron.l h \, delta chevron.r_(A \, m) quad \( h in H H_m \( A \) \, med delta in cal(T)^m \( A \) \) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:homology-transport>]

Existence, uniqueness, and invertibility follow from the nondegeneracy of (5.30) and the invertibility of $Psi^m$.

The homological comparison map asserted in Proposition 26 is now defined by

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.35)")
 $ Phi_(H H)^H = upright(bold(I))_B thin Phi_(*)^(H H) thin upright(bold(I))_A^(- 1) : H Omega_(- 2) \( A \) arrow.r^tilde.op H Omega_(- 2) \( B \) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:hodge-transport>]

The adjunctions (5.29) are understood. This map depends only on the fixed enhanced equivalence and may mix the individual Hodge bidegrees within the displayed spaces.

#proof[
  The first equality in (5.27) follows from (5.26), (5.21), and the definition of $Phi^(H T)$.
  For the second square, we verify compatibility with the cap action and the categorical character. If $beta in H H^2 \( A \)$, $h in H H_0 \( A \)$, and $delta in cal(T)^(- 2) \( A \)$, the definition of cap and the trace of a shifted perfect object give
  $ chevron.l beta ∩ h \, delta chevron.r_(A \, - 2) = chevron.l h \, delta \[ 2 \] compose beta chevron.r_(A \, 0) . $
  The shift is even, so its supertrace sign is $+ 1$. Applying this on $A$ and $B$, and using (5.32) and (5.34), gives
  $ chevron.l Phi_(*)^(H H) \( beta ∩ h \) \, Psi^(- 2) delta chevron.r_(B \, - 2) & = chevron.l beta ∩ h \, delta chevron.r_(A \, - 2)\
 & = chevron.l h \, delta \[ 2 \] compose beta chevron.r_(A \, 0)\
 & = chevron.l Phi_(*)^(H H) h \, \( Psi^(- 2) delta \) \[ 2 \] compose Phi^(H H) beta chevron.r_(B \, 0)\
 & = chevron.l Phi^(H H) beta ∩ Phi_(*)^(H H) h \, Psi^(- 2) delta chevron.r_(B \, - 2) . $
  The tests $Psi^(- 2) delta$ exhaust $cal(T)^(- 2) \( B \)$. Nondegeneracy proves the needed transport, and only in this degree:
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.36)")
   $ Phi_(*)^(H H) \( beta ∩ h \) = Phi^(H H) \( beta \) ∩ Phi_(*)^(H H) \( h \) \, #h(2em) beta in H H^2 \( A \) \, quad h in H H_0 \( A \) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:cap-transport>]
  Let $frak(h)_Q in H H_0 \( A \)$ correspond, under (5.29), to $h_Q = "Tr"_(Q \, H_A) \( A H_Q \)$. We verify its trace characterization using the same $A H_Q$ as in Proposition 25. For $delta : cal(O)_Delta arrow.r Sigma_A$, let $delta' : H_A arrow.r omega_A \[ d_A \]$ be its adjoint, so $delta = Delta_(*) delta' compose eta_Delta$. The adjunction $Delta_(!) tack.l L Delta^(*)$, defined by Serre duality above, is trace-compatible. Thus (5.24) and the standard partial-trace expression of the Serre trace give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.37)")
   $ chevron.l frak(h)_Q \, delta chevron.r_(A \, 0) & = "Tr"_(A \, cal(O)_A) \( delta' compose h_Q \)\
 & = "Tr"_(A \, cal(O)_A) ("Tr"_Q \( \( 1_Q ⊗ delta' \) compose A H_Q \))\
 & = "Tr"_(A \, Q) \( delta_Q \) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:character-trace>]
  The last equality uses evaluation of $Delta_(*) delta' compose eta_Delta$. It includes the supertrace when $Q$ is shifted. Because the pairing is perfect, this characterizes $frak(h)_Q$ uniquely; it is the categorical Chern character in $H H_0 \( A \)$. Its image under $upright(bold(I))_A$ is $"ch"_H \( Q \)$ by (5.20).
  For a test $delta_B = Psi^0 delta_A$, equations (5.34), (5.37), (5.33), and (5.31) yield
  $ chevron.l Phi_(*)^(H H) frak(h)_Q \, delta_B chevron.r_(B \, 0) & = chevron.l frak(h)_Q \, delta_A chevron.r_(A \, 0)\
 & = "Tr"_(A \, Q) \( \( delta_A \)_Q \)\
 & = "Tr"_(B \, Phi Q) \( \( delta_B \)_(Phi Q) \)\
 & = chevron.l frak(h)_(Phi Q) \, delta_B chevron.r_(B \, 0) . $
  The tests exhaust $cal(T)^0 \( B \)$, so uniqueness gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.38)")
   $ Phi_(*)^(H H) \( frak(h)_Q \) = frak(h)_(Phi Q) . $ <p3sw:hh-eq:character-transport>]
  Now take $alpha in H T^2 \( A \)$. By (5.16), the class $c_Q \( alpha \)$ is the $upright(bold(I))_A$-image of $"HKR"_A \( alpha \) ∩ frak(h)_Q$. Equations (5.36) and (5.38) take its homological transport to
  $ Phi^(H H) \( "HKR"_A \( alpha \) \) ∩ frak(h)_(Phi Q) . $
  Apply $upright(bold(I))_B$ and (5.16) on $B$. The result is $c_(Phi Q) \( Phi^(H T) alpha \)$, as required. The three comparison maps in (5.27) are isomorphisms. Taking kernels proves (5.28). ◻
]

===== 5.2.2.1 Source identification and shifts. <source-identification-and-shifts.>

The Serre-pairing definition also makes the source identification of homological transport explicit. Let $theta_(cal(C))$ be the trace-preserving Serre isomorphism of the equivalence $cal(C)_Phi$ between the two kernel categories. There is a unique

$ v : cal(C)_Phi \( Sigma_A^(- 1) \) arrow.r^tilde.op Sigma_B^(- 1) $

such that

$ u = sans(S)_(B times B) \( v \) compose \( theta_(cal(C)) \)_(Sigma_A^(- 1)) : cal(C)_Phi \( Sigma_A \) arrow.r Sigma_B . $

Indeed, apply the inverse Serre functor to $u compose \( theta_(cal(C)) \)_(Sigma_A^(- 1))^(- 1)$. Preservation of the Serre trace by $theta_(cal(C))$ then shows that (5.34) is exactly

$ h mapsto t compose cal(C)_Phi \( h \) compose v \[ m \]^(- 1) . $

The source identification is obtained from Serre and left–right adjunction coherence, whereas the target identification is $t$.

The distinction fixes the sign of a shift. Test it in $"Perf" \( bb(C) \)$ with $K = bb(C) \[ s \]$ and $L = bb(C) \[ - s \]$. The categorical trace of the identity of $bb(C) \[ s \]$ is $upright("ev") compose upright("braid") compose upright("coev") = \( - 1 \)^s$. Thus (5.31) forces $theta = \( - 1 \)^s$ in this test. It follows that $Psi^0$ is multiplication by $\( - 1 \)^s$, and (5.34) makes $Phi_(*)^(H H)$ on $H H_0$ multiplication by $\( - 1 \)^s$ as well. In agreement with perfect trace,

$ "ch"_H \( Q \[ s \] \) = \( - 1 \)^s "ch"_H \( Q \) . $

In particular a shift $\[ - 3 \]$ reverses this character. Cancelling $K$ and $L$ on both ends of a homology morphism without the Serre source identification would lose this sign. Degree-two evaluation has an even shift, so no additional odd exchange sign occurs there; it still uses the enhanced shift isomorphisms in (5.26).

The transported maps have source $H T^2$; no naturality of $sigma_Q$ on arbitrary Ext classes is asserted. The map $Phi_(H H)^H$ is defined by Hochschild homology and the fixed HKR maps, not identified with an independently normalized Betti integral transform.

=== 5.3 The invariant obstruction space of the Abel-orbit seed <p3sw:invariant-section>

Retain the seed of Proposition 17. Thus $C$ is a smooth nonhyperelliptic curve of genus three, $X = "Pic"^2 \( C \)$ has the specified group origin and is identified with its Jacobian, and $H = cal(O)_X \( Theta \)$ is its principal theta line. Put

$ theta_(upright(B)) = c_(1 \, upright(B)) \( H \) in H^2 \( X \, bb(Q) \) \, #h(2em) integral_X theta_(upright(B))^3 = 6 \, #h(2em) n = d + 1 gt.eq 4 . $

The two subschemes supplied there are disjoint unions

$ A = product.co_(j = 1)^n C_j \, #h(2em) Sigma = product.co_(j = 1)^n Sigma_j $

of translates of an Abel curve and of its inverse, respectively. The cyclic groups $G_2 \, G_1 subset X$, each of order $n$, act transitively on the components of $A \, Sigma$, respectively. Write

$ cal(I) = I_Sigma times.square I_A quad upright("on ") X^2 \, #h(2em) G_(upright("src")) = G_1 times G_2 . $

Each ideal has its canonical translation linearization as the ideal of an invariant subscheme, and $cal(I)$ has the product linearization. On $M = X times hat(X)$, the fixed transform of Proposition 24 is

$ tilde(Phi) = Phi compose #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( H times.square H \) ⊗ - #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, #h(2em) cal(G) = tilde(Phi) \( cal(I) \) \[ - 3 \] . $

Write $macron(G) tilde.eq G_(upright("src"))$ for the underlying translation group of its coherent line-translation action.

We will prove that the semiregularity map of Proposition 25 is injective on the full invariant derived group $"Ext"_M^2 \( cal(G) \, cal(G) \)^(macron(G))$. The proof has two computations: the invariant group for $cal(I)$ has dimension $48$, and contraction with its raw Hodge Chern character has rank $48$. The exact Hochschild–Atiyah identity then identifies the entire invariant group with the evaluation image. This calculation concerns the Abel-orbit seed in Markman’s secant-sheaf construction [25] Sections 8.3–8.4; we give the invariant calculation directly, using the independently movable orbit components.

==== 5.3.1 The Abel class and its normal directions <the-abel-class-and-its-normal-directions>

An #emph[Abel curve] in $X$ will mean a translate of the image of an Abel embedding $C arrow.r.hook X$, or a translate of its image under $\[ - 1 \]$, with the Jacobian and polarization conventions of Section 4.

#thm([Lemma 5.3.])[
  #emph[Let $D subset X$ be an Abel curve. Its normalized Betti class and Chern character are 
$ \[ D \]_(upright(B)) = theta_(upright(B))^2 / 2 \, #h(2em) "ch"_(upright(B)) \( cal(O)_D \) = theta_(upright(B))^2 / 2 - theta_(upright(B))^3 / 3 . $

 Normal restriction of translation-invariant vector fields is an isomorphism 
$ H^0 \( X \, T_X \) arrow.r^(med tilde.op med) H^0 \( D \, N_(D \/ X) \) \; $

 both spaces have dimension three. If $Z = product.co_(j = 1)^n D_j$ is a disjoint union of $n$ Abel curves, then its ideal satisfies the full normalized Betti identity 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.39)")
 $ "ch"_(upright(B)) \( I_Z \) = 1 - n / 2 theta_(upright(B))^2 + n / 3 theta_(upright(B))^3 . $ <p3sw:invariant-betti-character>]

]
] <p3sw:abel-character>

#proof[
  The fundamental-class formula is (4.6), proved there from the Jacobian lattice. Translation acts trivially on cohomology, and inversion acts by $+ 1$ on $H^4$, so it holds for every Abel curve in the stated sense.
  For the inclusion $i : D arrow.r.hook X$, use Grothendieck–Riemann–Roch in its smooth proper form
  $ "ch" \( i_(*) F \) "td" \( X \) = i_(*) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] "ch" \( F \) "td" \( D \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] $
  for a closed immersion of smooth projective varieties; this is the form of the Riemann–Roch theorem recorded by Borel and Serre [3]. The tangent bundle of $X$ is trivial, and $"td" \( D \) = 1 + 1 / 2 c_1 \( T_D \)$. Since $deg T_D = 2 - 2 dot.op 3 = - 4$ and $\[ upright("pt") \]_(upright(B)) = theta_(upright(B))^3 \/ 6$, it follows that
  $ "ch"_(upright(B)) \( cal(O)_D \) = i_(*) "td" \( D \) = \[ D \]_(upright(B)) - 2 \[ upright("pt") \]_(upright(B)) = theta_(upright(B))^2 / 2 - theta_(upright(B))^3 / 3 . $
  For disjoint $Z$, one has $cal(O)_Z = xor.big_j cal(O)_(D_j)$. Additivity in $0 arrow.r I_Z arrow.r cal(O)_X arrow.r cal(O)_Z arrow.r 0$ gives (5.39).
  It remains to calculate the normal sections. For the original Abel embedding the normal sequence is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.40)")
   $ 0 arrow.r T_C arrow.r cal(O)_C ⊗ H^1 \( C \, cal(O)_C \) arrow.r N_(C \/ X) arrow.r 0 . $ <p3sw:invariant-normal-sequence>]
  Here the middle bundle is $T_X \|_C$. The dual of the first arrow is the evaluation map $H^0 \( C \, K_C \) ⊗ cal(O)_C arrow.r K_C$: differentiating the Abel integral sends a tangent vector $v$ to the functional $omega mapsto omega \( v \)$. Consequently, under Serre duality on $C$, the dual of the map
  $ H^1 \( C \, T_C \) arrow.r H^1 \( C \, cal(O)_C \) ⊗ H^1 \( C \, cal(O)_C \) $
  in the long exact sequence of (5.40) is the multiplication map
  $ H^0 \( C \, K_C \) ⊗ H^0 \( C \, K_C \) arrow.r H^0 \( C \, K_C^2 \) . $
  This is the usual cohomological Serre duality [36], applied to the dual evaluation map.
  For completeness, multiplication is onto here by an elementary genus-three argument. For an effective divisor $D_2$ of length two, curve Riemann–Roch, in the form $h^0 \( L \) - h^0 \( K_C ⊗ L^(- 1) \) = deg L + 1 - g$, gives
  $ h^0 \( K_C - D_2 \) = h^0 \( D_2 \) = 1 . $
  The last equality uses nonhyperellipticity. Two independent sections of a degree-two effective line, after removing their common base divisor, would give a map to $bb(P)^1$ of degree at most two. Degree one would make $C$ rational, and degree two would make it hyperelliptic. Thus $K_C$ separates every length-two subscheme. It has three sections and degree four, so it embeds $C$ as an integral plane quartic. Such a curve is not contained in a conic. Restriction of quadrics therefore makes $"Sym"^2 H^0 \( K_C \) arrow.r H^0 \( K_C^2 \)$ injective. Both spaces have dimension six, the second by curve Riemann–Roch and $deg K_C^2 = 8 > 2 g - 2$. The multiplication map is consequently onto, and its dual is injective.
  Since $H^0 \( C \, T_C \) = 0$, the long exact sequence of (5.40) now identifies $H^0 \( C \, N_(C \/ X) \)$ with the middle bundle’s constant sections $H^1 \( C \, cal(O)_C \) = H^0 \( X \, T_X \)$. This is exactly normal restriction of translation-invariant vector fields. Translation and inversion carry the normal sequence and this isomorphism to every Abel curve. ◻
]

==== 5.3.2 The full invariant derived Ext group <the-full-invariant-derived-ext-group>

We next compute the derived deformation space of one orbit ideal. The calculation keeps the local-to-global contribution of the normal bundle; it does not replace derived Ext by the cohomology of an endomorphism sheaf.

#thm([Lemma 5.4.])[
  #emph[Let $Gamma subset X$ be a finite subgroup of order $n$, and let $Z = product.co_(j = 1)^n D_j$ be a disjoint union of Abel curves permuted transitively by $Gamma$. Give $I_Z$ its canonical linearization. There is an isomorphism of $Gamma$-representations 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.41)")
 $ "Ext"_X^1 \( I_Z \, I_Z \) tilde.eq H^1 \( X \, cal(O)_X \) xor xor.big_(j = 1)^n H^0 \( X \, T_X \) . $ <p3sw:invariant-orbit-representation>]

 The first summand is trivial, and $Gamma$ acts on the second by its regular permutation action on the components and the identity on each translation space. In particular, 
$ dim_(bb(C)) "Ext"_X^1 \( I_Z \, I_Z \)^Gamma = 6 \, #h(2em) dim_(bb(C)) "Ext"_X^2 \( I_Z \, I_Z \)^Gamma = 6 \, #h(2em) "Hom"_X \( I_Z \, I_Z \)^Gamma = bb(C) . $

]
] <p3sw:invariant-orbit-ext>

#proof[
  At a point of $Z$, write its regular codimension-two ideal in the regular local ring $R = cal(O)_(X \, x)$ as $I = \( f \, g \)$, where $f \, g$ are part of a regular system of parameters. It has the resolution
  $ 0 arrow.r R arrow.r^(\( - g \, f \)) R^2 arrow.r^(\( f \, g \)) I arrow.r 0 . $
  If an endomorphism sends $f \, g$ to $u \, v$, its relation is $g u = f v$. Regularity of $f \, g$ gives $u = f r$, $v = g r$ for a unique $r in R$, so $"Hom"_R \( I \, I \) = R$. Applying $"Hom"_R \( - \, I \)$ to the resolution shows that the degree-one cokernel is $I \/ I^2$. Under a change of generators, the dual of the leftmost generator acquires the inverse determinant. Consequently the global degree-one sheaf is
  $ \( I_Z \/ I_Z^2 \) ⊗ det \( I_Z \/ I_Z^2 \)^(- 1) tilde.eq \( I_Z \/ I_Z^2 \)^or = N_(Z \/ X) . $
  The rank-two isomorphism sends $v ⊗ ell^or$ to the functional $w mapsto ell^or \( v and w \)$, so it is compatible with changes of generators. We view $N_(Z \/ X)$ here as a sheaf on $X$ supported on $Z$. Away from $Z$ the ideal is $cal(O)_X$, and the resolution has length one. Thus, globally,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.42)")
   $ cal(H) o m_X \( I_Z \, I_Z \) = cal(O)_X \, #h(2em) cal(E) x t_X^1 \( I_Z \, I_Z \) = N_(Z \/ X) \, #h(2em) cal(E) x t_X^q \( I_Z \, I_Z \) = 0 quad \( q gt.eq 2 \) . $ <p3sw:invariant-local-ext>]
  The standard local-to-global spectral sequence
  $ E_2^(p \, q) = H^p #h(-0.16667em) (X \, cal(E) x t_X^q \( I_Z \, I_Z \)) arrow.r.double "Ext"_X^(p + q) \( I_Z \, I_Z \) $
  is the hypercohomology spectral sequence of $R cal(H) o m_X \( I_Z \, I_Z \)$. Its five-term sequence and (5.42) give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.43)")
   $ 0 arrow.r H^1 \( X \, cal(O)_X \) arrow.r "Ext"_X^1 \( I_Z \, I_Z \) arrow.r^(thin e thin) H^0 \( Z \, N_(Z \/ X) \) arrow.r H^2 \( X \, cal(O)_X \) . $ <p3sw:invariant-local-global>]
  The first arrow is also the map induced by the perfect unit $cal(O)_X arrow.r R cal(H) o m \( I_Z \, I_Z \)$: the perfect trace composed with this unit is multiplication by the rank, which is one.
  Translate the $n$ curves independently. The locus in $X^n$ where these translated proper curves remain disjoint is an open neighborhood of the original tuple. Over it their union is a disjoint union of flat translated families. Its ideal is flat as well, by the exact sequence from the structure sheaf of the union. Restriction of this family to first-order tangent vectors gives a linear map
  $ s : xor.big_(j = 1)^n H^0 \( X \, T_X \) arrow.r "Ext"_X^1 \( I_Z \, I_Z \) . $
  Indeed a flat deformation over $bb(C) \[ epsilon.alt \] \/ \( epsilon.alt^2 \)$ gives the extension $0 arrow.r I_Z arrow.r I_(Z \, epsilon.alt) arrow.r I_Z arrow.r 0$. For a vector field $xi$, the moving generators are $f - epsilon.alt xi \( f \)$ and $g - epsilon.alt xi \( g \)$. Lifting the displayed relation $\( - g \, f \)$ gives
  $ \( - g \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] f - epsilon.alt xi \( f \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] + f #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] g - epsilon.alt xi \( g \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = epsilon.alt #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] g xi \( f \) - f xi \( g \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  Thus the local Ext cocycle is represented by $overline(g xi \( f \) - f xi \( g \)) in I \/ I^2$, up to the single sign in the chosen Ext boundary convention. Choose $ell = macron(f) and macron(g)$ and $ell^or \( ell \) = 1$. Under the written determinant identification $v ⊗ ell^or mapsto \( w mapsto ell^or \( v and w \) \)$, this displayed representative sends $macron(f) \, macron(g)$ to $- overline(xi \( f \)) \, - overline(xi \( g \))$, respectively. Reversing the boundary convention reverses both signs. Consequently the edge map $e$ sends the geometric deformation to its normal restriction up to one fixed sign, independent of the vector field and the component. By Lemma 27, $e compose s$ is therefore the direct sum of the normal-restriction isomorphisms multiplied by that fixed sign. It follows that (5.43) reduces to the split short exact sequence
  $ 0 arrow.r H^1 \( X \, cal(O)_X \) arrow.r "Ext"_X^1 \( I_Z \, I_Z \) arrow.r^e H^0 \( Z \, N_(Z \/ X) \) arrow.r 0 \, $
  with splitting given by $s$ after this signed normal-restriction identification.
  This splitting is equivariant. Translation acts trivially on $H^1 \( X \, cal(O)_X \)$, as is seen from translation-invariant Dolbeault representatives, and it acts as the identity on $H^0 \( X \, T_X \)$. It sends the deformation of $D_j$ to the deformation of its translated component. Since a group of order $n$ acts transitively on $n$ components, that permutation action is regular. This proves (5.41); its invariant summands have dimensions three and three.
  We also use Serre duality for perfect complexes on a smooth projective threefold:
  $ "Ext"^i \( F \, G \)^or tilde.eq "Ext"^(3 - i) \( G \, F ⊗ omega_X \) . $
  This is the extension of the usual locally free Serre duality [36] by finite locally free resolutions. Choose a nonzero translation-invariant holomorphic three-form on $X$. For $F = G = I_Z$, the resulting perfect pairing is
  $ "Ext"^1 \( I_Z \, I_Z \) ⊗ "Ext"^2 \( I_Z \, I_Z \) arrow.r H^3 \( X \, cal(O)_X \) tilde.eq bb(C) . $
  It is $Gamma$-invariant: translations preserve the chosen form, composition, and the perfect trace. Thus these two Ext groups are dual $Gamma$-representations. Averaging over the finite group in characteristic zero identifies invariants of the dual with the dual of the invariants, so the invariant $"Ext"^2$ also has dimension six. Finally (5.42) gives $"Hom" \( I_Z \, I_Z \) = H^0 \( X \, cal(O)_X \) = bb(C)$, with trivial action. ◻
]

#thm([Proposition 5.5.])[
  #emph[For the source object $cal(I) = I_Sigma times.square I_A$ and its canonical $G_(upright("src")) = G_1 times G_2$ linearization, 
$ dim_(bb(C)) "Ext"_(X^2)^2 \( cal(I) \, cal(I) \)^(G_(upright("src"))) = 6 + 36 + 6 = 48 . $

 This is the invariant subspace of the full derived Ext group.]
] <p3sw:invariant-ext>

#proof[
  The ideal sheaves are perfect by their local length-one resolutions. The perfect Künneth isomorphism for their derived global Hom complexes is
  $ upright(bold(R)) "Hom"_(X^2) \( cal(I) \, cal(I) \) tilde.eq upright(bold(R)) "Hom"_X \( I_Sigma \, I_Sigma \) ⊗_(bb(C))^(upright(bold(L))) upright(bold(R)) "Hom"_X \( I_A \, I_A \) . $
  Taking degree two gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.44)")
   $ "Ext"_(X^2)^2 \( cal(I) \, cal(I) \) tilde.eq  & "Ext"_X^2 \( I_Sigma \, I_Sigma \) ⊗ "Hom"_X \( I_A \, I_A \)\
 & xor "Ext"_X^1 \( I_Sigma \, I_Sigma \) ⊗ "Ext"_X^1 \( I_A \, I_A \)\
 & xor "Hom"_X \( I_Sigma \, I_Sigma \) ⊗ "Ext"_X^2 \( I_A \, I_A \) . $ <p3sw:invariant-product-ext>]
  Here the isomorphism follows by taking cohomology of the tensor product over $bb(C)$ of the two derived global Hom complexes; over the field $bb(C)$ there are no additional Tor terms. It is equivariant for the independent actions of $G_1 \, G_2$. Averaging in each factor gives $\( V_1 ⊗ V_2 \)^(G_1 times G_2) = V_1^(G_1) ⊗ V_2^(G_2)$. Applying Lemma 28 to the three summands of (5.44) gives $6$, $6 dot.op 6$, and $6$. Because (5.44) is a decomposition of derived Ext itself, the count retains every local-to-global contribution. ◻
]

==== 5.3.3 The rank of contraction with the raw Hodge character <the-rank-of-contraction-with-the-raw-hodge-character>

We now compute the map that Proposition 25 factors through this $48$-dimensional invariant group. Use the gradings (5.1); in particular, for an abelian variety $B$,

$ H Omega_(- k) \( B \) = xor.big_(q - p = k) H^q \( B \, Omega_B^p \) #h(2em) \( k in bb(Z) \) . $

For a perfect object $Q$ on $B$, extend character contraction to every degree $k gt.eq 0$ by

$ c_Q^k : H T^k \( B \) arrow.r H Omega_(- k) \( B \) \, quad c_Q^k \( alpha \) = alpha corner.r.b "ch"_(upright(H)) \( Q \) . $

Here $"ch"_(upright(H))$ is the raw first-jet character (5.7), and contraction uses the left-interior action (5.3), including its Dolbeault Koszul sign. Thus $c_Q^2 = c_Q$ in Proposition 25.

The normalization of the character matters for that identity. Put $theta_(upright(H)) = a_H in H^1 \( X \, Omega_X^1 \)$. Under the raw de Rham comparison,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.45)")
 $ theta_(upright(H)) mapsto \( 2 pi i \) theta_(upright(B)) \, #h(2em) "ch"_(upright(H) \, k) \( Q \) mapsto \( 2 pi i \)^k "ch"_(upright(B) \, k) \( Q \) quad upright("in codimension ") k . $ <p3sw:invariant-weight-dictionary>]

This is the weightwise dictionary of Proposition 25; the raw first-jet class is the negative of the Buchweitz–Flenner Atiyah convention. In particular Lemma 27 gives for either orbit ideal

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.46)")
 $ f_n := "ch"_(upright(H)) \( I_Z \) = 1 - n / 2 theta_(upright(H))^2 + n / 3 theta_(upright(H))^3 . $ <p3sw:invariant-hodge-character>]

There is no codimension-one component. Each term in (5.46) has the raw weight specified by (5.45).

Use the abelian Hodge-cohomology identifications

$ sans(U) = H^1 \( X \, cal(O)_X \) \, #h(2em) sans(W) = H^0 \( X \, Omega_X^1 \) \, #h(2em) sans(T) = H^0 \( X \, T_X \) = sans(W)^or \, #h(2em) H^q \( X \, Omega_X^p \) = and^q sans(U) ⊗ and^p sans(W) . $

These follow from the invariant Dolbeault forms on the complex torus. The spaces $sans(U) \, sans(W) \, sans(T)$ each have dimension three. The polarization class $theta_(upright(H)) in sans(U) ⊗ sans(W)$ is nondegenerate, so in the fixed contraction convention

$ j : sans(T) arrow.r sans(U) \, #h(2em) j \( xi \) = xi corner.r.b theta_(upright(H)) $

is an isomorphism.

#thm([Lemma 5.6.])[
  #emph[For either orbit ideal $I_Z$, the map $c_(I_Z)^1$ is injective of rank six, and $c_(I_Z)^2$ is surjective of rank six.]
] <p3sw:invariant-one-factor-rank>

#proof[
  An element of $H T^1 \( X \) = sans(U) xor sans(T)$ is a pair $\( eta \, xi \)$. Using (5.46), its projections under $c_(I_Z)^1$ to $H^1 \( cal(O)_X \)$ and $H^2 \( Omega_X^1 \)$ are respectively
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.47)")
   $ eta \, #h(2em) - n thin j \( xi \) and theta_(upright(H)) . $ <p3sw:invariant-degree-one-projections>]
  The first uses the rank term of the character; the second uses $- n theta_(upright(H))^2 \/ 2$. There is no contribution from $xi$ to the first projection or from $eta$ to the second, because the codimension-one character is zero. The signs in (5.47) are those of the specified left-interior convention.
  The map $v mapsto v and theta_(upright(H))$ on $sans(U)$ is injective. If $v eq.not 0$, choose bases with $v = u_1$ and $theta_(upright(H)) = sum_(i = 1)^3 u_i ⊗ w_i$, absorbing the fixed contraction signs in the dual basis. The terms of $v and theta_(upright(H))$ with $i = 2 \, 3$ are linearly independent nonzero tensors. Therefore the two projections in (5.47) determine $eta$ and $xi$, proving the first assertion.
  The only summands of $H Omega_(- 2) \( X \)$ are
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.48)")
   $ H^2 \( X \, cal(O)_X \) = and^2 sans(U) \, #h(2em) H^3 \( X \, Omega_X^1 \) = and^3 sans(U) ⊗ sans(W) . $ <p3sw:invariant-degree-two-target>]
  Each has dimension three. The $H^2 \( cal(O)_X \) = and^2 sans(U)$ block of $H T^2 \( X \)$ maps identically to the first summand, using the rank term $f_(n \, 0) = 1$. The $H^1 \( T_X \) = sans(U) ⊗ sans(T)$ block has no contribution there, since $f_(n \, 1) = 0$, and its projection to the second summand is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.49)")
   $ eta ⊗ xi mapsto - n thin eta and j \( xi \) and theta_(upright(H)) . $ <p3sw:invariant-degree-two-map>]
  For each $k in { 1 \, 2 \, 3 }$, take $eta$ and $j \( xi \)$ to be the two basis elements $u_i \, u_j$ complementary to $u_k$. Their wedge with $theta_(upright(H))$ is a nonzero multiple of $\( u_1 and u_2 and u_3 \) ⊗ w_k$. Hence (5.49) spans the second summand. These two source blocks already give rank six; the target in (5.48) has dimension six. This proves surjectivity. The bivector block $H^0 \( and^2 T_X \)$ is not needed for this lower bound. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 5.7.])[
  #emph[For $cal(I) = I_Sigma times.square I_A$, the map 
$ c_(cal(I))^2 : H T^2 \( X^2 \) arrow.r H Omega_(- 2) \( X^2 \) $

 has rank $48$.]
] <p3sw:invariant-product-rank>

#proof[
  Künneth gives the domain decomposition
  $ H T^2 \( X^2 \) = #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] H T^2 \( X \) ⊗ H T^0 \( X \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] xor #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] H T^1 \( X \) ⊗ H T^1 \( X \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] xor #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] H T^0 \( X \) ⊗ H T^2 \( X \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  The product character is $f_n times.square f_n$, and $H T^0 \( X \) = bb(C)$ acts by $c_(I_Z)^0 \( 1 \) = f_n eq.not 0$. The written left-action and product formulas (5.3) and (5.4), applied to the Künneth decomposition, give the external-product contraction rule with its total-complex signs. They therefore send the three blocks onto
  $ "im" \( c_(I_Sigma)^2 \) ⊗ bb(C) f_n \, #h(2em) "im" \( c_(I_Sigma)^1 \) ⊗ "im" \( c_(I_A)^1 \) \, #h(2em) bb(C) f_n ⊗ "im" \( c_(I_A)^2 \) . $
  Their dimensions are $6 \, 36 \, 6$, by Lemma 30. The target has the direct Künneth decomposition
  $ H Omega_(- 2) \( X^2 \) = xor.big_(r + s = 2) H Omega_(- r) \( X \) ⊗ H Omega_(- s) \( X \) . $
  The first image lies in the factorwise Hodge-imbalance summand $\( 2 \, 0 \)$, the second in $\( 1 \, 1 \)$, and the third in $\( 0 \, 2 \)$. Here the imbalance of $H^q \( X \, Omega_X^p \)$ is $q - p$; the two-factor Hodge Künneth decomposition is a direct sum for these ordered pairs. Hence the three images cannot cancel, regardless of their Künneth signs. Their ranks add to $6 + 36 + 6 = 48$. ◻
]

==== 5.3.4 The exact rank comparison and the fixed transform <the-exact-rank-comparison-and-the-fixed-transform>

The two calculations now have the same dimension. We use the exact local Hochschild results to turn this equality into injectivity on the whole invariant group. Write $o_Q = "ob"_Q$ for evaluation and $sigma_Q$ for the perfect-trace map of Proposition 25. For a perfect object $Q$ on an abelian variety $B$, their domains and targets are

$ H T^2 \( B \) arrow.r^(med o_Q med) "Ext"_B^2 \( Q \, Q \) arrow.r^(med sigma_Q med) H Omega_(- 2) \( B \) . $

#thm([Proposition 5.8.])[
  #emph[For the source $cal(I) = I_Sigma times.square I_A$ with its canonical $G_(upright("src")) = G_1 times G_2$-linearization, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.50)")
 $ "im" \( o_(cal(I)) \) = "Ext"_(X^2)^2 \( cal(I) \, cal(I) \)^(G_(upright("src"))) \, #h(2em) ker \( o_(cal(I)) \) = ker \( c_(cal(I))^2 \) \, $ <p3sw:invariant-source-conclusion>]

 and $sigma_(cal(I))$ is injective on this full invariant group.]
  #emph[Let $M = X times hat(X)$ and use the fixed enhanced equivalence 
$ tilde(Phi) = Phi compose #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( H times.square H \) ⊗ - #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, #h(2em) cal(G) = tilde(Phi) \( cal(I) \) \[ - 3 \] . $

 Give $cal(G)$ the coherent line-translation action from Proposition 24, and denote its underlying translation group by $macron(G) tilde.eq G_(upright("src"))$. Then 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.51)")
 $ "im" \( o_(cal(G)) \) = "Ext"_M^2 \( cal(G) \, cal(G) \)^(macron(G)) \, #h(2em) ker \( o_(cal(G)) \) = ker \( c_(cal(G))^2 \) \, $ <p3sw:invariant-target-conclusion>]

 and 
$ sigma_(cal(G)) \|_("Ext"_M^2 \( cal(G) \, cal(G) \)^(macron(G))) quad upright("is injective") . $

]
] <p3sw:invariant-injectivity>

#proof[
  Proposition 25 proves for every perfect $Q$ the exact identity
  $ sigma_Q compose o_Q = c_Q^2 $
  with the definitions just given. In particular it is an identity for the perfect trace on derived Ext, with the same contraction convention as the rank computation. The components with $q > dim B - 2$ vanish because $H^(q + 2) \( B \, Omega_B^q \) = 0$.
  Translations act trivially on $H T^2 \( X^2 \)$: they fix the invariant tangent fields and the invariant Dolbeault representatives of $H^a \( cal(O)_(X^2) \)$. The translation-invariant diagonal-kernel HKR construction identifies this with their trivial action on $H H^2 \( X^2 \)$. Apply the diagonal-kernel evaluation square (5.26) to each translation equivalence, and identify its translate of $cal(I)$ with $cal(I)$ by the canonical linearization. Naturality of evaluation then gives $g dot.op o_(cal(I)) \( alpha \) = o_(cal(I)) \( g dot.op alpha \) = o_(cal(I)) \( alpha \)$ for $g in G_(upright("src"))$. Hence
  $ "im" \( o_(cal(I)) \) subset W_(upright("inv")) := "Ext"_(X^2)^2 \( cal(I) \, cal(I) \)^(G_(upright("src"))) . $
  Proposition 29 gives $dim W_(upright("inv")) = 48$, while Lemma 31 and the exact identity give
  $ 48 = "rank" \( c_(cal(I))^2 \) lt.eq "rank" \( o_(cal(I)) \) lt.eq dim W_(upright("inv")) = 48 . $
  Thus the inclusion is equality and both maps have rank $48$. The identity also gives $ker \( o_(cal(I)) \) subset ker \( c_(cal(I))^2 \)$; equality of ranks on their common domain makes the kernels equal. If $w = o_(cal(I)) \( alpha \) in W_(upright("inv"))$ and $sigma_(cal(I)) \( w \) = 0$, then $c_(cal(I))^2 \( alpha \) = 0$, so the kernel equality gives $w = 0$. This proves (5.50) and source injectivity.
  Apply Proposition 26 to the fixed enhanced equivalence $Q mapsto tilde(Phi) \( Q \) \[ - 3 \]$. It includes tensoring by $H times.square H$, the fixed Fourier–Mukai kernel, and the displayed shift. That proposition gives isomorphisms
  $ u : H T^2 \( X^2 \) arrow.r^tilde.op H T^2 \( M \) \, quad v : "Ext"^2 \( cal(I) \, cal(I) \) arrow.r^tilde.op "Ext"^2 \( cal(G) \, cal(G) \) \, quad gamma : H Omega_(- 2) \( X^2 \) arrow.r^tilde.op H Omega_(- 2) \( M \) $
  such that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.52)")
   $ v thin o_(cal(I)) = o_(cal(G)) thin u \, #h(2em) gamma thin c_(cal(I))^2 = c_(cal(G))^2 thin u . $ <p3sw:invariant-transport-squares>]
  The map $gamma$ is the HKR expression of the enhanced Hochschild-homology isomorphism: its cap and character naturality, including the shift sign, is exactly what gives the second equality. No identification of $gamma$ with a separately normalized Betti integral transform is used.
  Proposition 24 constructs the target action by coherent conjugation of the canonical source action by this same equivalence. Hence $v$ is an isomorphism of the corresponding representations. The lines in the target line-translation action cancel on derived endomorphisms, and the underlying group is the specified $macron(G) tilde.eq G_(upright("src"))$. Therefore
  $ v \( W_(upright("inv")) \) = "Ext"_M^2 \( cal(G) \, cal(G) \)^(macron(G)) . $
  The first equality of (5.52) transports the source image equality to the image equality in (5.51). Both $u$ and $gamma$ are isomorphisms, so its second equality transports the source kernel equality to the target kernel equality. Finally apply $sigma_(cal(G)) o_(cal(G)) = c_(cal(G))^2$, the target instance of Proposition 25. An invariant $z$ has the form $o_(cal(G)) \( alpha \)$; if $sigma_(cal(G)) \( z \) = 0$, the target kernel equality gives $z = 0$. This proves injectivity on the full target invariant Ext group. ◻
]

The fixed support construction identifies

$ cal(E) = R cal(H) o m_M \( cal(G) \, cal(O)_M \) \[ - 1 \] $

as the actual coherent perfect sheaf of positive rank, with the dual coherent action on the same underlying translation group. Proposition 40 uses the full injection just proved, transfers it by perfect duality, and then performs finite étale twisted descent. The exact dual sign and that derived descent are left to that proposition. The output here is injection on $"Ext"^2 \( cal(G) \, cal(G) \)^(macron(G))$ itself, not only on a chosen Hochschild subspace. No identification of that derived Ext group with $H^2 \( M \, cal(H)^0 \( R cal(H) o m_M \( cal(G) \, cal(G) \) \) \)$ is used.

== 6 The spin character and the polarized quadratic seed <p3:sec:spin>

The preceding construction gives a coherent perfect sheaf $cal(E)$ of rank $r = 8 d$ on $M = X times hat(X)$. Its normalized rational Betti character is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.1)")
 $ kappa_M = exp #h(-0.16667em) (- frac(c_(1 \, upright(B)) \( cal(E) \), r)) "ch"_(upright(B)) \( cal(E) \) \, $ <p3sw:spin-eq:normalized-character>]

where $"ch"_(upright(B))$ and $c_(1 \, upright(B))$ are the ordinary rational Betti Chern character and first Chern class. We compute $kappa_M$ and identify the polarized quadratic structure on this same sixfold. The calculation uses the secant spinors of Markman [25] Sections 2.4 and 6. We give a finite Clifford proof of the required transform identity, so the identity applies to the algebraic spin group, including its elements that do not preserve the initial Hodge structure.

=== 6.1 The fixed transform and its source character <the-fixed-transform-and-its-source-character>

Put $ell_X = 6 = 2 g_X$, with $g_X = 3$, and set

$ L = H^1 \( X \, bb(Q) \) \, #h(2em) U = L^or = H^1 \( hat(X) \, bb(Q) \) \, #h(2em) V = L xor U . $

Choose $e_1 \, dots.h \, e_(ell_X)$ with $integral_X e_1 and dots.h.c and e_(ell_X) = 1$, and let $f_1 \, dots.h \, f_(ell_X)$ be the dual basis of $U$. Our normalized Poincaré line has class

$ p = c_(1 \, upright(B)) \( cal(P) \) = sum_(i = 1)^(ell_X) e_i and f_i . $

For an ordered subset $P subset { 1 \, dots.h \, ell_X }$, write $e_P$ and $f_P$ for the corresponding increasing products. For disjoint ordered subsets $I \, J$, let $epsilon.alt_(I \, J)$ be the sign in $e_I e_J = epsilon.alt_(I \, J) e_(I union J)$, and set it to zero if they overlap. Define reversal by

$ tau \( e_P \) = \( - 1 \)^(\| P \| \( \| P \| - 1 \) \/ 2) e_P . $

We use Grothendieck–Riemann–Roch in the following standard form: if $f : B arrow.r C$ is a proper morphism of smooth projective complex varieties and $F$ is perfect, then

$ "ch"_(upright(B)) \( R f_(*) F \) "td" \( C \) = f_(*) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] "ch"_(upright(B)) \( F \) "td" \( B \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $

This is the Riemann–Roch theorem of Borel and Serre [3]. In a Fourier transform between abelian varieties the Todd classes are one. This gives the usual cohomological Poincaré transform; compare the calculation in [17] Section 4.2.3. The second-factor transform in the fixed functor $Phi$ of (4.29) uses the inverse Poincaré line and the shift $\[ g_X \]$. It is therefore

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.2)")
 $ F_(-) \( alpha \) = \( - 1 \)^(g_X) \( pi_(hat(X)) \)_(*) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] e^(- p) pi_X^(*) alpha #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <p3sw:spin-eq:fourier>]

The factor $\( - 1 \)^(g_X)$ is the Chern-character sign of the shift.

#thm([Lemma 6.1.])[
  (The fixed Fourier matrix).  #emph[For $P subset { 1 \, dots.h \, ell_X }$, $k = \| P \|$, one has 
$ F_(-) \( e_P \) = \( - 1 \)^(k \( k + 3 \) \/ 2) epsilon.alt_(P \, P^c) f_(P^c) . $

 In particular $F_(-)$ is a rational isomorphism preserving parity.]
] <p3sw:fourier-matrix>

#proof[
  In (6.2), only the complementary indices in $e^(- p)$ can contribute to integration on $X$. Moving their $f$-factors past the $e$-factors gives the coefficient
  $ \( - 1 \)^(thin g_X + \( ell_X - k \) \( ell_X - k + 1 \) \/ 2) epsilon.alt_(P \, P^c) . $
  The difference of the displayed exponent and $k \( k + 3 \) \/ 2$ is $2 \( g_X + 1 \) \( g_X - k \)$, since $ell_X = 2 g_X$, and hence is even. Every basis vector goes to a signed complementary basis vector, proving the remaining assertions. ◻
]

For $mu \( x \, y \) = \( x + y \, y \)$ define the graded Künneth maps

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.3)")
 $ phi.alt = \( 1 ⊗ F_(-) \) mu^(*) \, #h(2em) nu = \( F_(-) ⊗ 1 \) mu^(*) \, #h(2em) D = F_(-)^(- 1) ⊗ F_(-) \, #h(2em) phi.alt' = phi.alt \( 1 ⊗ tau \) = D nu \( 1 ⊗ tau \) . $ <p3sw:spin-eq:fixed-maps>]

The input of $D$ is written in the order $U xor L$, and its output in the order $L xor U$; all identifications use the graded shuffle. These are fixed rational isomorphisms, determined only by the oriented lattice, addition, and $p$. Equivalently,

$ phi.alt' \( s ⊗ t \) = \( - 1 \)^(g_X) integral_(X_y) s \( x + y \) tau \( t \) \( y \) e^(- sum_i y_i z_i) . $

This is finite exterior-algebra coefficient extraction in $y_1 dots.h.c y_(ell_X)$; the output variables $x \, z$ represent $L \, U$. In particular the maps do not vary with a complex period having the given marking.

We now identify the tensor to which this transform applies. Write $Theta = c_(1 \, upright(B)) \( H \) in Lambda^2 L$ for the principal class of the seed. Both orbit ideals have $n = d + 1$ disjoint Abel components, so Lemma 27 gives their common Betti character

$ f_n = 1 - n / 2 Theta^2 + n / 3 Theta^3 . $

Thus the sheaves $F_1 = I_A \( H \)$ and $F_2 = I_Sigma \( H \)$ used in $Phi$ have common character

$ v = e^Theta f_(d + 1) = 1 + Theta - d / 2 Theta^2 - d / 6 Theta^3 . $

Fix $K = bb(Q) \( u \)$, where $u^2 = - d$ and $d gt.eq 3$ is the integer of the seed construction, and put

$ lambda_(+) = e^(u Theta) \, #h(2em) lambda_(-) = e^(- u Theta) quad upright("in ") \( Lambda L \)_K . $

Expanding the exponentials in powers of $Theta$ gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.4)")
 $ v = 1 / 2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\[] \( 1 + u^(- 1) \) lambda_(+) + \( 1 - u^(- 1) \) lambda_(-) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\]] . $ <p3sw:spin-eq:source-character>]

The two exponential classes are linearly independent: both have constant term $1$, and their degree-two terms are opposite and nonzero. Reversal acts by $\( - 1 \)^j$ on $Theta^j$, so it interchanges $lambda_(+)$ and $lambda_(-)$. The rational tensor $T = v ⊗ tau v$ therefore lies in the tensor square of their two-dimensional $K$-span. Grothendieck–Riemann–Roch gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.5)")
 $ beta := "ch"_(upright(B)) Phi \( F_2 times.square F_1 \) = phi.alt \( v ⊗ v \) = phi.alt' \( T \) . $ <p3sw:spin-eq:sheaf-transform>]

To relate this class to the original sheaf, let $iota$ be the ring involution acting by $\( - 1 \)^j$ on $H^(2 j) \( M \, bb(Q) \)$. The shifts in $cal(G) = Phi \( F_2 times.square F_1 \) \[ - 3 \]$ and $cal(E) = D_M \( cal(G) \) \[ - 1 \]$ give

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.6)")
 $ cal(E) = D_M #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] Phi \( F_2 times.square F_1 \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \[ 2 \] \, #h(2em) "ch"_(upright(B)) \( cal(E) \) = iota beta \, #h(2em) beta_0 = r = 8 d . $ <p3sw:spin-eq:sheaf-duality>]

Here the rank is supplied by Proposition 23. We next introduce the Clifford action on $Lambda L$ and compare its transport through $phi.alt'$ with the graded action on $H^(*) \( M \)$. The exponential correction will disappear under the normalization defining $kappa_M$.

=== 6.2 The exact Clifford identity <the-exact-clifford-identity>

Give $V$ the symmetric split form

$ Q \( e_i \, f_j \) = delta_(i j) \, #h(2em) Q \( e_i \, e_j \) = Q \( f_i \, f_j \) = 0 . $

Use the Clifford algebra with relation $v w + w v = Q \( v \, w \)$. We use the standard Clifford construction of the spin group in this normalization: the norm-one even Clifford normalizer $"Spin" \( V \, Q \)$ acts on $V$ by conjugation through $rho : "Spin" \( V \, Q \) arrow.r S O \( V \, Q \)$, and acts on a Clifford module by multiplication. The norm is Clifford reversion times the element. For this split rational construction, see [17] Sections 3.2 and 3.4–3.5. Only this construction, not a realization of arbitrary spin elements by derived autoequivalences, is used here.

On $S = Lambda L$ the Fock action is

$ c \( e \) = epsilon.alt_e = e and - \, #h(2em) c \( f \) = iota_f . $

Write $m_g = c \( g \)$. Conjugation by the fixed $phi.alt'$ defines the rational algebraic representation

$ rho'_g = phi.alt' \( m_g ⊗ m_g \) \( phi.alt' \)^(- 1) quad upright("on ") Lambda V . $

The following calculation is the matrix-unit Clifford realization underlying [17] Proposition 3.2.1(e), with the signs of the fixed transform retained.

#thm([Proposition 6.2.])[
  (Fixed Clifford identity).  #emph[For every algebraic spin element $g$, one has the exact equality of rational representations 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.7)")
 $ rho'_g = e^(p \/ 2) Lambda rho \( g \) e^(- p \/ 2) = e^(\( p - Lambda^2 rho \( g \) p \) \/ 2) Lambda rho \( g \) . $ <p3sw:spin-eq:identity>]

 The exponentials denote exterior multiplication. In particular 
$ \[ rho'_g \( 1 \) \]_0 = 1 \, #h(2em) \[ rho'_g \( 1 \) \]_2 = 1 / 2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] p - Lambda^2 rho \( g \) p #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $

]
] <p3sw:spin-identity>

#proof[
  Exterior multiplication and contraction give every matrix unit between the monomial basis vectors of $S$: first project onto a specified monomial by products of the commuting occupation projections $epsilon.alt_(e_i) iota_(f_i)$ and $iota_(f_i) epsilon.alt_(e_i)$, and then remove and insert the required generators. The ordered Clifford monomials span at most $2^(2 ell_X)$ dimensions, which equals $dim "End" \( S \)$. Thus
  $ C \( V \, Q \) tilde.eq "End" \( S \) . $
  Its even part is the parity-preserving part: the inclusion follows from parity of the generators, and both sides have dimension $2^(2 ell_X - 1)$. Put $F = f_1 dots.h.c f_(ell_X)$ in the Clifford algebra and define
  $ phi : S ⊗ S arrow.r C \( V \, Q \) \, #h(2em) phi \( s ⊗ t \) = s F tau \( t \) . $
  On $S$ this is the rank-one operator
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.8)")
   $ c \( phi \( s ⊗ t \) \) \( r \) = \( - 1 \)^(g_X) \[ tau \( t \) and r \]_(upright("top")) thin s . $ <p3sw:spin-eq:rank-one>]
  Indeed $F$ contracts the full oriented monomial with sign $\( - 1 \)^(ell_X \( ell_X - 1 \) \/ 2) = \( - 1 \)^(g_X)$. The pairing $chevron.l t \, r chevron.r = \[ tau \( t \) and r \]_(upright("top"))$ is perfect. Exterior multiplication is self-adjoint for it by reversal of products. Contraction is self-adjoint as well: apply $iota_f$ to a zero product in exterior degree $ell_X + 1$ and use
  $ tau iota_f \|_(Lambda^k L) = \( - 1 \)^(k - 1) iota_f tau \|_(Lambda^k L) . $
  Consequently Clifford reversion is adjunction for this pairing. Every spin element has reversion norm one, and therefore preserves the pairing. Formula (6.8) now proves that $phi$ is an isomorphism intertwining the diagonal spin action with conjugation on $C \( V \, Q \)$.
  For any bilinear form $B$ on $V$ with $B \( v \, v \) = Q \( v \, v \) \/ 2$, set
  $ c_B \( v \) = epsilon.alt_v + iota_(B \( v \, - \)) quad upright("on ") Lambda V \, #h(2em) psi_B \( x \) = c_B \( x \) 1 . $
  The Clifford relations follow directly from the exterior and contraction relations. On each ordered Clifford monomial $psi_B$ has that exterior monomial as its leading term, so $psi_B$ is a triangular isomorphism. Take
  $ B_0 \( e_i \, f_j \) = delta_(i j) \, quad B_0 \( f_i \, e_j \) = 0 \, #h(2em) B_s = Q \/ 2 \, #h(2em) A_0 = B_0 - B_s = 1 / 2 sum_i e^i and f^i \, $
  with other entries of $B_0$ zero. Here $e^i \, f^i$ are the covectors dual to $e_i \, f_i$.
  The fixed map $nu$ is exactly the nonsymmetric Clifford symbol:
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.9)")
   $ nu \( 1 ⊗ tau \) = psi_(B_0) phi . $ <p3sw:spin-eq:symbol>]
  To check every sign, let $I' = P \\ I$, and let $j = \| J \|$. Both sides on $e_P ⊗ e_J$ equal
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.10)")
   $ \( - 1 \)^(j \( j - 1 \) \/ 2) sum_(I subset.eq P) epsilon.alt_(I' \, I) epsilon.alt_(I \, J) \( - 1 \)^(sum_(i in I') i - \| I' \|) f_(\( I' \)^c) and e_(I union J) . $ <p3sw:spin-eq:symbol-basis>]
  For the left side, expand $mu^(*) e_P$ and apply Lemma 33. For the right side use
  $ c_(B_0) \( e_i \) = epsilon.alt_(e_i) + iota_(f^i) \, #h(2em) c_(B_0) \( f_i \) = epsilon.alt_(f_i) \, $
  and
  $ iota_(f^(i_1)) dots.h.c iota_(f^(i_s)) f_({ 1 \, dots.h \, ell_X }) = \( - 1 \)^(sum_(i in I') i - \| I' \|) f_(\( I' \)^c) . $
  Moving the remaining $e_I$ past the $f$’s changes $epsilon.alt_(I \, I')$ into $epsilon.alt_(I' \, I)$. This proves (6.9).
  For this finite exterior calculation define $iota_(alpha and beta) = iota_beta iota_alpha$ for covectors on $V$. This fixes the contraction convention locally; the Hochschild contraction used earlier was specified independently. The ordinary commutator identity
  $ \[ iota_(alpha and beta) \, epsilon.alt_v \] = alpha \( v \) iota_beta - beta \( v \) iota_alpha = iota_(\( alpha and beta \) \( v \, - \)) $
  and commutation of even contractions with contractions give
  $ c_(B_0) \( v \) = e^(iota_(A_0)) c_(B_s) \( v \) e^(- iota_(A_0)) \, #h(2em) psi_(B_0) = e^(iota_(A_0)) psi_(B_s) . $
  The second equality has no scalar, since $e^(- iota_(A_0)) 1 = 1$. All exponentials are finite. If $R = Lambda rho \( g \)$, preservation of $Q$ gives
  $ R c_(B_s) \( v \) R^(- 1) = c_(B_s) \( rho \( g \) v \) \, #h(2em) R psi_(B_s) \( x \) = psi_(B_s) \( g x g^(- 1) \) . $
  Thus the symmetric symbol is spin-equivariant.
  It remains to conjugate $iota_(A_0)$ by $D$. On the input $Lambda \( U xor L \)$ fix the orientation $Omega = f_1 dots.h.c f_(ell_X) e_1 dots.h.c e_(ell_X)$. The exterior determinant pairing of $Q$ defines $P D_Q$ by
  $ \( P D_Q eta \, zeta \)_Q = \[ eta and zeta \]_Omega . $
  For $\| P \| = k$, $\| J \| = j$, and $delta = k + j$, direct evaluation gives
  $ P D_Q \( f_J e_P \) = \( - 1 \)^(k j) epsilon.alt_(J \, J^c) epsilon.alt_(P \, P^c) e_(J^c) f_(P^c) . $
  Applying Lemma 33 to the two factors of $D$ therefore yields
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.11)")
   $ D \|_(Lambda^delta V) = \( - 1 \)^(thin g_X + delta \( delta + 3 \) \/ 2) P D_Q . $ <p3sw:spin-eq:duality>]
  For example, before parity reduction the exponent in the coefficient of $D \( f_J e_P \)$ is
  $ \( ell_X - j \) \( ell_X - j + 3 \) \/ 2 + k \( k + 3 \) \/ 2 + j . $
  Its difference from $g_X + delta \( delta + 3 \) \/ 2 + k j$ is even; this verifies the factor order as well as the shift.
  Raising a covector by $Q$ is denoted by a superscript $sharp$. The exterior derivation rule, applied twice, gives
  $ P D_Q \( iota_(A_0) eta \) = A_0^sharp and P D_Q \( eta \) . $
  The signs in (6.11) in degrees $delta - 2$ and $delta$ have ratio $- 1$. Moreover $Q$ raises $e^i$ to $f_i$ and $f^i$ to $e_i$, so
  $ A_0^sharp = 1 / 2 sum_i f_i and e_i = - p \/ 2 \, #h(2em) D iota_(A_0) D^(- 1) = epsilon.alt_(p \/ 2) . $
  Thus $D e^(iota_(A_0)) D^(- 1) = e^(p \/ 2)$. The map $P D_Q$, and hence $D$, commutes with $R$: $R$ preserves $Q$, degree, and orientation, since $det rho \( g \) = 1$. It follows that $F_s = D psi_(B_s) phi$ intertwines the diagonal spin action with $R$. Combining the displayed identities gives
  $ phi.alt' = D psi_(B_0) phi = e^(p \/ 2) F_s \, $
  which proves (6.7). Every map used is a rational algebraic map, so the equality is one of algebraic representations. Applying it to $1$ proves the two stated components. ◻
]

In particular the degree-two term $a \( g \) = 1 / 2 \( p - Lambda^2 rho \( g \) p \)$ satisfies

$ a \( g h \) = a \( g \) + Lambda^2 rho \( g \) a \( h \) . $

This follows either by direct substitution or by composing (6.7). It fixes the normalization of the character calculation below.

=== 6.3 The quadratic action and its actual polarization <the-quadratic-action-and-its-actual-polarization>

To identify the polarization preserved by the spinor stabilizers, recall the contraction map of the principal class:

$ theta.alt : U arrow.r L \, #h(2em) theta.alt \( y \) = iota_y Theta \, #h(2em) iota_y \( e and e' \) = y \( e \) e' - y \( e' \) e . $

Thus $y' \( theta.alt y \) = Theta \( y \, y' \)$ and $theta.alt^t = - theta.alt$. The principal polarization gives an isomorphism

$ lambda : X arrow.r hat(X) \, #h(2em) lambda \( z \) = t_z^(*) H ⊗ H^(- 1) . $

The isomorphism in the sheaf construction is $a \( z \) = t_(- z)^(*) H ⊗ H^(- 1) = lambda \( - z \)$. For the normalized Poincaré class $p = sum e_i and f_i$, the signs proved in (4.4) are

$ lambda^(*) : U arrow.r L med upright(" is ") theta.alt \, #h(2em) a^(*) = - theta.alt . $

Under the dual identifications on homology, $lambda_(*) = - theta.alt$ and $a_(*) = theta.alt$. No symmetry of $H$ is required.

The two exponential spinors $lambda_(+) \, lambda_(-)$ introduced in (6.4) are exchanged by scalar conjugation. Their span is the scalar extension of the rational plane generated by $\( lambda_(+) + lambda_(-) \) \/ 2$ and $\( lambda_(+) - lambda_(-) \) \/ \( 2 u \)$. Let $J$ be the pointwise algebraic spin stabilizer of this rational plane, and let $tilde(L)_K$ be the ordered stabilizer of the two individual spinor lines over $K$.

#thm([Proposition 6.3.])[
  (The polarized quadratic seed).  #emph[On the actual sixfold $M = X times hat(X)$, define 
$ eta \( u \) \( x \, N \) = #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] a^(- 1) \( N \) \, \[ - d \] a \( x \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] - lambda^(- 1) \( N \) \, \[ d \] lambda \( x \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $

 This gives a unital $K$-action. The ample line 
$ cal(L)_M = "pr"_X^(*) H^(⊗ d) ⊗ "pr"_(hat(X))^(*) \( a^(- 1) \)^(*) H $

 has a class $theta_M = c_(1 \, upright(B)) \( cal(L)_M \)$ fixed by the vector images of $J$ and $tilde(L)_K$. The action is of Weil type $\( 3 \, 3 \)$, is compatible with this polarization, and its actual homological Hermitian form is hyperbolic.]
  #emph[More precisely, the pullback $f = eta \( u \)^(*)$ on $V = L xor U$ and its two eigenspaces are 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.12)")
 $ f \( w \, y \) & = \( d theta.alt y \, - theta.alt^(- 1) w \) \, #h(2em) f^2 = - d \,\
W_(+) & = "Ann" \( lambda_(+) \) = { \( - u theta.alt y \, y \) : y in U_K } \,\
W_(-) & = "Ann" \( lambda_(-) \) = { \( u theta.alt y \, y \) : y in U_K } . $ <p3sw:spin-eq:oriented-action>]

 The eigenvalue on $W_(+)$ is $+ u$, and that on $W_(-)$ is $- u$.]
] <p3sw:polarized-seed>

