=== 6.4 The ordered stabilizer and its exterior invariants <the-ordered-stabilizer-and-its-exterior-invariants>

The next calculation identifies exactly which invariant part can carry a Weil class. It also distinguishes fixing the two spinor vectors from merely fixing their lines.

#thm([Proposition 6.4.])[
  (Ordered spinor stabilizer).  #emph[Let $W = W_(+)$. Under $V_K = W xor W^(*)$, the ordered line stabilizer and the pointwise stabilizer are 
$ tilde(L)_K = { \( g \, t \) in G L \( W \) times bb(G)_m : t^2 = det g } \, #h(2em) m \( g \, t \) = t^(- 1) Lambda g \, #h(2em) J_K = { \( g \, 1 \) : g in S L \( W \) } . $

 The characters on $lambda_(+)$ and $lambda_(-)$ are $t \, t^(- 1)$. For $0 lt.eq j lt.eq 6$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.17)")
 $ H^(2 j) \( M \, bb(Q) \)^J = cases(delim: "{", bb(Q) theta_M^j \, & j eq.not 3 \,, bb(Q) theta_M^3 xor cal(W)_K \( M \) \, & j = 3 \,) #h(2em) H^(upright("odd")) \( M \, bb(Q) \)^J = 0 . $ <p3sw:spin-eq:invariants>]

 The ordered line stabilizer fixes every $theta_M^j$ and acts on the two scalar Weil lines by $t^2 \, t^(- 2)$.]
] <p3sw:ordered-stabilizer>

#proof[
  The two annihilators in (6.12) are complementary maximal isotropic spaces, paired perfectly by $Q$. Identify the second with $W^(*)$. The Clifford algebra on $W xor W^(*)$ acts on $Lambda W$ by wedge and contraction. As in the matrix-unit argument above it is the full matrix algebra on this module. Hence this module is isomorphic to $S_K$ as a Clifford module. The common annihilator of all wedge operators from $W$ is the top line $K omega$, while the common annihilator of contractions from $W^(*)$ is the vacuum line $K 1$. The two original spinor lines therefore correspond to $K omega \, K 1$, in that order.
  An ordered stabilizer must preserve the two annihilators, so its vector action is $"diag" \( g \, g^(- T) \)$ for $g in G L \( W \)$. Conversely a lift of such a block action preserves both one-dimensional annihilator spaces. The operator $P_g = Lambda g$ on $Lambda W$ implements this block action by direct conjugation of wedge and contraction. It is in the even Clifford algebra because it preserves parity. For the top reversal pairing,
  $ chevron.l P_g s \, P_g t chevron.r = det \( g \) chevron.l s \, t chevron.r . $
  Thus its Clifford reversion norm is $tilde(P)_g P_g = det g$. Any other Clifford lift of the same vector action differs by a scalar: their quotient commutes with all of the full matrix algebra. A norm-one lift is therefore $a P_g$ with $a^2 det g = 1$. Writing $t = a^(- 1)$ proves the asserted algebraic cover and action. The top and vacuum characters are $t^(- 1) det g = t$ and $t^(- 1)$. Fixing both vectors is exactly $t = 1$, hence exactly $S L \( W \)$. In particular the extra covering element $\( 1 \, - 1 \)$, which negates both spinors, is not in $J_K$. This is a statement of algebraic groups; it does not assert that every $K$-rational determinant has a square root in $K$. The map $t mapsto \( "diag" \( t^2 \, 1 \, dots.h \, 1 \) \, t \)$ is an algebraic section of the character $t$; in particular $t^2 \, t^(- 2)$ are distinct nontrivial characters.
  Now
  $ Lambda^k \( V_K \) = xor.big_(a + b = k) Lambda^a W ⊗ Lambda^b W^(*) . $
  The invariant vectors in a summand are the intertwiners $"Hom"_(S L \( W \)) \( Lambda^b W \, Lambda^a W \)$. Here is the elementary calculation of those intertwiners. In a basis $w_1 \, dots.h \, w_6$, the diagonal torus $product t_i = 1$ acts on $w_I$ with weight $product_(i in I) t_i$. Two such weights, for $I \, J subset { 1 \, dots.h \, 6 }$, agree on this torus exactly when their indicator vectors differ by a constant multiple of $\( 1 \, dots.h \, 1 \)$. Thus either $I = J$, or $\( I \, J \) = \( { 1 \, dots.h \, 6 } \, diameter \)$, or the reverse. For $1 lt.eq a lt.eq 5$, any invariant subspace of $Lambda^a W$ is a sum of its one-dimensional torus weight spaces. The elementary unipotents $w_i mapsto w_i + c w_j$ connect every two $a$-subsets by successive replacements, so a nonzero invariant subspace contains all the weight spaces. This proves irreducibility. In an endomorphism commuting with the torus the coefficients on these weight spaces are diagonal, and commuting with the same unipotents makes them all equal. Consequently the only invariant blocks are
  $ a = b \, #h(2em) \( a \, b \) = \( 6 \, 0 \) \, \( 0 \, 6 \) \, $
  and each is one-dimensional. The balanced identity tensor is the degree-two class $sum_i w_i and w_i^(*)$; its $j$-th power is nonzero and spans the balanced block $a = b = j$. The already constructed nonzero invariant $theta_M$ is a scalar multiple of that class. The other two lines are $det W \, det W^(*)$, with ordered-group characters $det g = t^2$ and $\( det g \)^(- 1) = t^(- 2)$.
  Invariants commute with extension of scalars: they are the kernel of the coaction difference, and field extension is flat. The group $J$ and $theta_M$ are rational. The two determinant lines are exchanged by scalar conjugation and descend to the rational Weil plane. They occupy the exterior summands $\( a \, b \) = \( 6 \, 0 \) \, \( 0 \, 6 \)$ in $Lambda^a W ⊗ Lambda^b W^(*)$, whereas $theta_M^3$ occupies the $\( a \, b \) = \( 3 \, 3 \)$ summand. Thus the sum in degree six is direct. Descent gives (6.17), including the vanishing in odd degrees. ◻
]

=== 6.5 The normalized character has a nonzero Weil component <the-normalized-character-has-a-nonzero-weil-component>

For an even class $alpha = sum_(j = 0)^6 alpha_j$, with $alpha_j in H^(2 j) \( M \, bb(Q) \)$ and $alpha_0 eq.not 0$, define

$ N \( alpha \) = e^(- alpha_1 \/ alpha_0) alpha . $

If $b$ has degree two, then $N \( e^b alpha \) = N \( alpha \)$, because the degree-two term changes by $alpha_0 b$. If $R$ is a graded algebra automorphism, then $N \( R alpha \) = R N \( alpha \)$.

#thm([Proposition 6.5.])[
  (The exceptional component of the actual sheaf).  #emph[The normalized character $kappa_M$ in (6.1) is a rational $J$-invariant even class, and its degree-six component has a nonzero rational Weil component: 
$ \( kappa_M \)_3 = a_M theta_M^3 + w_M \, #h(2em) a_M in bb(Q) \, quad 0 eq.not w_M in cal(W)_K \( M \) . $

]
] <p3sw:exceptional-character>

#proof[
  We return to the source tensor $T = v ⊗ tau v$ in (6.5). Expanding (6.4) gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.18)")
   $ T =  & frac(d + 1, 4 d) \( lambda_(+) ⊗ lambda_(+) + lambda_(-) ⊗ lambda_(-) \)\
 & + frac(d - 1, 4 d) \( lambda_(+) ⊗ lambda_(-) + lambda_(-) ⊗ lambda_(+) \)\
 & + frac(u, 2 d) \( lambda_(-) ⊗ lambda_(+) - lambda_(+) ⊗ lambda_(-) \) . $ <p3sw:spin-eq:secant-tensor>]
  It is rational and fixed by $J$. It is not fixed by $tilde(L)_K$: the two square components have the distinct nontrivial characters $t^2 \, t^(- 2)$, with nonzero coefficient $\( d + 1 \) \/ \( 4 d \)$, while the mixed components have character one.
  Since $T$ is $J$-fixed, the identity $beta = phi.alt' \( T \)$ makes $beta$ $J$-fixed for $rho'$. Proposition 34 and the two normalization identities above show that $N \( beta \)$ is $J$-fixed for $Lambda rho$.
  Suppose its degree-six component had zero Weil projection. Proposition 36 would then put every component of $N \( beta \)$ in the power ring $K \[ theta_M \]$, which is fixed by $tilde(L)_K$. The class $e^(- p \/ 2) beta$ is $J$-fixed for $Lambda rho$, by (6.7). Its degree-two term
  $ b = beta_1 - r p \/ 2 $
  is therefore in $H^2 \( M \, bb(Q) \)^J = bb(Q) theta_M$, and is also fixed by $tilde(L)_K$. But
  $ e^(- p \/ 2) beta = e^(b \/ r) N \( beta \) . $
  Under the supposition the right side is fixed by $tilde(L)_K$. The exact Clifford identity would make $beta$ fixed by $rho'$ on that group, and the invertibility of $phi.alt'$ would make $T$ fixed by the diagonal spin action. This contradicts (6.18). Hence
  $ N \( beta \)_3 = a theta_M^3 + w \, #h(2em) a in bb(Q) \, quad 0 eq.not w in cal(W)_K \( M \) . $
  The coefficients are rational because the direct sum (6.17) is rational.
  The duality identity (6.6) gives $kappa_M = iota N \( beta \)$. The involution commutes with the graded vector action and preserves the rational decomposition in (6.17). In degree six it multiplies both terms by $- 1$, so the Weil component remains nonzero. Renaming the two rational terms proves the proposition. ◻
]

=== 6.6 The full normalized character is Hodge on the Weil domain <the-full-normalized-character-is-hodge-on-the-weil-domain>

We now relate the vector stabilizer to the actual polarized complex structures. This uses the polarization matrix itself, so no cohomological Hermitian trace-dual convention is needed.

#thm([Proposition 6.6.])[
  (The actual polarized Hodge circle).  #emph[Keep the rational homology of the seed $M$, the action $eta$, and the Riemann form of $cal(L)_M$ fixed. At every compatible marked complex structure of Weil type $\( 3 \, 3 \)$, every component $\( kappa_M \)_j$ has Hodge type $\( j \, j \)$.]
] <p3sw:hodge-circle>

#proof[
  Let $C$ be the coefficient matrix of $theta_M$ on cohomology. It is also the matrix of its alternating Riemann form on dual homology. Equation (6.14) gives
  $ C = - f Q^(- 1) . $
  For a cohomology automorphism $g$ commuting with the invertible $f$, this implies
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.19)")
   $ g C g^t = C & arrow.l.r.double - f \( g Q^(- 1) g^t \) = - f Q^(- 1)\
 & arrow.l.r.double g Q^(- 1) g^t = Q^(- 1) arrow.l.r.double g^t Q g = Q . $ <p3sw:spin-eq:centralizer>]
  Thus among $K$-linear cohomology operators, fixing the actual polarization bivector is exactly orthogonality for $Q$.
  Let $I$ be a compatible complex structure on homology and let $T = eta \( u \)_(*)$. Put $h_t = cos t + sin t thin I$, and let $g_t = h_t^t$ be its cohomology pullback. Since $I T = T I$ and $T^t = f$, one has $g_t f = f g_t$. Compatibility $e \( I x \, I y \) = e \( x \, y \)$ says $I^t C I = C$. Since $I^2 = - 1$, it also gives $I^t C + C I = 0$; expansion of $h_t^t C h_t$ then gives $h_t^t C h_t = C$. This is $g_t C g_t^t = C$. Equation (6.19) places $g_t$ in the centralizer of $f$ in $O \( Q \)$, and its path from the identity places it in $S O \( Q \)$.
  After the usual complex scalar extension of $W_(+)$, the two Hodge-circle weights have multiplicities three and three. Hence
  $ det \( g_t \|_(W_(+)) \) = e^(3 i t) e^(- 3 i t) = 1 . $
  Using the inverse circle convention only interchanges the factors. By Proposition 36, after extending to $K$ the determinant-one vector centralizer is $S L \( W_(+) \)$, and its unique lift fixing both spinors is $\( g \, 1 \) in J_K$. This identity of algebraic groups descends from $K$. The actual polarized Hodge circle is therefore in the vector image of $J$.
  On $H^(p \, q)$ the circle has weight $p - q$, up to simultaneous reversal. A degree-$2 j$ class fixed by it has only type $\( j \, j \)$. Proposition 37 makes every component of $kappa_M$ $J$-invariant, proving the assertion at every compatible marked period. ◻
]

Only a constant rational transport will be needed for the quotient $q : M arrow.r Y_0$. Transporting homology, the $K$-action, and the polarization by $q_(*)$ identifies the marked complex structures; under these identifications $q^(*)$ is a constant rational cohomology map and respects every corresponding Hodge decomposition. This observation concerns classes on the marked domain, and requires no relative isogeny or relative descent of the sheaf.

== 7 Descent and deformation <p3:sec:deformation>

We now pass from the equivariant sheaf on the Jacobian product to an ordinary sheaf whose full obstruction group can be used in deformation. The first step is coherent twisted descent through the finite quotient and a projective-bundle conversion. The second is the restricted smooth-projective-germ semiregularity theorem needed for that ordinary sheaf.

=== 7.1 Twisted descent and conversion to an ordinary sheaf <p3sw:descent-section>

The deformation argument will use an ordinary coherent sheaf on a projective family. We first construct a twisted sheaf on the quotient of the abelian sixfold and compare its full derived obstruction group with the invariant obstruction group on the covering sixfold. We then cancel the twist on a relative projective bundle. Throughout, the group action on an endomorphism complex is the conjugation action induced by the given line-twisted action; an action on the sheaf by ordinary automorphisms is not required.

==== 7.1.1 Characters and the duality sign <characters-and-the-duality-sign>

For a perfect complex $P$ on a smooth complex manifold $V$, write

$ a_P = - "At"_(upright("BF")) \( P \) in "Ext"_V^1 \( P \, P ⊗ Omega_V^1 \) . $

This is the raw first-jet Atiyah class with positive line-bundle Chern sign. The opposite sign is the convention of Buchweitz–Flenner [4] Definition 3.4 and Definition 4.1. Use the Atiyah-power and perfect-trace conventions of Proposition 25. Define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.1)")
 $ "ch"_(H \, k) \( P \) & = frac("tr" \( a_P^k \), k !) \, #h(2em) "ch"_H \( P \) = sum_k "ch"_(H \, k) \( P \) \,\
sigma_P \( v \) & = ("Tr"_(P \, Omega_V^j \[ j + 2 \]) ((frac(a_P^j, j !)) \[ 2 \] compose v))_(j gt.eq 0) . $ <p3sw:descent-character>]

Here $"tr"$ is the perfect trace, and

$ sigma_P : "Ext"_V^2 \( P \, P \) arrow.r cal(H)_(- 2) \( V \) := xor.big_(j gt.eq 0) H^(j + 2) \( V \, Omega_V^j \) . $

The summands with $j > dim V - 2$ vanish. These definitions also apply to a perfect weight-one complex on a finite-band gerbe: its derived endomorphisms and their trace have weight zero and hence descend to the underlying manifold.

On a smooth projective $V$, let $iota_V : H^q \( V \, Omega_V^p \) arrow.r.hook H^(p + q) \( V \, bb(C) \)$ be the Hodge inclusion for the ordinary de Rham differential $d$. Our normalized Betti convention is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.2)")
 $ "ch"_(B \, k) \( P \) = \( 2 pi i \)^(- k) iota_V #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] "ch"_(H \, k) \( P \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, #h(2em) "ch"_B \( P \) = sum_k "ch"_(B \, k) \( P \) . $ <p3sw:descent-tate>]

For an ordinary perfect complex this is its rational topological Chern character. For a twisted complex the expression initially defines a complex Betti class; rationality in the present construction will follow from finite descent. The inclusion $iota_V$ is multiplicative and commutes with pullback. On the semiregularity summand $H^(j + 2) \( Omega_V^j \)$, the corresponding normalization is $\( 2 pi i \)^(- j) iota_V$: its weight is the form degree $j$, not half the total cohomological degree. Thus, if $iota_V \( gamma_H \) = \( 2 pi i \) gamma_B$ for $gamma_H in H^1 \( Omega_V^1 \)$, multiplication by $gamma_H$ on the raw targets corresponds weightwise to multiplication by $gamma_B$. All identities involving $sigma$ will remain in raw Hodge cohomology.

Return to the fixed perfect complex $cal(G)$ on $M = X times hat(X)$. Write

$ D \( cal(G) \) = R cal(H) o m_M \( cal(G) \, cal(O)_M \) \, #h(2em) E = D \( cal(G) \) \[ - 1 \] = cal(E) . $

By Proposition 23, $E$ is a coherent perfect sheaf of rank $r = 8 d > 0$. Proposition 24 supplies the coherent line-twisted actions on $cal(G)$ and $E$, both indexed by $G_(upright("src"))$ and identified with the translation subgroup $macron(G)$ through $g mapsto b_g$. Duality inverts the line factors and preserves the translations and coherent composition law. Proposition 32 proves that $sigma_(cal(G))$ is injective on the full group $"Ext"_M^2 \( cal(G) \, cal(G) \)^(macron(G))$. The next calculation transfers this injection to $E$.

#thm([Lemma 7.1.])[
  #emph[Even transpose induces an isomorphism 
$ d_2 : "Ext"_M^2 \( cal(G) \, cal(G) \)^(macron(G)) arrow.r^(med tilde.op med) "Ext"_M^2 \( E \, E \)^(macron(G)) . $

 With the convention (7.1), its components obey 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.3)")
 $ sigma_(E \, j) \( d_2 v \) = \( - 1 \)^(j + 1) sigma_(cal(G) \, j) \( v \) quad upright("for every ") j gt.eq 0 . $ <p3sw:descent-dual-sign>]

 Consequently, injectivity of $sigma_(cal(G))$ on the full $macron(G)$-invariant $"Ext"^2$ implies injectivity of $sigma_E$ on the full $macron(G)$-invariant $"Ext"^2$.]
] <p3sw:descent-duality>

#proof[
  Perfect duality is an anti-equivalence and turns the given line-twisted action into its inverse-line action. The quasi-isomorphism from $D \( cal(G) \) \[ - 1 \]$ to the degree-zero sheaf $E$ is canonical in the derived category and is equivariant. Transpose therefore gives the asserted invariant isomorphism in even degree.
  The sign can be computed in a finite complex of free modules. The dual Atiyah class is the negative transpose of the original class. Transposing a product reverses both its Ext-degree-one factors and its one-form factors. The two reversal Koszul signs cancel: equivalently, an $Omega^1 \[ 1 \]$-valued Atiyah operation has even combined parity. The Ext-degree-two operation $v$ is also even, so its movement past these factors gives no further sign. Supertrace is invariant under transpose, while the shift $\[ - 1 \]$ changes supertrace by $- 1$. The $j$ negative dual Atiyah factors and this last sign give (7.3). Evaluation, transpose, and the Atiyah construction respect changes of finite free model, so the local calculation glues. The diagonal operator with entries $\( - 1 \)^(j + 1)$ is invertible, which proves the final assertion. ◻
]

==== 7.1.2 Determinant normalization and derived descent <determinant-normalization-and-derived-descent>

Let

$ q : M arrow.r Y_0 := M \/ macron(G) $

be the finite etale translation isogeny of Proposition 24. We write the action on $E$ using pullback translations. For $h = - b_g$, set $L_h = N_g^(- 1)$. Since $t_(b_g \, *) = t_(- b_g)^(*) = t_h^(*)$, the action (4.49) becomes

$ L_h ⊗ t_h^(*) E tilde.eq E #h(2em) \( h in macron(G) \) . $

These isomorphisms retain the unital associative line compositors of that proposition. Reindexing by inversion on $macron(G)$ preserves the invariant subspace of the derived endomorphisms.

#thm([Proposition 7.2.])[
  #emph[There are a class $alpha in H^2 \( Y_0 \, mu_r \)$, a coherent $alpha$-twisted sheaf $cal(B)$ of rank $r$ with $det cal(B) tilde.eq cal(O)_(Y_0)$, and an invertible $q^(*) alpha$-twisted sheaf $cal(L)$ on $M$, such that 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.4)")
 $ q^(*) cal(B) tilde.eq E ⊗ cal(L) . $ <p3sw:descent-twisted-pullback>]

 Let $frak(Y)_0$ be the $mu_r$-gerbe of $alpha$. For every integer $i$, this isomorphism induces 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.5)")
 $ "Ext"_(frak(Y)_0)^i \( cal(B) \, cal(B) \) tilde.eq "Ext"_M^i \( E \, E \)^(macron(G)) \, #h(2em) "Ext"_0^i \( cal(B) \, cal(B) \) tilde.eq "Ext"_0^i \( E \, E \)^(macron(G)) \, $ <p3sw:descent-ext>]

 where the subscript $0$ denotes the kernel of the perfect trace to $H^i \( cal(O) \)$. Put 
$ lambda_H = a_(det E) / r = frac("tr" \( a_E \), r) . $

 Under the first isomorphism $phi.alt$ in degree two, the raw Hodge square 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.6)")
 $ "Ext"^2 \( cal(B) \, cal(B) \) & arrow.r^(med phi.alt med) & "Ext"^2 \( E \, E \)^(macron(G))\
arrow.b sigma_(cal(B)) &  & arrow.b e^(- lambda_H) sigma_E\
cal(H)_(- 2) \( Y_0 \) & arrow.r^(med q^(*) med) & cal(H)_(- 2) \( M \) $ <p3sw:descent-square>]

 commutes, both on full Ext and on its trace-free summand. In particular, full invariant injectivity for $E$ makes $cal(B)$ semiregular. By Lemma 39, full invariant injectivity for $cal(G)$ is sufficient for this conclusion.]
] <p3sw:twisted-descent>

#proof[
  We construct the twisted sheaf before comparing its derived endomorphisms. Choose an evenly covered good analytic cover of $Y_0$, refined so that the finitely many transition lines are trivial on the required sheet intersections. Take every sheet over every cover member, so the sheets $U_i$ cover $M$, and retain its image $V_i$ as a labelled member of the cover. Transport $E \|_(U_i)$ to a sheaf $B_i$ on $V_i$. On $V_i ∩ V_j$ for $i < j$, the deck transformation comparing the two sheets and a local trivialization of its line $L_h$ give an isomorphism $rho_(i j) : B_j arrow.r B_i$. Use the unit and inverse composition data to impose $rho_(i i) = "id"$ and $rho_(j i) = rho_(i j)^(- 1)$. On a triple intersection, the coherent composition law cancels the translations; the remaining line trivializations differ by an invertible scalar function. Thus $rho_(i j) rho_(j k) rho_(k i) = c_(i j k) "id"$.
  We use the determinant functor for perfect complexes in its elementary local form: a finite free complex has the alternating tensor product of the determinants of its terms. It commutes with restriction and isomorphism, and the determinant of $c "id"$ on a rank-$r$ perfect complex is $c^r$. Each $B_i$ is perfect, so $det B_i$ is a line. Trivialize these lines and let $eta_(i j)$ be the determinant of $rho_(i j)$; then $eta_(i i) = 1$ and $eta_(j i) = eta_(i j)^(- 1)$. After a further good-cover refinement, choose an $r$-th root $tilde(eta)_(i j)$ of this nowhere-zero holomorphic function for $i < j$, and impose $tilde(eta)_(i i) = 1$ and $tilde(eta)_(j i) = tilde(eta)_(i j)^(- 1)$. Set $rho'_(i j) = tilde(eta)_(i j)^(- 1) rho_(i j)$. These maps have determinant one and satisfy $rho'_(i i) = "id"$ and $rho'_(j i) = \( rho'_(i j) \)^(- 1)$. The coherent composition law gives an invertible holomorphic scalar $epsilon.alt_(i j k)$ defined by
  $ rho'_(i j) rho'_(j k) = epsilon.alt_(i j k) rho'_(i k) . $
  Taking determinants gives $epsilon.alt_(i j k)^r = 1$, so these scalars are locally constant with values in $mu_r$. Associativity on a fourfold intersection gives the exact Cech cocycle identity
  $ epsilon.alt_(i j k) epsilon.alt_(i k l) = epsilon.alt_(j k l) epsilon.alt_(i j l) . $
  The repeated-index multipliers are one; scalar multiplication is faithful because $B_i$ has positive rank on a smooth manifold. The resulting $mu_r$-cocycle defines $alpha$, and the sheaves $B_i$ with transitions $rho'_(i j)$ define $cal(B)$. Their determinant trivializations glue, so $det cal(B) tilde.eq cal(O)_(Y_0)$. Rank, coherence, and perfection are local, and hence pass from $E$ to $cal(B)$. This is the determinant-normalized form of the local construction in [25] Lemma 9.3.6 and equation (9.3.3); it uses the perfect determinant, not an exterior power of a locally free sheaf.
  Let $theta_i : \( q^(*) B_i \) \|_(U_i) arrow.r^tilde.op E \|_(U_i)$ be the local identification from transport. On an actual overlap $U_i ∩ U_j$, the comparing deck transformation is the identity, so the coherent line action gives an invertible scalar $l_(i j)$ with
  $ theta_i \( q^(*) rho'_(i j) \) theta_j^(- 1) = l_(i j) "id"_E . $
  The normalized transitions give $l_(i i) = 1$, $l_(j i) = l_(i j)^(- 1)$, and $l_(i j) l_(j k) = \( q^(*) epsilon.alt_(i j k) \) l_(i k)$. Use $l_(i j)$ as the transition from the $j$-th trivial line to the $i$-th. These lines define the weight-one $q^(*) alpha$-twisted line $cal(L)$ with the same multiplier as $q^(*) cal(B)$, and the local identifications give (7.4). On translated intersections, conjugation by these identifications is the original conjugation action on the derived endomorphisms of $E$.
  For clarity, a twisted sheaf here is a coherent weight-one sheaf on $frak(Y)_0$, equivalently the just described Cech data. Weight-zero sheaves and complexes descend uniquely to $Y_0$. A coherent twisted sheaf on the smooth manifold is absolutely perfect: in a local trivialization of the gerbe it is a finite module over a regular analytic local ring and has a finite free resolution. Write
  $ cal(C)_(cal(B)) = R cal(H) o m_(frak(Y)_0) \( cal(B) \, cal(B) \) $
  for its weight-zero descent. It is a perfect complex on $Y_0$, locally $cal(B)^or ⊗^L cal(B)$.
  We use the following derived base-change identity. For an absolutely perfect $P$, any complex $Q'$, and any morphism $f : T arrow.r V$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.7)")
   $ L f^(*) R cal(H) o m_V \( P \, Q' \) tilde.eq R cal(H) o m_T \( L f^(*) P \, L f^(*) Q' \) . $ <p3sw:descent-rhom-basechange>]
  Indeed, locally the left side is $L f^(*) \( P^or ⊗^L Q' \)$; derived pullback is symmetric monoidal and carries the dual of a perfect complex to the dual of its pullback. This also proves the identity on gerbe trivializations. The unit and trace, being coevaluation and evaluation, commute with the same derived pullbacks. This is an assertion about derived pullback; ordinary pullback under a nonflat map is not being substituted.
  Apply (7.7) to the etale map $q$, where ordinary and derived pullback agree. Line cancellation in (7.4) gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.8)")
   $ q^(*) cal(C)_(cal(B)) tilde.eq R cal(H) o m \( q^(*) cal(B) \, q^(*) cal(B) \) tilde.eq R cal(H) o m_M \( E \, E \) . $ <p3sw:descent-rhom>]
  Both the line factors and their scalar gluing cancel. The descent datum on the right is consequently the honest conjugation action of $macron(G)$, although the action on $E$ itself is line-twisted.
  For a finite covering, $q_(*)$ on sheaves is exact: over an evenly covered open set it is the direct sum of the finitely many sheet restrictions. Etale descent gives $cal(C)_(cal(B)) tilde.eq \( q_(*) q^(*) cal(C)_(cal(B)) \)^(macron(G))$. Over $bb(C)$, invariants are the split summand defined by $\| macron(G) \|^(- 1) sum_(h in macron(G)) h$. Applying derived global sections to that summand and using exactness of $q_(*)$ gives
  $ R Gamma \( Y_0 \, cal(C)_(cal(B)) \) tilde.eq R Gamma \( M \, q^(*) cal(C)_(cal(B)) \)^(macron(G)) . $
  Taking cohomology and using (7.8) proves the full Ext comparison in (7.5).
  The perfect unit and trace are equivariant maps
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.9)")
   $ cal(O)_(Y_0) arrow.r^u cal(C)_(cal(B)) arrow.r^("tr") cal(O)_(Y_0) \, #h(2em) "tr" u = r "id" . $ <p3sw:descent-trace>]
  The last equality is the alternating-rank calculation in a finite free model. Thus $u \/ r$ splits the trace. If $cal(C)_(cal(B) \, 0) = "fib" \( "tr" \)$, then
  $ cal(C)_(cal(B)) tilde.eq cal(O)_(Y_0) xor cal(C)_(cal(B) \, 0) \, #h(2em) H^i R Gamma \( Y_0 \, cal(C)_(cal(B) \, 0) \) = ker #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] "Ext"^i \( cal(B) \, cal(B) \) arrow.r H^i \( cal(O)_(Y_0) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  The splitting commutes with (7.8), since derived pullback and tensoring by an invertible line preserve evaluation and coevaluation. Descent of this summand proves the trace-free comparison. In particular, the calculation retains the higher sheaf-Ext terms: it does not replace $H^2 R Gamma \( cal(C)_(cal(B) \, 0) \)$ by $H^2 \( cal(E) n d_0 \( cal(B) \) \)$.
  It remains to compare the semiregularity maps. The cotangent target on a $mu_r$-gerbe is that of its underlying manifold. Equivalently, the scalar triple-gluing functions are locally constant, so their differentials vanish in first jets. The pullback and tensor rules for Atiyah classes, with the differential $q^(*) Omega_(Y_0)^1 tilde.eq Omega_M^1$, give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.10)")
   $ q^(*) a_(cal(B)) = a_E + a_(cal(L)) "id"_E \, #h(2em) a_(cal(L)) = - a_(det E) / r = - lambda_H . $ <p3sw:descent-atiyah>]
  The second equality follows by tracing the first and using $det cal(B) = cal(O)_(Y_0)$; the trace of $a_E$ is the Atiyah class of its perfect determinant. The pullback compatibility is also [4] Proposition 3.14; the tensor rule follows from the Leibniz rule for first jets on a local perfect model. The scalar $- lambda_H$ commutes with the Atiyah operations in the combined Ext/form grading. Expanding the exponential and applying the natural perfect trace yields
  $ q^(*) sigma_(cal(B)) \( v \) = e^(- lambda_H) sigma_E \( phi.alt v \) \, $
  which is (7.6). Multiplication by $lambda_H in H^1 \( Omega_M^1 \)$ raises both Hodge indices and is nilpotent on $cal(H)_(- 2) \( M \)$; $e^(- lambda_H)$ therefore has inverse $e^(lambda_H)$. Full invariant injectivity on the right implies full injectivity on the left. The trace-free comparison also shows that injectivity restricted to the invariant trace-free group would suffice: the $j = 0$ component of $sigma_(cal(B))$ is the perfect trace itself, so its vanishing first puts a class into the trace-free summand. ◻
]

The normalized Betti counterpart of $lambda_H$ is

$ lambda_B = frac(c_1^B \( det E \), r) \, #h(2em) iota_M \( lambda_H \) = \( 2 pi i \) lambda_B \, $

where $c_1^B$ is the usual integral Betti Chern class. Applying (7.10) to the character and then using (7.2) weight by weight gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.11)")
 $ q^(*) "ch"_H \( cal(B) \) & = e^(- lambda_H) "ch"_H \( E \) \,\
q^(*) "ch"_B \( cal(B) \) & = e^(- lambda_B) "ch"_B \( E \) . $ <p3sw:descent-character-pullback>]

The right side of the second equality is rational. The Betti trace $q_(*)^B$ satisfies $q_(*)^B q^(*) = \( deg q \) "id"$, so $\( deg q \)^(- 1) q_(*)^B$ proves that $"ch"_B \( cal(B) \)$ is rational as well. No integrality of $lambda_B$ is required.

==== 7.1.3 A relative projective bundle carrying an ordinary sheaf <a-relative-projective-bundle-carrying-an-ordinary-sheaf>

Let $a : Y arrow.r S$ be a holomorphic abelian scheme of relative dimension six over a sufficiently small contractible representative of a smooth analytic germ, with central fiber $Y_0$. Assume that the family is polarized. Locally it admits a line $cal(L)_Y$ representing the polarization and ample relative to $a$. More precisely, if only the polarization homomorphism was specified, the standard rigidified relative Picard component of representing lines is a torsor under the smooth dual abelian scheme; a local analytic section through a chosen central representative supplies $cal(L)_Y$. Relative ampleness holds after shrinking. This is the only local choice of a representing polarization line that will be used.

We realize the twisting class by a finite projective representation and extend its projective-space bundle over $Y$. On the central fiber, the inverse-twisted tautological line cancels the twist of the pulled-back sheaf $cal(B)$. The vanishing of higher cohomology of the structure sheaf on projective space retains the full obstruction group in this conversion.

#thm([Proposition 7.3.])[
  #emph[For the class $alpha$ and the semiregular twisted coherent sheaf $cal(B)$ in Proposition 40, put $m = r^12$ and $N = 6 + m - 1$. There exist a holomorphic $bb(P)^(m - 1)$-bundle $p : Z arrow.r Y$, a determinant-trivial $alpha$-twisted vector bundle $Q_0$ of rank $m$ on $Y_0$, and an inverse-twisted tautological line $ell_0 = cal(O)_(bb(P) \( Q_0 \)) \( 1 \)$, with $Z_0 = bb(P) \( Q_0 \)$. The ordinary line 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.12)")
 $ H = omega_(Z \/ Y)^(- 1) = det T_(Z \/ Y) $ <p3sw:descent-anticanonical>]

 satisfies $H \|_(Z_0) tilde.eq ell_0^m$ and restricts to $cal(O)_(bb(P)^(m - 1)) \( m \)$ on every $p$-fiber. The normalized Betti class and raw Hodge class 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.13)")
 $ zeta_(B \, s) = frac(c_1^B \( H \|_(Z_s) \), m) \, #h(2em) zeta_(H \, s) = a_(H \|_(Z_s)) / m \, #h(2em) iota_(Z_s) \( zeta_(H \, s) \) = \( 2 pi i \) zeta_(B \, s) $ <p3sw:descent-zeta>]

 have the following properties: $zeta_B$ is a horizontal rational class of type $\( 1 \, 1 \)$, and its restriction to every $p_s$-fiber is the normalized hyperplane class. Every $Z_s$ and $p_s$ is projective algebraic, and $a compose p$ has a relative ample line.]
  #emph[The sheaf 
$ F_0 = p_0^(*) cal(B) ⊗ ell_0 $

 is ordinary coherent on $Z_0$, and it is semiregular. More precisely, there is a full derived isomorphism 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.14)")
 $ "Ext"_(Z_0)^2 \( F_0 \, F_0 \) tilde.eq "Ext"_(frak(Y)_0)^2 \( cal(B) \, cal(B) \) $ <p3sw:descent-ordinary-ext>]

 under which 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.15)")
 $ sigma_(F_0) \( p_0^(*) v \) = e^(zeta_(H \, 0)) p_0^(*) sigma_(cal(B)) \( v \) . $ <p3sw:descent-ordinary-sigma>]

 If a rational horizontal section $kappa_B = sum_(k = 0)^6 kappa_(B \, k)$ specializes to $"ch"_B \( cal(B) \)$ and has type $\( k \, k \)$ in degree $2 k$, then 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.16)")
 $ A_B = p^(*) kappa_B e^(zeta_B) = sum_(k = 0)^N A_(B \, k) $ <p3sw:descent-horizontal-character>]

 is rational, horizontal, and of type $\( k \, k \)$ in degree $2 k$. Its raw Hodge section in that degree is the edge of $\( 2 pi i \)^k A_(B \, k)$, and its central value is $"ch"_(H \, k) \( F_0 \)$. Finally, on every fiber, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.17)")
 $ p_(s *)^B #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] zeta_(B \, s)^(thin m - 1) e^(- zeta_(B \, s)) A_B \( s \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = kappa_B \( s \) \, $ <p3sw:descent-extraction>]

 where $p_(s *)^B$ is normalized Betti fiber integration.]
] <p3sw:ordinary-conversion>

#proof[
  We first realize the exact finite-band class $alpha$. Set $Lambda = H_1 \( Y_0 \, bb(Z) \) tilde.eq bb(Z)^12$. The universal coefficient theorem for the torus gives
  $ H^2 \( Y_0 \, mu_r \) = "Hom" \( and.big^2 Lambda \, mu_r \) \; $
  there is no Ext term because $H_1 \( Y_0 \, bb(Z) \)$ is free. Let $b$ be the alternating pairing corresponding to $alpha$, and put $G_r = Lambda \/ r Lambda$. For an ordered basis $e_1 \, dots.h \, e_12$ of $Lambda$, define
  $ c \( x \, y \) = product_(i < j) b \( e_i \, e_j \)^(x_i y_j) quad \( x \, y in G_r \) . $
  This is a well-defined bimultiplicative cocycle with $c \( x \, y \) c \( y \, x \)^(- 1) = b \( x \, y \)$. On the $m = \| G_r \| = r^12$ dimensional vector space with basis ${ v_y : y in G_r }$, set
  $ T_x v_y = c \( x \, y \) v_(x + y) . $
  Then $T_x T_y = c \( x \, y \) T_(x + y)$, so $x mapsto \[ T_x \]$ is a projective representation. For $r = 1$ this is the trivial one-dimensional representation.
  The multiplication map $\[ r \] : Y arrow.r Y$ is a finite etale torsor under $Y \[ r \]$. Its differential on the relative tangent bundle is multiplication by $r$; it is proper, and every abelian fiber has $r^12$ inverse images of a point. Thus it is a proper local biholomorphism of that degree. Contractibility of $S$ trivializes the finite local system $Y \[ r \]$ as $G_r$, with its central identification. Form $Z$ as the quotient of $Y times bb(P)^(m - 1)$ by the free diagonal action covering translation by $G_r$, taking the inverse action on the projective coordinate. If local sections of the torsor satisfy $s_j = s_i + x_(i j)$, this convention gives $\[ s_j \, z \] = \[ s_i \, \[ T_(x_(i j)) \] z \]$. Local sections of the finite covering show that $p : Z arrow.r Y$ is a holomorphic projective-space bundle with these projective transitions.
  We must check the class in $H^2 \( Y_0 \, mu_r \)$, not only its image in the analytic Brauer group. Rescale each $T_x$ to have determinant one. The multiplier of these special-linear lifts is $mu_m$-valued: it is scalar with determinant one. It differs from $c$ by a scalar coboundary and still has commutator $b$. The monodromy of the central $\[ r \]$-torsor is $Lambda arrow.r Lambda \/ r Lambda$. For any constant coefficient group $C'$,
  $ H^2 \( Y_0 \, C' \) = "Hom" \( and.big^2 Lambda \, C' \) . $
  In the bar complex the cycle $\[ e_i \| e_j \] - \[ e_j \| e_i \]$ represents $e_i and e_j$, and an inflated multiplier evaluates on it as its alternating commutator. Thus the special-linear obstruction class has pairing $b$. The inclusion $mu_m arrow.r.hook bb(Q) \/ bb(Z)$ induces an injection on this $H^2$, so that class is the image of $alpha$ under $mu_r arrow.r.hook mu_m$. On a common good cover, its $mu_m$-cocycle therefore differs from a chosen $mu_r$-cocycle for $alpha$ by the coboundary of a $mu_m$-valued one-cochain. Multiplying the special-linear lifts by that cochain gives transitions with exactly the chosen $mu_r$-multiplier and still determinant one. They define $Q_0$, and their projectivizations give $Z_0$.
  Use the convention that $bb(P) \( Q_0 \)$ parametrizes lines in $Q_0$. The tautological $ell_0 = cal(O) \( 1 \)$ then has the inverse twist. The twisted factors cancel in the Euler sequence
  $ 0 arrow.r cal(O)_(Z_0) arrow.r p_0^(*) Q_0 ⊗ ell_0 arrow.r T_(Z_0 \/ Y_0) arrow.r 0 . $
  Since $det Q_0 = cal(O)_(Y_0)$, its determinant is $H \|_(Z_0) = ell_0^m$. On any projective-space fiber the same anticanonical line is $cal(O) \( m \)$.
  On each $s$, the quotient construction is the algebraic quotient of the projective variety $Y_s times bb(P)^(m - 1)$ by a free finite algebraic action. It is projective: tensor the translates of an ample line on the product, use the permutation linearization on that tensor product, descend it along the free finite quotient, and use descent of ampleness under a finite surjection. The map $p_s$ is algebraic and projective, for instance by its closed graph in a projective product. The global line $H$ is $p$-ample. The composition rule for relative ampleness gives positive $u \, v$ for which $H^u ⊗ p^(*) cal(L)_Y^v$ is ample for $a compose p$. The class $zeta_(B \, s)$ is the restriction of the global class $c_1^B \( H \) \/ m$. Restrictions of a total class along a proper smooth submersion are horizontal, and a holomorphic line gives type $\( 1 \, 1 \)$. Its fiber degree is one by the calculation of $H$. The raw equality in (7.13) is the degree-one comparison (7.2); centrally it gives $a_(ell_0) = zeta_(H \, 0)$.
  We now verify ordinary semiregularity. The twists of $p_0^(*) cal(B)$ and $ell_0$ cancel, so $F_0$ is ordinary coherent. It need not be locally free. Since $p_0$ is flat, the derived identity (7.7) and line cancellation give
  $ R cal(H) o m_(Z_0) \( F_0 \, F_0 \) tilde.eq p_0^(*) cal(C)_(cal(B)) . $
  Moreover $R p_(0 *) cal(O)_(Z_0) = cal(O)_(Y_0)$. To check this equality, pull back along the finite etale cover $\[ r \] : Y_0 arrow.r Y_0$; the bundle becomes $Y_0 times bb(P)^(m - 1)$, where the assertion is the vanishing of the higher cohomology of $cal(O)_(bb(P)^(m - 1))$. Flat base change for this fixed projective fiber and faithful flatness give the equality on $Y_0$. The perfect projection formula now yields
  $ "Ext"_(Z_0)^2 \( F_0 \, F_0 \) & tilde.eq H^2 R Gamma \( Z_0 \, p_0^(*) cal(C)_(cal(B)) \)\
 & tilde.eq H^2 R Gamma \( Y_0 \, cal(C)_(cal(B)) \) = "Ext"_(frak(Y)_0)^2 \( cal(B) \, cal(B) \) . $
  This is (7.14); it uses the full derived Hom complex. Pullback and line tensoring preserve evaluation and coevaluation, so this identification commutes with the unit and perfect trace under $p_0^(*) : H^2 \( Y_0 \, cal(O)_(Y_0) \) arrow.r^tilde.op H^2 \( Z_0 \, cal(O)_(Z_0) \)$ and identifies their trace-free kernels. The tensor rule and $a_(ell_0) = zeta_(H \, 0)$ give (7.15).
  The raw Hodge pullback in that equation is injective. Indeed, for $eta_H in H^(j + 2) \( Y_0 \, Omega_(Y_0)^j \)$, put $eta_B = \( 2 pi i \)^(- j) iota_(Y_0) \( eta_H \)$, a complex Betti class. Comparison commutes with pullback. With $f = m - 1$, normalized Betti integration gives
  $ p_(0 *)^B #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] zeta_(B \, 0)^(thin f) p_0^(*) eta_B #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = eta_B \, #h(2em) integral_(bb(P)^f) c_1^B \( cal(O) \( 1 \) \)^f = 1 . $
  Thus $p_0^(*) eta_H = 0$ implies $eta_B = 0$, and then $eta_H = 0$. This avoids any choice of normalization for Hodge Gysin. The exponential $e^(zeta_(H \, 0))$ is invertible on the finite direct sum $cal(H)_(- 2) \( Z_0 \)$. Equation (7.15) and semiregularity of $cal(B)$ prove full semiregularity of $F_0$.
  Finally, pullback is a morphism of cohomology local systems and $zeta_B$ is horizontal, so (7.16) is horizontal. Each degree-$2 k$ part is rational of type $\( k \, k \)$. Its flat lift $\( 2 pi i \)^k A_(B \, k)$ lies in $F^k$, and its edge in $R^k \( a compose p \)_(*) Omega_(Z \/ S)^k$ is the raw Hodge class to be used in deformation theory. Multiplicativity of the comparison and the one Tate factor contributed by each $zeta_H$ give, centrally,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.18)")
   $ A_H \( 0 \) & = p_0^(*) "ch"_H \( cal(B) \) e^(zeta_(H \, 0)) = "ch"_H \( F_0 \) \,\
A_B \( 0 \) & = p_0^(*) "ch"_B \( cal(B) \) e^(zeta_(B \, 0)) = "ch"_B \( F_0 \) . $ <p3sw:descent-central-character>]
  Here $A_(H \, k)$ denotes the edge of $\( 2 pi i \)^k A_(B \, k)$, and $A_H = sum_k A_(H \, k)$. The normalized projection formula and the hyperplane degree give
  $ p_(s *)^B #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] zeta_(B \, s)^(thin m - 1) e^(- zeta_(B \, s)) A_B \( s \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] & = p_(s *)^B #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] zeta_(B \, s)^(thin m - 1) p_s^(*) kappa_B \( s \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]\
 & = kappa_B \( s \) thin p_(s *)^B \( zeta_(B \, s)^(thin m - 1) \) = kappa_B \( s \) . $
  All exponentials are finite polynomials in cohomology. This proves (7.17) and the proposition. ◻
]

=== 7.2 Deformation over a smooth projective analytic germ <p3sw:bf-section>

The ordinary coherent-sheaf deformation statement below is the projective case of the argument in Buchweitz–Flenner [4] Theorem 5.1 and its proof, pp. 174–179, whose proof constructs a flat analytic sheaf with the prescribed central fiber. We reconstruct that argument for the projective bundle in Proposition 41, keeping the full coefficient obstruction group explicit. Projectivity permits a comparison of the Chern characters of all infinitesimal lifts and an effectivity argument through compatible quotients.

#thm([Theorem 7.4.])[
  #emph[Let $S = \( S \, 0 \)$ be a smooth complex analytic germ, and let $pi : cal(X) arrow.r S$ be a proper holomorphic submersion of relative dimension $n$, equipped with a holomorphic $pi$-ample line $cal(L)_(cal(X))$. Let $F_0$ be an ordinary coherent sheaf on $X_0$. For $I subset.eq { 1 \, dots.h \, n }$, suppose the following raw semiregularity map is injective: 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.19)")
 $ sigma_(I \, 0) : "Ext"_(X_0)^2 \( F_0 \, F_0 \) & arrow.r xor.big_(k in I) H^(k + 1) \( X_0 \, Omega_(X_0)^(k - 1) \) \,\
v & mapsto ("Tr"_(F_0 \, Omega_(X_0)^(k - 1) \[ k + 1 \]) ((frac(a_(F_0)^(k - 1), \( k - 1 \) !)) \[ 2 \] compose v))_(k in I) $ <p3sw:bf-seed-sigma>]

 Suppose there are rational flat sections $beta_(B \, k)$ of $R^(2 k) pi_(*) bb(Q)$, for $k in I$, such that 
$ beta_(B \, k) \( 0 \) = "ch"_(B \, k) \( F_0 \) \, #h(2em) beta_(B \, k) in F^k \( R^(2 k) pi_(*) bb(C) ⊗ cal(O)_S \) . $

 The second condition means that the flat de Rham class itself lies in the indicated Hodge filtration. Then, after shrinking $S$, there is a coherent $S$-flat sheaf $F$ on $cal(X)$, with $F \|_(X_0) tilde.eq F_0$, for which 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.20)")
 $ "ch"_(B \, k) \( F \|_(X_s) \) = beta_(B \, k) \( s \) quad \( s upright(" near ") 0 \, med k in I \) . $ <p3sw:bf-fiber-character>]

 These classes are rational algebraic cycle classes. No rank, simplicity, stability, local-freeness, reflexivity, or fixed-support hypothesis is imposed on $F_0$.]
] <p3sw:projective-germ>

We use the raw convention $a = - "At"_(upright("BF"))$ from (7.1). With ordinary de Rham differential $d$, the raw degree-$k$ Chern character is the Hodge edge of $\( 2 pi i \)^k "ch"_(B \, k)$. Thus the prescribed raw Hodge section in the theorem is the edge of $\( 2 pi i \)^k beta_(B \, k)$. The factor does not change horizontality or membership in $F^k$.

==== 7.2.1 Absolute perfectness and the coefficient obstruction group <absolute-perfectness-and-the-coefficient-obstruction-group>

Write

$ R = cal(O)_(S \, 0) = bb(C) { t_1 \, dots.h \, t_e } \, #h(2em) A_nu = R \/ frak(m)^(nu + 1) \, #h(2em) X_nu = cal(X) times_S "Specan" A_nu . $

Here $frak(m)$ is the maximal ideal of $R$. The integer $nu gt.eq 0$ records the infinitesimal order.

We construct compatible $A_nu$-flat sheaves $E_nu$ with central fiber $F_0$. If $E_nu$ has been constructed, the obstruction to the next lift has coefficients in $J_nu = ker \( A_(nu + 1) arrow.r A_nu \)$. The first lemma identifies its group with $"Ext"_(X_0)^2 \( F_0 \, F_0 \) ⊗_(bb(C)) J_nu$, on which central semiregularity remains injective. For these extensions, differentiation gives an injection $J_nu arrow.r Omega_(A_(nu + 1) \/ bb(C))^1 ⊗_(A_(nu + 1)) A_nu$. The comparison with Gauss–Manin then forces the obstruction to vanish if each selected component of the relative Chern character of $E_nu$ is the edge of its prescribed flat filtered class. We prove this character identity for every infinitesimal lift in Lemma 45; thus any lift produced at one stage satisfies the hypothesis needed at the next. Finally, we express the sheaves as quotients of one vector bundle by lifting the quotient-defining sections compatibly. The resulting formal section of the relative Quot space admits an analytic approximation preserving the central quotient. Pulling back the universal quotient gives a flat sheaf with central fiber $F_0$, whose fiber characters are determined by flat transport.

