==== 7.2.2 A square-zero lift and its semiregularity obstruction <a-square-zero-lift-and-its-semiregularity-obstruction>

Fix $nu$, put $A' = A_(nu + 1)$, $A = A_nu$, and write

$ J = frak(m)^(nu + 1) \/ frak(m)^(nu + 2) \, #h(2em) K = Omega_(A' \/ bb(C))^1 ⊗_(A') A \, #h(2em) j : X = X_nu arrow.r.hook X' = X_(nu + 1) . $

The ideal of $X$ is $J_X = cal(O)_X ⊗_A J$. We have $J^2 = 0$ and $frak(m)_A J = 0$. Moreover

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.23)")
 $ K = Omega_(R \/ bb(C))^1 \/ frak(m)^(nu + 1) Omega_(R \/ bb(C))^1 tilde.eq A^(xor e) \, #h(2em) d : J arrow.r.hook K . $ <p3sw:bf-jacobi>]

Indeed $d \( frak(m)^(nu + 2) \) subset frak(m)^(nu + 1) Omega_R^1$. On homogeneous terms the last map is

$ "Sym"^(nu + 1) \( frak(m) \/ frak(m)^2 \) arrow.r "Sym"^nu \( frak(m) \/ frak(m)^2 \) ⊗ \( frak(m) \/ frak(m)^2 \) \, $

given by differentiation. Euler’s formula $sum_i t_i partial_i f = \( nu + 1 \) f$ proves injectivity in characteristic zero.

