==== 7.2.3 The Chern character of every infinitesimal lift <the-chern-character-of-every-infinitesimal-lift>

The one-step lemma can be iterated only after checking that a chosen lift has the prescribed relative character, rather than merely the prescribed central restriction. The projectivity assumption supplies a direct proof for every stage.

#thm([Lemma 7.7.])[
  #emph[For every $A_nu$-flat coherent sheaf $E_nu$ on $X_nu$ with central fiber $F_0$, and every $0 lt.eq k lt.eq n$, the ordinary flat comparison 
$ H_(upright("dR"))^l \( X_nu \/ A_nu \) tilde.eq H^l \( X_0 \, bb(C) \) ⊗_(bb(C)) A_nu $

 satisfies 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(7.33)")
 $ "ch"_(H \, k)^(upright("rel")) \( E_nu \) & = "edge"_k #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( 2 pi i \)^k "ch"_(B \, k) \( F_0 \) ⊗ 1 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \,\
\( 2 pi i \)^k "ch"_(B \, k) \( F_0 \) ⊗ 1 & in F^k H_(upright("dR"))^(2 k) \( X_nu \/ A_nu \) . $ <p3sw:bf-artin-character-formula>]

 The assertion holds independently of the choice of the lift.]
] <p3sw:bf-artin-character>

#proof[
  For $k = 0$ the assertion is constancy of rank on each connected component. Assume $k gt.eq 1$. The projective model of $X_nu$ has an ample line. Repeated Serre global-generation surjections from finite sums of negative powers of that line resolve $E_nu$. The local projective-dimension bound in Lemma 43 makes the $n$-th syzygy locally free. GAGA gives a global finite holomorphic vector-bundle resolution $V^bullet arrow.r E_nu$. No compatibility of these resolutions as $nu$ varies is asserted or needed.
  Choose a differentiable trivialization of the proper submersion $cal(X) arrow.r S$. Holomorphic $t$-jets through order $nu$ of relative smooth forms define a morphism
  $ Omega_(X_nu \/ A_nu)^bullet arrow.r cal(A)_(X_0)^bullet ⊗_(bb(C)) A_nu \, $
  where $cal(A)_(X_0)^bullet$ is the smooth de Rham complex. Explicitly, after the trivialization take
  $ sum_(\| upright(bold(a)) \| lt.eq nu) frac(\( partial_t^(upright(bold(a))) omega \) \|_(t = macron(t) = 0), upright(bold(a)) !) thin t^(upright(bold(a))) . $
  Leibniz’s rule makes this compatible with products, and jets commute with the fiber differential. The jets of the fiberwise type projections give a bigrading on the target; their integrability identities persist under taking jets. In a local holomorphic product chart the resulting Dolbeault complex is the ordinary one with coefficients $A_nu$, up to an invertible formal smooth coordinate change. The coefficientwise Dolbeault lemma therefore makes it a fine resolution of each $Omega_(X_nu \/ A_nu)^p$, respecting the form filtration.
  Both total complexes resolve the constant sheaf $A_nu$. For the holomorphic complex, filter by powers of $frak(m)$; its associated graded complex is $Omega_(X_0)^bullet ⊗_(bb(C)) "gr" A_nu$, to which the holomorphic Poincare lemma applies. The jet map is the identity on constants. It therefore realizes the ordinary flat de Rham comparison in the statement.
  A holomorphic vector bundle $V$ on $X_nu$ becomes a smooth $A_nu$-bundle under the same jet map, and is smoothly isomorphic to $V_0^oo ⊗ A_nu$. Indeed, modulo successive powers of $frak(m)$, the discrepancy between its transition matrices and the central ones is a Cech one-cocycle with values in smooth sections of $"End" \( V_0^oo \) ⊗ frak(m)^a \/ frak(m)^(a + 1)$. This sheaf is fine, so a smooth gauge change removes that discrepancy. Induction through the finitely many powers of $frak(m)$ gives the isomorphism.
  Choose a smooth right-module connection $D = macron(partial)_V + nabla^(1 \, 0)$ on this bundle, and put $F_D = D^2$. Its curvature has no $\( 0 \, 2 \)$-part. In the Dolbeault resolution the BF commutator $\[ macron(partial) \, nabla \]$, with its form coefficient written after the $\( 0 \, q \)$-form, maps under exterior multiplication to $F_D^(1 \, 1)$. The sign also follows from the first-jet calculation above: for a line with frames $e_j = e_i g_(i j)$, the BF cocycle is $- d log g_(i j)$, while the connection matrices differ by $d log g_(i j)$; the Cech–Dolbeault boundary carries this cocycle to $+ accent(partial A, ‾)_i$. Thus $a = - "At"_(upright("BF"))$ is represented by $- F_D^(1 \, 1)$. It follows that the closed form
  $ frac(\( - 1 \)^k, k !) "tr" \( F_D^k \) $
  belongs to $F^k$ and that its edge is the raw degree-$k$ Atiyah character.
  This de Rham class is the constant central class. Use a constant connection on $V_0^oo ⊗ A_nu$ and the smooth isomorphism just constructed. For $D_u = D_0 + u \( D_1 - D_0 \)$, the Chern–Simons identity is
  $ partial_u frac("tr" \( F_(D_u)^k \), k !) = d thin frac("tr" \( \( D_1 - D_0 \) F_(D_u)^(k - 1) \), \( k - 1 \) !) . $
  Integration in $u$ proves independence of the connection; the polynomial identity is valid over the finite complex algebra $A_nu$. For this right-connection convention, the normalized topological character is $"tr" exp \( - F_D \/ \( 2 pi i \) \)$. Hence the signed unscaled form above represents $\( 2 pi i \)^k "ch"_(B \, k) \( V_0 \)$ in the flat comparison.
  Apply the calculation termwise to $V^bullet$. A grading-preserving connection represents $"At"_(upright("BF"))$ by its commutator with $d_(V^bullet) + macron(partial)$, and $a$ by the negative of that commutator. In a power, every term involving $d_(V^bullet)$ has positive internal degree and zero supertrace. The remaining terms are the alternating sum of the signed bundle curvature traces. Their edge is therefore $"ch"_(H \, k)^(upright("rel")) \( E_nu \)$. By (7.22), the central restriction of the resolution resolves $F_0$. Its alternating central character is $"ch"_(B \, k) \( F_0 \)$, which proves (7.33). ◻
]

