For $p : Z arrow.r Y$ in Proposition 41, the morphism $a compose p$ satisfies the theorem’s projectivity hypothesis, $F_0$ is ordinary coherent and fully semiregular, and its relative dimension is $N = 6 + m - 1$. Taking $I = { 1 \, dots.h \, N }$, the raw input in degree $k$ is exactly the edge of $\( 2 pi i \)^k A_(B \, k)$. The theorem uses neither a relative version of the finite isogeny $q$ nor a deformation of the twisted sheaf $cal(B)$. Its proof also does not assert arbitrary classical base change for global Ext groups or higher direct images. The smooth projective germ and the ordinary coherent-sheaf condition are the stated limits of the deformation result.

== 8 Algebraicity at every compatible period <p3:sec:all-periods>

We apply the descent and deformation results to the normalized character computed for the polarized seed. Projective-bundle operations first give local algebraicity. A marked period family and proper Hilbert parameter spaces then extend the Weil plane to every compatible period, and rational isogenies remove the dependence on the initial lattice.

=== 8.1 The descended character on the marked Weil domain <p3sw:endpoint-marked-domain>

Fix $K = bb(Q) \( u \)$, with $u^2 = - d$ and $d gt.eq 3$, and use the seed $M = X times hat(X)$ constructed above. Its coherent perfect sheaf $cal(E)$ has rank $r = 8 d$. We write $"cl"_B$ for the usual rational Betti cycle-class map, normalized so that the first Chern class $c_1^B \( L \)$ of a line bundle is integral. The normalized character on the seed is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.1)")
 $ kappa_(M \, B) = exp #h(-0.16667em) (- frac(c_1^B \( cal(E) \), r)) "ch"_B \( cal(E) \) = sum_(j = 0)^6 kappa_(M \, B \, j) . $ <p3sw:endpoint-seed-character>]

Here $c_1^B \( cal(E) \) = c_1^B \( det cal(E) \)$, so this is the class $kappa_M$ of Section 6 in normalized Betti notation. Proposition 35 supplies the unital $K$-action on $M$ and its ample class $theta_M$, and Proposition 37 gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.2)")
 $ kappa_(M \, B \, 3) = c_theta theta_M^3 + w_M \, #h(2em) c_theta in bb(Q) \, #h(2em) 0 eq.not w_M in cal(W)_K \( M \) . $ <p3sw:endpoint-exceptional-component>]

Proposition 38 proves that every $kappa_(M \, B \, j)$ has type $\( j \, j \)$ at every marked complex structure of Weil type $\( 3 \, 3 \)$ compatible with this action and polarization. We will transport these properties to the quotient on which the descended sheaf is defined.

==== 8.1.1 The actual homological Hermitian space <the-actual-homological-hermitian-space>

Let $e_M$ be the Riemann form of the ample class $theta_M$. The actual homological Hermitian form is

$ h_M \( x \, y \) = e_M \( x \, eta_M \( u \)_(*) y \) + u e_M \( x \, y \) . $

In the second-linear convention of (3.1), Proposition 35 proves that this form is hyperbolic of signature $\( 3 \, 3 \)$; its three hyperbolic blocks are computed in (6.16).

Let $q : M arrow.r Y_0$ be the isogeny in Proposition 40. Put

$ V_0 = H_1 \( Y_0 \, bb(Q) \) \, #h(2em) Lambda_0 = H_1 \( Y_0 \, bb(Z) \) \, #h(2em) theta_0 = \( q^(*) \)^(- 1) theta_M . $

Transport the rational action through $q$ in the category up to isogeny, and write $T_0 = eta_0 \( u \)_(*)$ for the transported action on $V_0$. The alternating form $e_0$ associated to $theta_0$ is characterized by

$ e_0 \( q_(*) x \, q_(*) y \) = e_M \( x \, y \) . $

It is rational and is a positive Riemann form at $Y_0$; a positive integer multiple is integral on $Lambda_0$ and is an ample polarization. The second-linear Hermitian form

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.3)")
 $ h_0 \( x \, y \) = e_0 \( x \, T_0 y \) + u e_0 \( x \, y \) $ <p3sw:endpoint-reference-hermitian>]

is carried to $h_M$ by $q_(*)$, so it too is hyperbolic of signature $\( 3 \, 3 \)$.

==== 8.1.2 The full compatible domain <the-full-compatible-domain>

Let $cal(D)$ be the set of real complex structures $I$ on $V_(0 \, bb(R))$ satisfying

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.4)")
 $ I^2 = - 1 \, #h(2em) I T_0 = T_0 I \, #h(2em) e_0 \( I x \, I y \) = e_0 \( x \, y \) \, #h(2em) e_0 \( x \, I x \) > 0 quad \( x eq.not 0 \) . $ <p3sw:endpoint-compatible-structures>]

This is the full marked compatible domain for the fixed rational space, form, and lattice. We verify both its connectedness and the complex structure used below.

Choose the embedding $sigma : K arrow.r.hook bb(C)$ for which $sigma \( u \) = i sqrt(d)$, and view $V_(0 \, sigma) = V_0 ⊗_(K \, sigma) bb(C)$ with its Hermitian form $h_(0 \, sigma)$. A real $I$ commuting with $K$ is complex-linear on this space. Commutation with $T_0$ together with preservation of $e_0$ implies that $I$ is unitary for $h_(0 \, sigma)$. Its $i$- and $- i$-eigenspaces are therefore orthogonal. On the first of them $T_0 = sqrt(d) thin I$, and on the second $T_0 = - sqrt(d) thin I$. Since $h_0 \( x \, x \) = e_0 \( x \, T_0 x \)$, Riemann positivity makes the first eigenspace positive and the second negative. Each has dimension three because $h_0$ has signature $\( 3 \, 3 \)$.

Conversely, if $P subset V_(0 \, sigma)$ is a positive three-plane, then $P^perp$ is negative of dimension three. Set $I = i$ on $P$ and $I = - i$ on $P^perp$. This is a real complex structure commuting with $K$ and preserving $h_0$, hence also $e_0$, which is the coefficient of $u$ in (8.3). For $x = x_(+) + x_(-)$ in this orthogonal decomposition,

$ e_0 \( x \, I x \) = d^(- 1 \/ 2) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] h_(0 \, sigma) \( x_(+) \, x_(+) \) - h_(0 \, sigma) \( x_(-) \, x_(-) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] > 0 quad \( x eq.not 0 \) . $

Thus positive three-planes parameterize exactly $cal(D)$. Fix a positive/negative orthogonal splitting $V_(0 \, sigma) = V_(+) xor V_(-)$. Projection of a positive three-plane to $V_(+)$ is an isomorphism, so that plane is the graph of a unique map $Z : V_(+) arrow.r V_(-)$. Positivity is precisely

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.5)")
 $ 1 - Z^(*) Z > 0 \, #h(2em) upright("equivalently") #h(2em) lr(bar.v.double Z bar.v.double)_(upright("op")) < 1 . $ <p3sw:endpoint-matrix-ball>]

The operator-norm unit ball in $"Hom"_(bb(C)) \( V_(+) \, V_(-) \)$ is convex. In particular the underlying domain is connected and contractible and has complex dimension nine.

For the holomorphic convention, use cohomological periods. Write $W_sigma xor W_(macron(sigma)) = H^1 \( Y_0 \, bb(C) \)$ for the two $K$-eigenspaces. The cohomological polarization induced by $e_0$ pairs these two spaces perfectly and vanishes on each separately. If $F^1$ is a compatible Lagrangian Hodge subspace, then $P^or = F^1 ∩ W_sigma$ is a three-plane and

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.6)")
 $ F^1 = P^or xor "Ann" \( P^or \) subset W_sigma xor W_(macron(sigma)) \, $ <p3sw:endpoint-lagrangian-period>]

where the annihilator is taken using that perfect pairing. Conversely this formula, restricted by the open Riemann positivity condition, gives precisely the compatible periods. The annihilator map between the two Grassmannians is algebraic. We give $cal(D)$ the complex structure in the coordinate $P^or$; then (8.6) is holomorphic into the Siegel period domain. This coordinate can be the conjugate of the homological graph coordinate in (8.5); the connected underlying space and its complex dimension are unchanged.

==== 8.1.3 A fine principal-level family and the seed lattice <a-fine-principal-level-family-and-the-seed-lattice>

Choose a rational symplectic basis $a_1 \, dots.h \, a_6 \, b_1 \, dots.h \, b_6$ for $e_0$, normalized by $e_0 \( a_i \, b_j \) = delta_(i j)$, and let $Lambda_(upright("pp"))$ be its integral span. Such a basis follows by repeatedly choosing a pair of pairing one and taking its symplectic orthogonal complement. The restriction of $e_0$ to $Lambda_(upright("pp"))$ is integral and unimodular. The Riemann criterion for polarized tori therefore makes

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.7)")
 $ A_I^(upright("pp")) = \( V_(0 \, bb(R)) \, I \) \/ Lambda_(upright("pp")) \, #h(2em) I in cal(D) \, $ <p3sw:endpoint-reference-tori>]

principally polarized abelian sixfolds. The rational $K$-action persists in $"End"^0 \( A_I^(upright("pp")) \)$: every rational $K$-operator has an integer multiple preserving $Lambda_(upright("pp"))$, and it commutes with $I$.

We use the following precise fine-level theorem. Over $bb(C)$, fix a primitive fourth root for the symplectic Weil pairing. The functor of principally polarized abelian schemes of relative dimension six with a full symplectic level-$4$ isomorphism to the standard $\( bb(Z) \/ 4 \)^6 times mu_4^6$ has a smooth quasi-projective fine moduli scheme $cal(A)_6 \( 4 \)$ and a universal abelian scheme $pi : cal(U) arrow.r cal(A)_6 \( 4 \)$. On the chosen analytic component it is the quotient of the universal marked torus family on $frak(H)_6$ by the principal congruence subgroup $Gamma_6 \( 4 \)$; both this component and the universal total space are quasi-projective algebraic varieties. These are Chai’s symplectic-level and uniformization statements [9] Chapter I, Theorems 1.4 and 1.7.2, and Sections 2.3–2.5, pp. 5–13. The hypotheses here are explicit: $e_0$ is perfect on $Lambda_(upright("pp"))$, the chosen basis supplies the level, $4 gt.eq 3$ removes stabilizers, and the ground field has characteristic zero.

The universal $pi$ is smooth and projective. Smoothness and properness are part of being the universal abelian scheme. To see projectivity from the stated theorem without choosing a universal theta line, embed the quasi-projective $cal(U)$ locally closed in some $bb(P)_(bb(C))^b$. Its graph over the separated scheme $cal(A)_6 \( 4 \)$ is a locally closed immersion

$ cal(U) arrow.r bb(P)_(cal(A)_6 \( 4 \))^b . $

This morphism is proper: for any morphism from an $cal(A)_6 \( 4 \)$-proper scheme to an $cal(A)_6 \( 4 \)$-separated scheme, the graph is closed and the second projection is a base change of a proper morphism. A proper immersion is a closed immersion. The displayed map is therefore a projective embedding over $cal(A)_6 \( 4 \)$.

The symplectic basis identifies the space of all positive Lagrangians with $frak(H)_6$. The compatible Lagrangians in (8.6) give a holomorphic map $cal(D) arrow.r frak(H)_6$. Composition with the level quotient gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.8)")
 $ nu : cal(D) arrow.r cal(A)_6 \( 4 \)^(upright("an")) . $ <p3sw:endpoint-level-period-map>]

The quoted marked uniformization identifies $nu^(*) cal(U)^(upright("an"))$ with the family (8.7). This construction uses the ordinary principally polarized level family. It asserts neither that $cal(D)$ or its image is algebraic nor that the ambient universal family has a $K$-action.

We also obtain the precise family with central fiber $Y_0$. Choose an integer $c_(upright("lat")) > 0$ such that $c_(upright("lat")) Lambda_(upright("pp")) subset Lambda_0$. The finite group

$ G_(upright("lat")) = c_(upright("lat"))^(- 1) Lambda_0 \/ Lambda_(upright("pp")) subset V_0 \/ Lambda_(upright("pp")) $

consists of torsion points of uniformly bounded order. In the marked family on the contractible $cal(D)$ these are holomorphic torsion sections. Indeed, the kernel of multiplication by their common order is a finite étale holomorphic cover of $cal(D)$, and the marking identifies it with the constant torsion lattice. The chosen torsion classes therefore select sections, which are holomorphic because the cover is locally biholomorphic. Translation by $G_(upright("lat"))$ is free. Taking the quotient, locally by disjoint translated coordinate neighborhoods, gives a smooth holomorphic group family with zero section. It is proper because it is the finite quotient of a proper family. Its fiber is $\( V_(0 \, bb(R)) \, I \) \/ \( c_(upright("lat"))^(- 1) Lambda_0 \)$. Multiplication by $c_(upright("lat"))$ identifies it with

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.9)")
 $ Y_I = \( V_(0 \, bb(R)) \, I \) \/ Lambda_0 \, $ <p3sw:endpoint-seed-lattice-family>]

and identifies the seed fiber with $Y_0$.

This family is polarized in the relative sense required in Proposition 41. Indeed, choose an integer $c_(upright("pol")) > 0$ so that $c_(upright("pol")) e_0$ is integral on $Lambda_0$. Let $cal(P)_I$ be the normalized Poincaré line on $Y_I times hat(Y)_I$. Mark the dual homology by

$ H_1 \( hat(Y)_I \, bb(Z) \) arrow.r^tilde.op Lambda_0^or \, #h(2em) xi mapsto #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\[] y mapsto c_(1 \, upright(B)) \( cal(P)_I \) \( \( y \, 0 \) \, \( 0 \, xi \) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\]] . $

In this marking the dual complex structure is $- I^t$. The constant lattice map

$ F : Lambda_0 arrow.r Lambda_0^or \, #h(2em) x mapsto #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\[] y mapsto c_(upright("pol")) e_0 \( y \, x \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\]] $

extends to the marked real vector spaces. It is complex-linear from $I$ to the dual complex structure $- I^t$, since

$ F \( I x \) \( y \) = c_(upright("pol")) e_0 \( y \, I x \) = - c_(upright("pol")) e_0 \( I y \, x \) = \( - I^t F \( x \) \) \( y \) . $

Equivalently, its constant complexification preserves the holomorphic Hodge subbundles in the marked uniformizations, so it induces a holomorphic homomorphism from the family $Y$ to its dual family. On each fiber the Riemann criterion supplies an ample line $L_I$ with alternating form $c_(upright("pol")) e_0$. The normalized mixed-biextension identity (4.3), evaluated on $\( y \, 0 \) \, \( 0 \, x \)$, gives

$ \( \( phi.alt_(L_I) \)_(*) x \) \( y \) = c_(upright("pol")) e_0 \( y \, x \) \, #h(2em) phi.alt_(L_I) \( z \) = t_z^(*) L_I ⊗ L_I^(- 1) . $

Homomorphisms of complex tori are determined by their maps on the universal covers, so the family homomorphism is fiberwise this polarization. Its ample class is the marked transport of $c_(upright("pol")) theta_0$. In particular all fibers are projective. The local representing line, and its relative ampleness after shrinking, are supplied by the relative Picard argument immediately preceding Proposition 41. Restricting (8.9) to a small contractible open neighborhood $S$ of the seed gives the polarized holomorphic abelian scheme $a : Y arrow.r S$ used there; $S$ is a smooth nine-dimensional analytic germ.

The quotient also gives, on each fiber, an isogeny

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.10)")
 $ j_I : A_I^(upright("pp")) arrow.r Y_I $ <p3sw:endpoint-reference-isogeny>]

induced by $c_(upright("lat")) "id"_(V_0)$. It is $K$-linear in the category up to isogeny. It is algebraic: both tori are projective, and Serre’s GAGA Proposition 15 says that a holomorphic map from a compact algebraic variety to an algebraic variety is regular [37] Section 4, no. 19, Proposition 15, pp. 29–30. Only this fiberwise isogeny is needed; the central isogeny $q$ has not been extended as a relative algebraic isogeny.

==== 8.1.4 The flat descended character <the-flat-descended-character>

Proposition 40 supplies the determinant-trivial twisted coherent sheaf $cal(B)$ of rank $r$ on $Y_0$, with full twisted-derived semiregularity. Its character comparison (7.11) is the equality

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.11)")
 $ kappa_B := "ch"_B \( cal(B) \) in xor.big_(j = 0)^6 H^(2 j) \( Y_0 \, bb(Q) \) \, #h(2em) q^(*) kappa_B = kappa_(M \, B) . $ <p3sw:endpoint-descended-character>]

Rationality here is part of that comparison, or follows by applying the rational trace $\( deg q \)^(- 1) q_(*)^B$ to its right side. Because $q_(*)$ is an isomorphism of rational first homology, $q^(*)$ is an isomorphism of rational cohomology rings. It is $K$-linear on first cohomology for the transported action and therefore identifies the two Weil planes. Consequently (8.2) becomes

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.12)")
 $ kappa_(B \, 3) = c_theta theta_0^3 + w_0 \, #h(2em) 0 eq.not w_0 in cal(W)_K \( Y_0 \) . $ <p3sw:endpoint-descended-component>]

Use the lattice marking in (8.9) to transport $kappa_B$, $theta_0$, and $w_0$, denoting their values by $kappa_B \( I \)$, $theta_I$, and $w_I$. They are rational flat sections. For each $I in cal(D)$, transporting $I$ through $q_(*)$ gives a compatible polarized complex structure on the rational homology of $M$. The constant map $q^(*)$ is a Hodge map for these corresponding structures. Proposition 38, applied to the invariant character in (8.1), thus proves

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.13)")
 $ kappa_(B \, j) \( I \) in H^(j \, j) \( Y_I \) ∩ H^(2 j) \( Y_I \, bb(Q) \) quad \( 0 lt.eq j lt.eq 6 \, med I in cal(D) \) . $ <p3sw:endpoint-flat-hodge>]

Also $theta_I$ is a rational polarization class at every period, and (8.12) remains the flat equality

$ kappa_(B \, 3) \( I \) = c_theta theta_I^3 + w_I \, #h(2em) 0 eq.not w_I in cal(W)_K \( Y_I \) . $

This is an assertion on the marked domain. No section on an arithmetic quotient and no relative twisted sheaf is used to define these flat classes.

=== 8.2 Local algebraicity and projective extraction <p3sw:endpoint-local-section>

#thm([Proposition 8.1.])[
  (Local algebraicity of the descended character).  #emph[There is a nonempty analytic neighborhood of the seed in $cal(D)$ on which every component $kappa_(B \, j) \( I \)$ is a rational algebraic cycle class on $Y_I$.]
] <p3sw:local-algebraicity>

#proof[
  Take the smooth contractible nine-dimensional germ $S$ and its polarized abelian scheme $a : Y arrow.r S$ constructed above. Apply Proposition 41 to the descended sheaf $cal(B)$, and retain its notation
  $ m = r^12 \, #h(2em) N = 6 + m - 1 \, #h(2em) p : Z arrow.r Y \, #h(2em) F_0 = p_0^(*) cal(B) ⊗ ell_0 . $
  Thus $a compose p$ is a proper holomorphic submersion of relative dimension $N$ with a relative ample line. The sheaf $F_0$ is ordinary coherent, and its raw semiregularity map is injective on the entire group $"Ext"_(Z_0)^2 \( F_0 \, F_0 \)$. The proposition also supplies the relative anticanonical line and its normalized Betti class
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.14)")
   $ H = omega_(Z \/ Y)^(- 1) \, #h(2em) zeta_(B \, s) = frac(c_1^B \( H \|_(Z_s) \), m) . $ <p3sw:endpoint-zeta>]
  The class $zeta_B$ is rational, flat, and of type $\( 1 \, 1 \)$, and restricts to the hyperplane class on every $p_s$-fiber. Each $Z_s$ is projective algebraic, $p_s$ is a projective algebraic morphism, and $H \|_(Z_s)$ is an algebraic line bundle.
  Now put
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.15)")
   $ A_B \( s \) = p_s^(*) kappa_B \( s \) e^(zeta_(B \, s)) = sum_(k = 0)^N A_(B \, k) \( s \) . $ <p3sw:endpoint-horizontal-A>]
  Equations (8.13) and (8.14) show that $A_(B \, k)$ is a rational flat section of $R^(2 k) \( a compose p \)_(*) bb(Q)$ of type $\( k \, k \)$. In particular its flat de Rham lift lies in $F^k$. The central character identity (7.18) gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.16)")
   $ A_B \( 0 \) = p_0^(*) "ch"_B \( cal(B) \) e^(zeta_(B \, 0)) = "ch"_B \( F_0 \) . $ <p3sw:endpoint-central-A>]
  In the raw $d log$ convention, the prescribed Hodge section in degree $k$ is the edge of $\( 2 pi i \)^k A_(B \, k)$. Its central value is $"ch"_(H \, k) \( F_0 \)$ by (7.2) and (8.16). Apply Theorem 42 with $beta_(B \, k) = A_(B \, k)$ and $I_(upright("BF")) = { 1 \, dots.h \, N }$. This index set retains the full semiregularity injection: every potentially nonzero target $H^(j + 2) \( Z_0 \, Omega_(Z_0)^j \)$ has $j lt.eq N - 2$ and occurs at $k = j + 1$; the target for $k = N$ is zero. All the theorem’s hypotheses now hold, so after one common shrinking of $S$ every $A_(B \, k) \( s \)$, $1 lt.eq k lt.eq N$, is rational algebraic. The degree-zero term $A_(B \, 0) = r$ is already algebraic.
  On a fiber of this smaller germ, choose rational Chow classes representing all $A_(B \, k) \( s \)$, and represent $zeta_(B \, s)$ by $c_1^(upright("CH")) \( H \|_(Z_s) \) \/ m$. Products and the finite exponential polynomial give a rational Chow class whose Betti image is $zeta_(B \, s)^(m - 1) e^(- zeta_(B \, s)) A_B \( s \)$. Proper pushforward along the algebraic $p_s$ has Betti class $kappa_B \( s \)$ by (7.17). Every exponential is truncated in the cohomology or Chow ring at the dimension $N$. Taking homogeneous components of this proper pushforward proves that every $kappa_(B \, j) \( s \)$ is rational algebraic. ◻
]

==== 8.2.1 Two algebraic generators of the rational plane <two-algebraic-generators-of-the-rational-plane>

#thm([Lemma 8.2.])[
  #emph[Let $A$ be an abelian sixfold with a unital action of the imaginary quadratic field $K$. If a nonzero vector of $cal(W)_K \( A \)$ is a rational algebraic class, then every vector of $cal(W)_K \( A \)$ is a rational algebraic class.]
] <p3sw:endpoint-two-generators>

#proof[
  Choose $k = 1 + t u in K^times$ with $t in bb(Q)$ such that $sigma \( k \)^6 eq.not macron(sigma) \( k \)^6$. Such $t$ exists because
  $ \( 1 + t u \)^6 - \( 1 - t u \)^6 $
  is a nonzero polynomial in $t$, with linear coefficient $12 u$; only finitely many rational $t$ are excluded. Over $K$ (or $overline(bb(Q))$) the plane is the direct sum of the two conjugate sixth exterior eigenspaces from the definition of $cal(W)_K \( A \)$. A nonzero rational vector $w$ has nonzero coordinates in both lines: scalar conjugation exchanges them, so one zero coordinate would force the other to be zero. On the two lines, $k^(*) = eta \( k \)^(*)$ has respective eigenvalues $sigma \( k \)^6$ and $macron(sigma) \( k \)^6$. The determinant formed by $w$ and $k^(*) w$ in those coordinates is therefore nonzero. They are linearly independent over $bb(Q)$, and form a basis of $cal(W)_K \( A \)$.
  Choose an integer $n_k > 0$ clearing the denominator of $eta \( k \) in "End"^0 \( A \)$. The resulting $h = n_k eta \( k \)$ is an algebraic endomorphism, and on $H^6$ one has $k^(*) = n_k^(- 6) h^(*)$. Pullback by $h$ preserves rational algebraic cycle classes, so $k^(*) w$ is algebraic when $w$ is. Both basis vectors, hence the whole rational plane, are algebraic. ◻
]

On the open supplied by Proposition 46, the class

$ w_I = kappa_(B \, 3) \( I \) - c_theta theta_I^3 $

is rational algebraic: an integer multiple of $theta_I$ is an ample polarization class. It is nonzero everywhere because it is the transport of the single nonzero marked vector $w_0$. Lemma 47 makes $cal(W)_K \( Y_I \)$ algebraic on that open. The isogeny (8.10) pulls this plane isomorphically to $cal(W)_K \( A_I^(upright("pp")) \)$: its pullback on $H^1$ is a $K$-linear rational isomorphism and thus identifies both sixth exterior eigenspaces. Algebraic pullback preserves their cycle classes.

Let

$ bb(W)_(upright("pp")) subset nu^(*) R^6 pi_(*)^(upright("an")) bb(Q) $

be the constant rank-two rational local subsystem defined by the fixed marked $K$-eigenspaces; its fiber is $cal(W)_K \( A_I^(upright("pp")) \)$. We have proved that all its fibers are algebraic on a nonempty analytic open of $cal(D)$. Only this subsystem, not the individual character $kappa_B$, will be used in the global step.

=== 8.3 Spreading the plane in the fine family <p3sw:endpoint-spread-section>

#thm([Proposition 8.3.])[
  (Analytic Hilbert spread).  #emph[Let $pi : cal(X) arrow.r S$ be a smooth projective morphism of relative dimension $n$ over a smooth quasi-projective complex variety. Let $nu : T arrow.r S^(upright("an"))$ be holomorphic, where $T$ is a connected complex manifold. Fix $0 lt.eq c lt.eq n$ and a finite-rank rational local subsystem 
$ bb(V) subset nu^(*) R^(2 c) pi_(*)^(upright("an")) bb(Q) . $

 If every vector of $bb(V)_t$ is a rational algebraic codimension-$c$ class on $cal(X)_(nu \( t \))$ for all $t$ in a nonempty analytic open subset of $T$, then the same is true for every $t in T$.]
] <p3sw:spread>

#proof[
  The assertion is immediate if the rank $b$ of $bb(V)$ is zero, so assume $b > 0$. Fix a relatively very ample line for $cal(X) \/ S$. For each of $b$ candidate generators choose a finite list of rational coefficients and, for each term in that list, a Hilbert polynomial of degree $n - c$. There are only countably many such finite choices. For one choice let $cal(H)$ be the product over $S$ of the corresponding relative Hilbert schemes, with structure map $rho : cal(H) arrow.r S$.
  The exact representability result used here is Grothendieck’s fixed-polynomial Hilbert theorem: for a projective scheme over a noetherian base, a fixed relatively very ample line and Hilbert polynomial $P$ give a projective relative Hilbert scheme representing closed subschemes flat over a locally noetherian test scheme with fiber polynomial $P$, with its universal flat subscheme. It is the case $F = cal(O)_(cal(X))$ of the fixed-polynomial Quot theorem [19] Section 3, Theorem 3.2, pp. 259–260, and the Hilbert specialization on p. 265. Here $S$ is noetherian and $pi$ is projective, so the theorem applies. In particular $cal(H) arrow.r S$ is projective. The space $cal(H)$ may be singular or nonreduced; we do not discard any of its fibers.
  After analytic base change to $T$, the parameters whose $b$ rational combinations span $bb(V)$ will form an open and closed locus. Properness will make its image a closed analytic subset of $T$. The countably many choices cover the assumed open algebraicity locus, so Baire’s theorem and the identity principle will force one image to be all of $T$. The first step is to prove the cycle-class constancy that makes the spanning condition locally constant, including at singular parameter points.
  Put $X_(cal(H)) = cal(X) times_S cal(H)$. For each factor of $cal(H)$, let $cal(F)$ be the structure sheaf of its pulled-back universal subscheme on $X_(cal(H))$. It is coherent and flat over $cal(H)$. It is also perfect on $X_(cal(H))$, with a uniform local projective-dimension bound $n$. To see this, at $x mapsto h$ write
  $ R = cal(O)_(cal(H) \, h) \, #h(2em) B = cal(O)_(X_(cal(H)) \, x) \, #h(2em) M = cal(F)_x \, #h(2em) overline(B) = B ⊗_R k \( h \) . $
  Both $B$ and $M$ are $R$-flat. The local fiber ring $overline(B)$ is regular of dimension at most $n$, since $X_(cal(H)) arrow.r cal(H)$ is smooth of relative dimension $n$. Derived associativity and this flatness give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.17)")
   $ M ⊗_B^(upright(bold(L))) k \( x \) tilde.eq \( M ⊗_R k \( h \) \) ⊗_(overline(B))^(upright(bold(L))) k \( x \) . $ <p3sw:endpoint-hilbert-tor>]
  A regular local ring of dimension $e$ has global dimension at most $e$, as in the regular-local theorem [39] Tag 00O7, Proposition 10.110.1. The right side of (8.17) has no Tor homology above $n$. Over the noetherian local ring $B$, build a minimal resolution of the finite module $M$ by choosing minimal finite generators of each successive kernel. All its differentials become zero after tensoring with $k \( x \)$. The vanishing of $"Tor"_(n + 1)^B \( M \, k \( x \) \)$ therefore forces its $\( n + 1 \)$-st free term to vanish. This proves $"pd"_B M lt.eq n$.
  The scheme $X_(cal(H))$ is quasi-projective over $bb(C)$: both $cal(H) arrow.r S$ and $X_(cal(H)) arrow.r cal(H)$ are projective and $S$ is quasi-projective. Choose an ample line on $X_(cal(H))$. The ample-line resolution property says that on a quasi-compact scheme with such a line every finite-type quasi-coherent sheaf is a quotient of a finite direct sum of negative powers of the line [39] Tag 01Q3, Proposition 28.27.13(8). Successively take these vector-bundle surjections onto $cal(F)$ and its coherent kernels. The $n$-th syzygy is locally free by the local projective-dimension bound just proved (and if $n = 0$, $cal(F)$ itself is locally free). Thus $cal(F)$ has a finite global vector-bundle resolution on $X_(cal(H))$. Derived restriction of that resolution to $h$ is an ordinary resolution of $cal(F)_h$: its terms are $cal(H)$-flat because $X_(cal(H))$ is $cal(H)$-flat, and $cal(F)$ is $cal(H)$-flat, so derived tensoring with $k \( h \)$ introduces no higher Tor.
  Here is a topological justification of local constancy that does not require a smooth Hilbert space. The original $pi^(upright("an")) : cal(X)^(upright("an")) arrow.r S^(upright("an"))$ is a proper smooth submersion between manifolds, because $S$ and $pi$ are smooth. Ehresmann’s fibration theorem gives a differentiable product over a sufficiently small coordinate ball $B$ around $s_0$: $cal(X)_(s_0)^(upright("an")) times B tilde.eq pi^(- 1) \( B \)$. Pull back this trivialization of the original smooth family to $cal(H)^(upright("an"))$; smoothness of the Hilbert space is not required.
  Under this product, restrict one vector bundle in the global resolution to $F times V$, where $F = cal(X)_(s_0)^(upright("an"))$ is compact and $V$ is a neighborhood of a chosen $h_0 in cal(H)^(upright("an"))$ over $B$. Shrinking $V$, finitely many product neighborhoods $U_i times V$ trivialize the bundle and cover $F times V$. A partition of unity on $F$ subordinate to the $U_i$, multiplied into these trivializations, embeds the bundle continuously in a fixed trivial complex vector bundle on $F times V$. Let $P \( x \, h \)$ be the orthogonal projection onto its image; it is continuous. Compactness of $F$ permits a further shrinking such that
  $ lr(bar.v.double P \( x \, h \) - P \( x \, h_0 \) bar.v.double) < 1 #h(2em) \( x in F \, med h in V \) . $
  The map $P \( x \, h \)$ from $"im" P \( x \, h_0 \)$ to $"im" P \( x \, h \)$ is then injective, since a nonzero vector in its kernel would contradict this inequality; the ranks agree, so it is an isomorphism. These maps are continuous bundle isomorphisms. Thus the topological Chern character of the bundle on each fiber is locally constant in $h$. Apply this to all the finitely many terms of the resolution. Their alternating Chern character is $"ch"_B \( cal(F)_h \)$ by the exact fiber restriction above. It defines a locally constant section of the cohomology local system pulled back to $cal(H)^(upright("an"))$. This argument is valid at singular and nonreduced parameter points, since their underlying topology and the restricted topological bundles are all that was used.
  To identify the cycle class including nonreduced multiplicities, use Baum–Fulton–MacPherson’s map $tau : K_0 arrow.r "CH"_(*) \( - \)_(bb(Q))$ for quasi-projective schemes over a field. It preserves the support-dimension filtration and sends $\[ cal(O)_V \]$ on the associated graded to $\[ V \]$; on smooth $X$, it sends a vector bundle $E$ to $"ch"_(upright("CH")) \( E \) "td" \( T_X \) ∩ \[ X \]$ [2] Chapter II, Theorem, pp. 119–120; Chapter III, Section 1, Theorem and Lemma, pp. 128–129. Put $s = rho \( h \)$. For $cal(F)_h$ supported in dimension at most $n - c$, let $V_i$ be its dimension-$\( n - c \)$ components with generic points $eta_i$, and put $ell_i = "length"_(cal(O)_(cal(X)_s \, eta_i)) \( cal(F)_(h \, eta_i) \)$. Devissage gives
  $ \[ cal(F)_h \] equiv sum_i ell_i \[ cal(O)_(V_i) \] med \( mod med upright("classes supported in dimension ") < n - c \) . $
  Indeed, generic composition series spread to dense opens; their discrepancies are lower-dimensional. Thus $tau \( cal(F)_h \)$ has no term above dimension $n - c$ and has top term $sum_i ell_i \[ V_i \]$. Additivity on a vector-bundle resolution gives
  $ tau \( cal(F)_h \) = "ch"_(upright("CH")) \( cal(F)_h \) "td" \( T_(cal(X)_s) \) ∩ \[ cal(X)_s \] . $
  Since $"td"_0 = 1$, comparison in successive codimensions gives $"ch"_(upright("CH") \, j) = 0$ for $j < c$ and $"ch"_(upright("CH") \, c) \( cal(F)_h \) ∩ \[ cal(X)_s \] = sum_i ell_i \[ V_i \]$. Compatibility of the cycle map with normalized Betti Chern classes [2] Section 0.8, p. 106 therefore gives, for the universal fiber subscheme $cal(Z)_h$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.18)")
   $ "ch"_(B \, c) \( cal(O)_(cal(Z)_h) \) = "cl"_B #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \[ cal(Z)_h \]_(n - c) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <p3sw:endpoint-leading-character>]
  Here $\[ cal(Z)_h \]_(n - c)$ is the fundamental cycle with its generic scheme lengths. Lower-dimensional and embedded contributions affect only higher character terms. The preceding local constancy of the character therefore proves precisely the local constancy of these rational cycle classes on $cal(H)^(upright("an"))$.
  Now form the analytic base change
  $ cal(H)_T = cal(H)^(upright("an")) times_(S^(upright("an"))) T arrow.r T . $
  It is proper and holomorphic: a projective embedding of $cal(H)$ over $S$ realizes this base change as a closed analytic subspace of $T times bb(P)^a$, whose projection is proper. The fixed rational combinations of the universal classes in (8.18) give locally constant sections of the pulled-back cohomology local system. In local trivializations the condition that these $b$ sections lie in and span $bb(V)$ is a condition on fixed rational vectors. It is locally constant. Its good locus is open and closed in $cal(H)_T$, hence is a closed analytic subspace and remains proper over $T$.
  We use Remmert’s proper-image theorem in its reduced-space form: the image of a proper holomorphic map between reduced complex spaces is an analytic subset. Grauert gives this precise statement and its derivation from the coherent direct-image theorem in Section 7, Satz 1, p. 60 [18]. Reduce the good locus before applying it. Reduction does not change its underlying proper map or its image, while $T$ is already reduced. The theorem thus applies even though the original Hilbert space could be nonreduced or singular. The image of the good locus is a closed analytic subset of $T$, closed also by properness.
  The countably many choices of coefficients and polynomials give countably many such good images. Their union is exactly the locus where $bb(V)_t$ is algebraic. In one direction, a good point supplies $b$ rational algebraic classes spanning the fiber. Conversely, if that fiber is algebraic, choose a rational basis and express each basis vector as a finite rational combination of integral codimension-$c$ subvarieties. Their reduced subschemes have Hilbert polynomials of degree $n - c$ and give a good point for one of the choices.
  A closed analytic subset unequal to the connected manifold $T$ is nowhere dense by the analytic identity principle. The assumed nonempty open subset of $T$ is itself a Baire space, so its countable covering by the good images cannot consist entirely of such nowhere dense subsets. Hence one good image equals $T$, which proves the assertion. ◻
]

Apply Proposition 48 with

$ S = cal(A)_6 \( 4 \) \, quad cal(X) = cal(U) \, quad T = cal(D) \, quad n = 6 \, quad c = 3 \, quad bb(V) = bb(W)_(upright("pp")) . $

The family is smooth projective, $cal(D)$ is a connected complex manifold, and the local algebraicity hypothesis was proved above. Therefore

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.19)")
 $ cal(W)_K \( A_I^(upright("pp")) \) subset "im" #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] "CH"^3 \( A_I^(upright("pp")) \)_(bb(Q)) arrow.r^("cl"_B) H^6 \( A_I^(upright("pp")) \, bb(Q) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] #h(2em) \( I in cal(D) \) . $ <p3sw:endpoint-all-reference-periods>]

The proof applies to the analytically pulled-back rational subsystem. It does not require an algebraic Weil locus or an algebraic $K$-action on the ambient fine family.

=== 8.4 Every hyperbolic rational lattice <p3sw:all-periods>

#thm([Proposition 8.4.])[
  #emph[For the fixed $K = bb(Q) \( u \)$ with $u^2 = - d$, $d gt.eq 3$, let $A$ be any complex abelian sixfold with a unital $K$-action and a compatible polarization. If its actual homological Hermitian form $h_A \( x \, y \) = e_A \( x \, u y \) + u e_A \( x \, y \)$ is hyperbolic, then $cal(W)_K \( A \)$ is a rational algebraic plane.]
] <p3sw:endpoint-all-lattices>

#proof[
  The hyperbolic-basis assertion of Lemma 10 identifies every nondegenerate rank-six hyperbolic Hermitian space over $K$, in the second-linear convention, with three planes of Gram matrix $mat(delim: "(", 0, 1; 1, 0)$. That elementary assertion was proved by splitting isotropic pairs, independently of the Weil-plane theorem. Since both $h_A$ and the actual seed form $h_0$ are hyperbolic, it gives a $K$-isometry
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.20)")
   $ f_A : \( H_1 \( A \, bb(Q) \) \, h_A \) arrow.r \( V_0 \, h_0 \) . $ <p3sw:endpoint-hermitian-isometry>]
  Let $Lambda_A = H_1 \( A \, bb(Z) \)$ and let $I_A$ be the complex structure of $A$. Transport it to $I = f_A I_A f_A^(- 1)$ on $V_(0 \, bb(R))$. The isometry is $K$-linear, so $I$ commutes with $T_0$. The Riemann form is recovered without any dual convention: it is exactly the coefficient of $u$ when $h \( x \, y \) = e \( x \, u y \) + u e \( x \, y \)$ is expanded in the $bb(Q)$-basis $1 \, u$ of $K$. Thus (8.20) implies
  $ e_0 \( f_A x \, f_A y \) = e_A \( x \, y \) . $
  It follows that $I$ preserves $e_0$ and satisfies $e_0 \( x \, I x \) > 0$ for $x eq.not 0$. Hence $I$ is a point of the full connected domain $cal(D)$. Equation (8.19) algebraizes the Weil plane of $A_I^(upright("pp"))$ at this particular period.
  The full rational lattices $f_A \( Lambda_A \)$ and $Lambda_(upright("pp"))$ in $V_0$ are commensurable. Choose an integer $n_A > 0$ such that $n_A f_A \( Lambda_A \) subset Lambda_(upright("pp"))$. The invertible complex-linear map $n_A f_A$ then induces a surjective holomorphic homomorphism with finite kernel
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(8.21)")
   $ phi : A = \( H_1 \( A \, bb(R) \) \, I_A \) \/ Lambda_A arrow.r A_I^(upright("pp")) = \( V_(0 \, bb(R)) \, I \) \/ Lambda_(upright("pp")) . $ <p3sw:endpoint-target-isogeny>]
  Both tori are projective, so this homomorphism is algebraic by Serre’s Proposition 15 cited above. It is a $K$-linear isogeny in the category up to isogeny. Its pullback on first rational cohomology is therefore a $K$-linear isomorphism. It maps each of the two embedding eigenspaces isomorphically, and taking their sixth exterior powers gives
  $ phi^(*) cal(W)_K \( A_I^(upright("pp")) \) = cal(W)_K \( A \) . $
  Pullback along the algebraic isogeny preserves rational codimension-three cycle classes. The left side is algebraic by (8.19), proving the proposition.
  The isogeny need not identify the integral polarizations: its pullback scales the rational Riemann form by $n_A^2$. Nor was a lift of $f_A$ to a rational spin group used. Only the elementary hyperbolic-basis argument and a lattice denominator were required, so extra endomorphisms or a product decomposition of $A$ impose no restriction. ◻
]

#proof[
  Every imaginary quadratic field can be written $K = bb(Q) \( u_0 \)$, with $u_0^2 = - d_0$ for a positive squarefree integer $d_0$. Set $u = 2 u_0$ and $d = 4 d_0 gt.eq 4$. This is the same field and is a parameter in the range of the seed construction. For any compatible Riemann form,
  $ h_u \( x \, y \) = e \( x \, u y \) + u e \( x \, y \) = 2 h_(u_0) \( x \, y \) . $
  More generally, changing a purely imaginary generator by any nonzero rational factor scales this Hermitian form by that factor. Such scaling does not change its isotropic subspaces; in signature $\( 3 \, 3 \)$ it does not change the signature either. Thus the hyperbolic hypothesis of the theorem is the hyperbolic hypothesis for the generator $u$ used by this seed. Proposition 49 applies and gives the stated algebraicity of $cal(W)_K \( A \)$. The full domain in (8.4) and the lattice-clearing isogeny include every compatible period, with no simplicity or endomorphism-generality assumption. ◻
]

